/* Guided-mode focus packs: big idea, why it matters, plain-words gist per part, core/extra flag, things to remember. Guidance only; the lesson text is unchanged. */
window.FOCUS=Object.assign(window.FOCUS||{},{"p41m2":{"big":"Quantum computing is linear algebra over complex numbers: a state is a list of amplitudes, a gate is a unitary matrix, and probability is an amplitude's squared size.","why":"Every later module computes with these objects, and knowing the math means knowing what Qiskit's output should be before you call the function.","parts":[{"t":"Complex numbers","gist":"A complex number has a size, called the modulus, and a phase. Probability is the squared modulus of an amplitude. Amplitudes need i because some gates, like S, turn the phase by a quarter turn.","core":true},{"t":"Vectors and Dirac notation","gist":"A ket |ψ⟩ is a column vector and a bra ⟨ψ| is its conjugate transpose, a row. Their inner product is a number, and its squared size is the probability of that outcome.","core":true},{"t":"Unitary and Hermitian matrices","gist":"Gates are unitary matrices (U†U = I), so probabilities still add up to 1 and every gate can be undone. Observables are Hermitian matrices, and ⟨ψ|Z|ψ⟩ = P(0) − P(1).","core":true},{"t":"Tensor products and multi-qubit states","gist":"Qubits combine by the tensor product, so n qubits need 2ⁿ amplitudes. A state that can't be split into one description per qubit is entangled. Qiskit is little-endian: the rightmost bit is qubit 0.","core":true},{"t":"A complete two-qubit calculation","gist":"One state worked from start to finish: check it is normalized, read off the probabilities, measure one qubit and renormalize, compute ⟨ZZ⟩, then apply H and recompute. Every quantum algorithm is this at scale.","core":true},{"t":"Why 2ⁿ is the whole story of simulation","gist":"Each extra qubit doubles the memory needed for exact simulation: 30 qubits need 16 GiB, 50 need 16 PiB. That exponential is why quantum computers are hard to build and worth building.","core":false}],"remember":[{"p":"Probability is the squared modulus of the amplitude: |z|² = z·z* = a² + b². Two states with equal probabilities can still differ in phase.","part":0},{"p":"A gate is a unitary matrix, U†U = I: it preserves normalization and its inverse is U†.","part":2},{"p":"⟨ψ|Z|ψ⟩ = P(0) − P(1), the number Qiskit's Estimator returns for the observable Z.","part":2},{"p":"Qiskit is little-endian: in '01' the rightmost character is qubit 0, so qubit 0 is 1 and qubit 1 is 0.","part":3},{"p":"n qubits have 2ⁿ amplitudes, so simulating 30 qubits exactly needs 16 GiB.","part":5}]},"p41m3":{"big":"A quantum program is a sequence of reversible gates followed by measurement, and those gates can entangle qubits but can never copy an unknown state.","why":"Bell states and no-cloning are the building blocks of everything later: error correction, quantum key distribution and every algorithm on the exam.","parts":[{"t":"Single-qubit gates as matrices","gist":"Each single-qubit gate is a matrix that rotates the qubit's point on the Bloch sphere: X flips the bit, Z flips the phase, H swaps the X and Z bases. Real chips have a small native gate set.","core":true},{"t":"Two- and three-qubit gates","gist":"CNOT flips a target qubit when the control is 1; SWAP costs three CNOTs. {H, T, CNOT} can build any gate, while Clifford-only circuits are easy to simulate, so T gates carry the quantum advantage.","core":true},{"t":"Entanglement and the Bell states","gist":"H then CNOT makes the Bell state (|00⟩ + |11⟩)/√2, which always measures 00 or 11. Its correlations beat any pre-set-answers theory (CHSH 2√2 > 2), yet send no message.","core":true},{"t":"No-cloning and reversibility","gist":"Linearity alone forbids copying an unknown quantum state, so there are no backups mid-computation and eavesdroppers can't copy key photons. Every gate is reversible because U⁻¹ = U† always exists.","core":true},{"t":"Measurement, the computational basis and circuit diagrams","gist":"Measurement is the one irreversible, probabilistic step, normally in the Z basis; for X, apply H first. Diagrams read left to right, with single lines for qubits and double lines for classical bits.","core":false},{"t":"Building and simulating in Qiskit","gist":"The Bell circuit in code: build it, look at its statevector before measuring, run shots on a simulator, draw it, and export it as OpenQASM 3, the vendor-neutral text format for circuits.","core":true}],"remember":[{"p":"H on qubit 0, then CNOT from 0 to 1, turns |00⟩ into the Bell state (|00⟩ + |11⟩)/√2: you see only 00 or 11.","part":2},{"p":"The Bell state reaches CHSH 2√2 ≈ 2.83, above the bound of 2 for any theory where each qubit carries pre-set answers.","part":2},{"p":"Entanglement sends no message: each qubit alone reads 50/50, and the correlations appear only when records are compared over a classical channel.","part":2},{"p":"{H, T, CNOT} is universal; the Clifford set {H, S, CNOT} alone is not, and Clifford-only circuits are efficiently simulable.","part":1},{"p":"No-cloning: no unitary can copy every unknown state, because linearity turns the would-be copy into a different vector.","part":3}]},"p41m4":{"big":"Quantum algorithms turn function values into phases and arrange interference so wrong answers cancel and the right one can be measured.","why":"Shor's period finding is why RSA, Diffie–Hellman and elliptic curves must be replaced, while Grover only halves the exponent of symmetric keys, so NIST recommends AES-256.","parts":[{"t":"Oracles and phase kickback","gist":"An oracle is the function f built as a reversible gate. Put its output qubit in |−⟩ and f(x) shows up as a plus or minus sign on the input, and signs can interfere.","core":true},{"t":"Deutsch–Jozsa, Bernstein–Vazirani, Simon","gist":"Deutsch–Jozsa tells a constant f from a balanced one in one query, where a deterministic classical method needs 2ⁿ⁻¹ + 1 in the worst case. Bernstein–Vazirani and Simon show bigger gaps; Simon's inspired Shor.","core":true},{"t":"Grover's search and the √N arithmetic","gist":"Grover finds a marked item in about (π/4)√N tries instead of N/2 by rotating the state toward the answer each round. Too many rounds overshoot. It is a quadratic speedup, not an exponential one.","core":true},{"t":"The quantum Fourier transform","gist":"The QFT turns a repeating pattern in the amplitudes into a sharp peak you can measure. It needs far fewer gates than the classical FFT, but its output can't be read directly, so it only helps inside larger algorithms.","core":true},{"t":"Shor's algorithm: period finding breaks RSA and ECC","gist":"Shor factors N by using the QFT to find the period of aˣ mod N, then a classical gcd step. The same method solves discrete logarithms, so Diffie–Hellman and elliptic curves fall along with RSA.","core":true},{"t":"Variational algorithms for noisy machines","gist":"For today's noisy hardware, a short quantum circuit with adjustable parameters is tuned by a classical optimizer. VQE estimates ground energies, QAOA tackles problems like MaxCut, and neither has a proven speedup yet.","core":true}],"remember":[{"p":"Phase kickback: with the output qubit in |−⟩, the oracle writes f(x) as a sign, (−1)^{f(x)}, on the input register.","part":0},{"p":"Deutsch–Jozsa needs one query to tell constant from balanced; a deterministic classical algorithm needs 2ⁿ⁻¹ + 1 in the worst case.","part":1},{"p":"Grover needs about (π/4)√N iterations. For N = 8, two give 94.5% success; three overshoot to 33.1%.","part":2},{"p":"Shor's quantum part finds only the period r of aˣ mod N; a classical gcd then gives the factors, such as 15 = 3 × 5.","part":4},{"p":"VQE and QAOA are hybrid quantum-classical loops for noisy hardware, with no proven speedup so far.","part":5}]},"p41m5":{"big":"Qubits constantly lose information to noise, and only error correction running below a threshold, not suppression or mitigation, can make individual runs reliable at scale.","why":"These numbers let you check any 'quantum will break RSA soon' claim with arithmetic instead of trust, and the noise-handling settings are what the IBM exam tests through its primitives.","parts":[{"t":"Decoherence: T1, T2 and the clock that is always running","gist":"A qubit slowly leaks information to its surroundings. T1 is how long |1⟩ lasts before decaying, T2 how long its phase lasts, and T2 ≤ 2·T1. Every gate, idle wait and readout spends this time budget.","core":true},{"t":"Gate fidelity and the calibration sheet","gist":"Gate error is 1 minus fidelity, measured with randomized benchmarking. Today's best two-qubit gates have errors near 10⁻³, and readout errors are larger. The numbers drift, so IBM recalibrates continuously and yesterday's sheet is only a guide.","core":false},{"t":"Noise models: what Aer simulates","gist":"Noise is modeled as channels acting on a density matrix: bit flip, depolarizing, amplitude damping (T1), phase damping and readout error. Aer can copy a real device's calibration or take a model you build by hand.","core":true},{"t":"Suppression, mitigation, correction: three different promises","gist":"Suppression lowers errors as they happen. Mitigation repairs averaged results afterward and costs extra shots, sometimes exponentially many. Only correction makes each individual run trustworthy.","core":true},{"t":"Error correction from first principles","gist":"You can't copy a qubit, but you can spread it across entangled qubits and check their parities. The checks show where a flip happened without revealing the state. The three-qubit code fails only at 3p² − 2p³.","core":true},{"t":"The threshold theorem and the surface code","gist":"If every operation fails less often than a threshold, bigger codes push logical errors as low as you like; above it, they make things worse. The surface code's threshold is near 1%, and Λ measures the gain per step.","core":true},{"t":"Logical versus physical: the overhead arithmetic","gist":"A logical qubit is built from many physical ones. At p = 10⁻³ you need distance about 21, roughly 900 physical qubits per logical qubit. Better gates shrink the machine a lot, so Λ matters more than raw qubit count.","core":true},{"t":"Reading the 2024–2026 milestones","gist":"Recent results, like Google's Willow with Λ = 2.14, are memories and small logical circuits, not a fault-tolerant computer running a useful algorithm. It also lists which noise settings the exam tests.","core":false}],"remember":[{"p":"T1 is energy loss: |1⟩ survives time t with probability e^(−t/T1). T2 is phase loss, and T2 ≤ 2·T1 always.","part":0},{"p":"Mitigation (TREX, ZNE, PEC) repairs averaged expectation values only; every shot stays as noisy as before. Only error correction makes single runs reliable.","part":3},{"p":"The three-qubit repetition code fails at 3p² − 2p³: at p = 1% that is 2.98 × 10⁻⁴, about thirty times better than a bare qubit.","part":4},{"p":"A distance-d surface code uses 2d² − 1 qubits; below its threshold near 1%, logical error falls by a factor Λ each time d grows by two.","part":5},{"p":"At p = 10⁻³, long algorithms need about d = 21, roughly 900 physical qubits per logical qubit: the thousand-to-one rule.","part":6}]},"p41m6":{"big":"Before a real quantum chip will run your circuit, it must be transpiled to the chip's native gates and wiring, and you should predict the result before spending QPU time.","why":"This workflow is the core of the IBM exam, and with only 10 free QPU minutes per 28 days, testing locally and predicting results first is how you avoid wasting runs.","parts":[{"t":"Set up: environment, account, credentials","gist":"Install Qiskit in a virtual environment, then save your IBM API key and instance CRN once. Treat the key like a password. The free Open Plan gives up to 10 QPU minutes per 28 days, so plan runs carefully.","core":true},{"t":"Choosing a backend from its calibration data","gist":"Each backend publishes its numbers: T1, T2, gate errors and readout errors. Current IBM chips all use CZ as their two-qubit gate. Medians hide outliers, so check the specific qubits your circuit lands on.","core":false},{"t":"Transpilation: from your circuit to the chip's instruction set","gist":"A chip runs only its native gates on physically connected qubits, so the transpiler picks qubits, adds SWAPs and translates gates. Every extra SWAP adds error, so check the two-qubit count and depth before running.","core":true},{"t":"Running: primitives, PUBs, shots and execution modes","gist":"The Sampler returns bitstrings and the Estimator returns expectation values with standard errors. You send them PUBs, bundles of a circuit and its inputs, in job, batch or session mode; the Open Plan has no sessions.","core":true},{"t":"Reading results honestly","gist":"Counts are samples, so a probability from N shots has a standard error of √(p(1−p)/N). More shots shrink random noise but never fix hardware bias. Always compare against a prediction made before you ran.","core":true},{"t":"Local first: Aer, fake backends and your GPU","gist":"Develop on simulators and spend QPU time last. Aer runs circuits on your machine, fake backends copy a real device's layout and noise, and a 24 GB NVIDIA card can simulate 30 qubits in double precision.","core":true},{"t":"Other SDKs and clouds","gist":"Cirq, PennyLane, Amazon Braket and CUDA-Q each suit different work. OpenQASM 3 is the shared text format that moves circuits between them, and IBM's REST API accepts it too.","core":false}],"remember":[{"p":"The Open Plan gives up to 10 minutes of QPU time per rolling 28-day window, with job and batch modes but no sessions.","part":0},{"p":"Since 2024 the Runtime primitives accept only ISA circuits, native gates on connected qubits, so you must transpile before running.","part":2},{"p":"Map observables onto the chosen physical qubits with obs.apply_layout(isa.layout), or the Estimator measures the wrong qubits.","part":2},{"p":"Estimator resilience_level 1 (the default) is TREX for readout error; level 2 adds ZNE.","part":3},{"p":"Standard error is √(p(1−p)/N): halving it costs four times the shots, and shots never remove hardware bias.","part":4}]},"p41m7":{"big":"Quantum computers threaten today's public-key cryptography, and the practical defense is migrating to post-quantum cryptography now, with quantum key distribution only a niche complement.","why":"Recorded traffic is already at risk from harvest-now-decrypt-later, and quantum-safe migration is the largest near-term part of the quantum job market, the one a security engineer can enter soonest.","parts":[{"t":"BB84: key distribution from the no-cloning theorem","gist":"Alice sends random bits in random bases, and they keep only the positions where Bob's basis matched, about half. An eavesdropper must measure photons, which causes 25% errors; secret key is possible below about 11%.","core":true},{"t":"QKD in the real world: fiber, satellites and trusted nodes","gist":"Real QKD loses photons with distance and can't use optical amplifiers, because amplifying means copying. Long links chain trusted relays or use satellites, and at each relay the key exists in the clear.","core":false},{"t":"QKD versus post-quantum cryptography","gist":"QKD rests on physics but gives no authentication and needs special optics. Post-quantum cryptography is software on existing networks. The NSA and several European agencies say: migrate to post-quantum cryptography first.","core":true},{"t":"Harvest now, decrypt later: the deadline that already passed","gist":"Adversaries can record encrypted traffic today and decrypt it once a large quantum computer exists. Mosca's inequality tells you whether your data is exposed. So key exchange migrates first, then signatures.","core":true},{"t":"The quantum workforce: who gets hired, for what","gist":"Quantum jobs span PQC migration, quantum software, control and FPGA work, photonics and lab work, and research. PQC migration is the largest near-term market, and fiber work leads into photonics and QKD deployment.","core":false},{"t":"The certification and your study plan","gist":"The C1000-179 exam has 68 questions in 90 minutes, and you pass with 47 correct. An eight-week plan walks through the objectives; book only when practice scores sit at 85% or above.","core":true},{"t":"Honest expectations","gist":"No machine today can break RSA or elliptic curves, and quantum computers are co-processors, not laptop replacements. The skill that lasts is checking claims with arithmetic, which also makes a good penetration tester.","core":false}],"remember":[{"p":"In intercept-resend, Eve picks the wrong basis half the time and Bob's result is then a coin flip: ½ × ½ = 25% errors on the sifted key.","part":0},{"p":"With one-way post-processing, BB84 yields secret key only while the QBER stays below about 11%.","part":0},{"p":"QKD provides no authentication and long links need trusted relays; the NSA and European agencies recommend post-quantum cryptography (FIPS 203, 204, 205) first.","part":2},{"p":"Mosca's inequality: if X (years data must stay secret) plus Y (years to migrate) exceeds Z (years until the machine), today's data will be read.","part":3},{"p":"C1000-179: 68 questions in 90 minutes, pass with at least 47 correct (69%), up to $200.","part":5}]},"p42m1":{"big":"You can reason about any radio signal with three numbers, frequency, wavelength and power in dBm, plus knowing which rules govern the band you are on.","why":"Every link budget, exam question and later module rests on this arithmetic: wavelength tells you the hardware, dBm tells you the budget, and the allocation tells you whether you may transmit.","parts":[{"t":"The one equation you will use every day","gist":"Wavelength in meters is about 300 divided by the frequency in MHz. Antennas are a fraction of a wavelength, so the band tells you an antenna's size before you buy any wire.","core":true},{"t":"The bands and who lives in them","gist":"The spectrum splits into bands from LF to microwave, each behaving differently, from ionospheric skip on HF to strict line of sight at microwave. The FCC decides who uses each slice: licensed services, unlicensed Part 15 devices, or ISM equipment.","core":true},{"t":"Power: the decibel recap","gist":"Radio power is counted in decibels, so you add instead of multiply: +3 dB doubles, +10 dB is ten times, and 0 dBm is 1 mW. A 5 W handheld is 37 dBm, and a receiver works at −100 dBm.","core":true},{"t":"The amateur bands and what each license unlocks","gist":"Each license class unlocks more spectrum. Technicians get every amateur frequency above 30 MHz plus some HF segments; General adds large parts of every HF band; Amateur Extra gets everything.","core":true},{"t":"From Hertz to the SDR in your pocket","gist":"A short history from Hertz's 1887 demonstration through FM, the FCC and the transistor to the cheap software-defined radio. It ends by mapping the module to Technician and General exam topics.","core":false}],"remember":[{"p":"λ(m) ≈ 300 / f(MHz): a 146 MHz signal is 2.05 m long, so its half-wave antenna is 1.03 m.","part":0},{"p":"Wi-Fi is unlicensed, not unregulated: Part 15 devices must accept any interference and must not cause harmful interference.","part":1},{"p":"+3 dB doubles power, +10 dB is ten times, −3 dB halves, and 0 dBm is 1 mW.","part":2},{"p":"A 5 W handheld transmits 37 dBm and a receiver is happy at −100 dBm, so the path can lose 137 dB and still work.","part":2},{"p":"Technicians have all amateur frequencies above 30 MHz, plus HF CW segments and data and SSB phone on parts of 10 m at 200 W PEP.","part":3}]},"p42m2":{"big":"Modulation carries information by varying a carrier's amplitude, frequency or phase, and every radio is limited by the same two things: bandwidth and noise.","why":"Understanding modulation, bandwidth and SNR lets you read a waterfall, predict a Wi-Fi data rate, and see why weak-signal digital modes work when voice cannot.","parts":[{"t":"Analog: AM, SSB and FM","gist":"AM varies the amplitude and spends two-thirds of its power on a carrier that carries no information. SSB drops the carrier and one sideband, fitting voice in 2.4–2.8 kHz. FM varies frequency, resists noise, but takes much more bandwidth.","core":true},{"t":"Digital: symbols, bits and constellations","gist":"Digital radios send symbols, and each symbol from M possible states carries log2(M) bits. Packing in more states, like 16-QAM's 4 bits or 1024-QAM's 10, raises speed but needs roughly 3 dB more SNR per step.","core":true},{"t":"OFDM: many slow carriers instead of one fast one","gist":"Reflections arriving late would smear one fast symbol stream. OFDM splits the channel into hundreds of narrow, slow subcarriers so echoes do little harm. Wi-Fi, LTE and 5G all use it.","core":true},{"t":"Noise, SNR and Shannon","gist":"Every receiver hears thermal noise, −174 dBm per hertz, so a wider channel hears more noise. Shannon's C = B log2(1 + S/N) caps the data rate. FT8 trades speed for decoding signals at −20 dB SNR.","core":true},{"t":"Decoding digital modes today, receive-only, legally","gist":"With a 40-dollar SDR and free software you can decode FT8, APRS and RTTY this week, receive-only, with no license. Which modes a Technician may later transmit depends on the band.","core":false}],"remember":[{"p":"SSB fits voice in 2.4–2.8 kHz; 2 m FM takes about 16 kHz by Carson's rule, 2(deviation + audio frequency).","part":0},{"p":"Bit rate = symbol rate × log2(M): 250,000 symbols per second with 16 states is 1 Mbps.","part":1},{"p":"Each higher QAM step needs roughly 3 dB more SNR; 1024-QAM needs about 35 dB, so routers fall back at the edge of coverage.","part":1},{"p":"Thermal noise is −174 dBm per hertz at 290 K, and Shannon's C = B log2(1 + S/N) caps any channel's data rate.","part":3},{"p":"A Technician may transmit FT8 on 28.074 MHz and APRS on 2 m; FT8 on 20 and 40 m needs General.","part":4}]},"p42m3":{"big":"Cut an antenna to resonance, match it to its feedline, aim its gain, and a link budget tells you before buying anything whether the signal will arrive.","why":"The antenna is the only part of a station that adds gain for free, and the link budget decides whether a link will work before any money is spent.","parts":[{"t":"Resonance: the half-wave dipole","gist":"A half-wave dipole is two wires fed in the middle; its total length in feet is 468 ÷ f(MHz). Cut it a little long and trim toward resonance: wire is easy to shorten and impossible to lengthen.","core":true},{"t":"The family","gist":"Antennas don't create power, they concentrate it, so gain in one direction means nulls elsewhere. dBd + 2.15 = dBi. Polarization matters as much as gain: cross-polarized antennas lose 20 dB or more.","core":true},{"t":"Impedance, SWR and the feedline","gist":"When an antenna's impedance doesn't match the 50 Ω line, some power reflects, measured as SWR. Coax then loses more with length and frequency: 50 ft of RG-58 at 446 MHz turns 5 W into 1.5 W.","core":true},{"t":"The link budget: Friis","gist":"Add transmit power and antenna gains, subtract cable loss and free-space path loss, and compare with the receiver's sensitivity; the difference is your margin. Then check the Fresnel zone is clear.","core":true},{"t":"Safety: RF exposure, towers and power lines","gist":"Every amateur station must be evaluated against FCC RF-exposure limits. Never work on an antenna that can transmit, ground feedlines in storms, keep antennas well away from power lines, and never climb alone.","core":true}],"remember":[{"p":"Half-wave dipole: L(ft) = 468 / f(MHz). At 146 MHz that is 38.5 inches total, 19.2 inches per leg.","part":0},{"p":"dBd + 2.15 = dBi, so a '5 dBd' antenna is 7.15 dBi. Always ask which reference a gain figure uses.","part":1},{"p":"SWR 2:1 reflects 11% of the power and 3:1 reflects 25%; most transceivers fold back power above about 2:1.","part":2},{"p":"FSPL = 20 log10 d(km) + 20 log10 f(MHz) + 32.44; an outdoor link wants a 15–20 dB fade margin.","part":3},{"p":"The FCC general-population limit between 30 and 300 MHz is 0.2 mW/cm², averaged over 30 minutes.","part":4}]},"p42m4":{"big":"VHF and UHF travel mostly in straight lines, so height sets their range, while HF bends back off the ionosphere, which the sun switches on and off.","why":"Propagation explains what you hear and what you don't, from a repeater to a satellite to a Wi-Fi dead zone, and it runs through the Technician exam.","parts":[{"t":"Line of sight and the radio horizon","gist":"VHF and UHF go mostly line of sight, so height lets an antenna see farther over the earth's curve: the horizon in km is about 4.12 √h(m). That is why repeaters sit high.","core":true},{"t":"The ionosphere and the solar cycle","gist":"The sun ionizes layers of the upper atmosphere. The D layer absorbs low HF bands by day; the F2 layer bends signals back for long-distance contacts. The 11-year solar cycle decides which high bands open.","core":true},{"t":"VHF and UHF surprises","gist":"Sometimes VHF and UHF go much farther than line of sight: sporadic E patches, temperature-inversion ducts, meteor trails and even bouncing off the moon can carry signals.","core":false},{"t":"Repeaters: borrowed height","gist":"A repeater listens on one frequency and retransmits on another at the same time, from a high site. You set its offset, ±600 kHz on 2 m and ±5 MHz on 70 cm, and usually an access tone.","core":true},{"t":"Satellites: repeaters in orbit","gist":"Low-orbit satellites like the ISS work as repeaters in orbit, reachable with a handheld and a small Yagi during a 10–15 minute pass. Their speed shifts the frequency, so you retune for Doppler.","core":true},{"t":"Why 5 GHz Wi-Fi reaches less far than 2.4 GHz","gist":"5 GHz loses 7.5 dB more than 2.4 GHz in free space, walls absorb more of it, and it bends less around corners. It still wins on capacity where it reaches: range and capacity are different things.","core":true}],"remember":[{"p":"Radio horizon d(km) ≈ 4.12 √h(m): a handheld at 1.8 m sees 5.5 km, a repeater 300 m up sees 71 km.","part":0},{"p":"Above the MUF a wave escapes to space; the D layer absorbs low HF by day, which makes 80 and 40 m night bands.","part":1},{"p":"Standard repeater offsets are ±600 kHz on 2 m and ±5 MHz on 70 cm, usually with a CTCSS tone.","part":3},{"p":"LEO Doppler is about ±11 kHz on 437.8 MHz and ±3.6 kHz on 145.8 MHz, so you step the UHF side during a pass.","part":4},{"p":"Going from 2,440 to 5,800 MHz adds 20 log10(2.38) = 7.5 dB of free-space path loss.","part":5}]},"p42m5":{"big":"A software-defined radio turns the wave into numbers early so software does every job, and the law decides what you may receive and transmit.","why":"The SDR is the cheapest laboratory in the curriculum and an everyday instrument of wireless-security work; knowing the legal lines keeps it a career asset.","parts":[{"t":"The chain","gist":"A tuner shifts a slice of spectrum down, an ADC turns it into numbers, and software on your computer does the filtering, demodulating and decoding. That is why one dongle can do many different jobs.","core":true},{"t":"Sampling: Nyquist and I/Q","gist":"I/Q sampling digitizes two copies of the signal 90° apart, so a complex sample rate captures that same width of spectrum. Bit depth sets dynamic range: 8 bits give about 50 dB.","core":true},{"t":"The hardware","gist":"Start with the RTL-SDR Blog V4 receiver. HackRF, PlutoSDR and LimeSDR can also transmit, but transmitting is legal only inside a license you hold or with a certified Part 15 device.","core":true},{"t":"Software and a GNU Radio receiver","gist":"SDR++ and GQRX are for listening. GNU Radio builds a radio from connected blocks; the lab FM receiver chains a source, a low-pass filter that also lowers the sample rate, a WBFM demodulator and an audio output.","core":true},{"t":"What to receive, and the law","gist":"Broadcasts, aircraft ADS-B, ship AIS, weather satellites, hams and your own sensors are lawful to receive. Pagers, cellular and anything encrypted are not, and you may not divulge private communications you intercept.","core":true}],"remember":[{"p":"With complex I/Q samples, captured bandwidth equals the sample rate: an RTL-SDR at 2.4 MSPS shows 2.4 MHz.","part":1},{"p":"Dynamic range ≈ 6.02 N + 1.76 dB: 8 bits give about 50 dB, 12 bits 74 dB.","part":1},{"p":"Receiving needs no permission; transmitting needs a license covering that frequency, or a certified Part 15 device.","part":2},{"p":"The GNU Radio FM receiver: source, low-pass filter with decimation, WBFM Receive, audio sink.","part":3},{"p":"ADS-B at 1,090 MHz, AIS and Meteor-M images are lawful to receive; pager data, cellular and encrypted traffic are off limits under ECPA.","part":4}]},"p42m6":{"big":"The license comes from a well-oiled process of study, exam, fee and call sign, and operating well means following a few Part 97 rules and listening first.","why":"It turns your study into a credential issued by a federal agency and teaches the operating culture that makes other hams respect you from your first transmission.","parts":[{"t":"The exam process, end to end","gist":"Study the published question pool, get an FRN, pass 26 of 35 at a volunteer exam session, pay the FCC's $35 fee within 10 days, and your call sign appears in ULS. The license lasts 10 years.","core":true},{"t":"Call signs and the rules you will actually use","gist":"Your call sign shows your license and call district. The core rules: identify at least every 10 minutes, no business traffic or ciphers, the control operator is responsible, use minimum power, and never deliberately interfere.","core":true},{"t":"Station setup and your first repeater contact","gist":"Start with a certified dual-band handheld and program local repeaters with their offsets and tones. Listen before you talk, check into nets the way net control asks, keep transmissions short, and use 146.520 MHz for simplex.","core":true},{"t":"Digital voice and the mesh","gist":"DMR, D-STAR and System Fusion are three incompatible digital voice systems. Meshtastic is a LoRa text mesh: Part 15 and encrypted by default, or Part 97 with your call sign and no encryption, never both.","core":true},{"t":"Emergency communications and public service","gist":"ARES supports served agencies like hospitals and the Red Cross, RACES is activated by civil-defense authorities, and SKYWARN spotters report severe weather. You are a communicator supporting professionals, never a first responder.","core":true},{"t":"Portable operating and logging","gist":"Parks on the Air turns portable operating into a sport. Log every contact with UTC time, frequency, mode, call sign, reports and grid square; your log also proves what you transmitted and when.","core":false}],"remember":[{"p":"Technician exam: 35 questions from a 409-question pool, 26 to pass. No Morse code is required for any license class.","part":0},{"p":"The $35 FCC fee is separate from the exam session fee and must be paid in CORES within 10 days.","part":0},{"p":"Identify with your call sign at least every 10 minutes and at the end of a contact; no business traffic and no ciphers.","part":1},{"p":"Program each repeater with its output, offset (±600 kHz on 2 m, ±5 MHz on 70 cm) and CTCSS or DCS tone.","part":2},{"p":"A Meshtastic node is either Part 15 with encryption on, or licensed Part 97 mode with your call sign and no encryption, never both.","part":3}]},"p42m7":{"big":"Every RF threat, from replay and relay to jamming, spoofing and eavesdropping, has signs a defender can see and an engineering defense that resists it.","why":"Most security teams can read a packet capture but not a waterfall, so this turns your radio knowledge into a rare defensive skill and a career path with named certifications.","parts":[{"t":"The RF attack surface, defensively","gist":"Replay, relay, jamming, spoofing and eavesdropping each exploit a weak assumption, and each leaves signs a defender can spot. Using a jammer is illegal, and jamming is also the easiest attack to see.","core":true},{"t":"Wi-Fi and Bluetooth security, from the RF side (Domain 10 recap)","gist":"WPA3 makes Protected Management Frames mandatory, ending forged-deauthentication attacks, and 802.1X with EAP-TLS ties clients to certificates. Bluetooth flaws were fixed by updates. The RF engineer sets sensible power levels and plans monitoring.","core":true},{"t":"Spectrum monitoring: the defender's instrument","gist":"Spectrum monitoring means recording a normal baseline, then spotting what's different. Note time, frequency, bandwidth, level and location. Each source has a look: a jammer is a flat hump, a microwave oven drifts near 2.45 GHz.","core":true},{"t":"RF site surveys and the CWNA","gist":"A site survey is a link budget for a building: set requirements like −67 dBm and 25 dB SNR for voice, model it, walk it passively and actively, then write a channel plan. This is the CWNA's body of knowledge.","core":true},{"t":"Careers, and how the license stacks","gist":"RF roles range from cell-site technician to wireless security consultant. An amateur license plus fiber field experience, CWNA and device knowledge is an unusual combination, and unusual combinations get hired.","core":false}],"remember":[{"p":"Interfering with any radio service is a federal offense under 47 U.S.C. § 333, regardless of motive.","part":0},{"p":"Jamming shows as the noise floor rising 20–40 dB, with packet errors climbing while RSSI stays high.","part":0},{"p":"Rolling codes, nonces and challenge–response defeat replay; time-of-flight distance bounding defeats relay.","part":0},{"p":"WPA3 makes Protected Management Frames (802.11w) mandatory, which ends the forged-deauthentication denial of service.","part":1},{"p":"Voice Wi-Fi wants −67 dBm or better and at least 25 dB SNR; 2.4 GHz has only three non-overlapping channels, 1/6/11.","part":3}]},"p43m1":{"big":"A Mac is Apple's frameworks on a Unix core with a sealed system volume, and its shared unified memory decides which local AI models it can run.","why":"Apple fleets run in a large share of companies, schools and support desks, so you need this to support them, and the memory arithmetic tells you whether a Mac is worth buying for local AI.","parts":[{"t":"Darwin, XNU and launchd","gist":"macOS is built on Darwin, whose XNU kernel mixes Mach, a BSD layer and the IOKit driver framework. launchd is process 1 and starts and restarts every daemon. Your Unix habits mostly fit, with a few differences.","core":true},{"t":"APFS and the sealed system","gist":"The operating system lives on a read-only volume that Apple signs, and the Mac verifies it at boot. Your files live on a separate Data volume, so even root can't change /System.","core":true},{"t":"Apple silicon and unified memory","gist":"CPU, GPU and the other engines share one pool of memory with no separate VRAM. Capacity decides which AI models fit; bandwidth decides how fast they run. Memory is soldered, so you buy it once.","core":true},{"t":"The iOS and iPadOS security model","gist":"An iPhone runs only code Apple has signed, checked step by step from an unchangeable Boot ROM. The Secure Enclave guards keys and biometric data, files are encrypted by protection class, and every app is sandboxed.","core":true},{"t":"Apple Business, Apple School Manager and the device journey","gist":"Organizations register devices in Apple Business, which replaced Apple Business Manager in April 2026, or in Apple School Manager. Each device is assigned to an MDM and enrolls itself at first power-on.","core":true},{"t":"Mac or PC: the honest decision","gist":"Keep the PC for CUDA and x86 security labs, and add a Mac when a purpose justifies it, such as models larger than 32 GB or a Mac admin career.","core":false}],"remember":[{"p":"XNU is a hybrid of Mach, a BSD layer and IOKit; launchd is PID 1, replacing init, cron and inetd.","part":0},{"p":"The Signed System Volume is read-only and Apple-signed; your files sit on a separate Data volume.","part":1},{"p":"Model weights ≈ parameters × bits ÷ 8, and decode speed is capped near memory bandwidth ÷ bytes read per token.","part":2},{"p":"Notarization is an automated malware scan Gatekeeper checks for on downloaded Mac apps, not an App Store-style review.","part":3},{"p":"Purchase to enrollment: the device appears in Apple Business by serial number, is assigned to an MDM, and enrolls itself at first power-on.","part":4}]},"p43m2":{"big":"You can run a Mac entirely from the shell with Apple's own tools, but four protection layers, FileVault, Gatekeeper, SIP and TCC, can still refuse even root.","why":"These commands are the daily vocabulary of Mac administration and what every Jamf policy script assumes, so you can manage a Mac reproducibly without touching its mouse.","parts":[{"t":"Users, groups and secure tokens","gist":"Mac accounts live in Directory Services, not /etc/passwd, and you manage them with dscl, sysadminctl and dseditgroup. Only secure-token holders can unlock FileVault, so it matters who has one.","core":true},{"t":"The four layers that say no","gist":"FileVault protects a stolen Mac's data, Gatekeeper blocks unsigned or unnotarized downloads, SIP stops even root changing system files, and TCC asks the user before apps reach the camera, microphone, screen or files.","core":true},{"t":"Preferences and inventory","gist":"Settings are property lists you read and write with defaults, plutil and PlistBuddy, and settings pushed by MDM override the user's. system_profiler with -json describes a Mac's hardware and software for scripts.","core":true},{"t":"launchd: agents, daemons and launchctl","gist":"launchd runs agents as a user at login and daemons as root at boot, each defined by a plist. launchctl loads, inspects, restarts and unloads them, and print shows the last exit code.","core":true},{"t":"Scripting: zsh, Python, Shortcuts, Homebrew, Time Machine","gist":"Write fleet scripts in zsh and ship your own Python, since macOS has no system Python for your scripts. Homebrew is a per-user developer tool, and tmutil drives Time Machine from the shell.","core":false}],"remember":[{"p":"Only secure-token holders can unlock FileVault; check with sysadminctl -secureTokenStatus.","part":0},{"p":"SIP can be changed only from recoveryOS, so even sudo cannot modify protected system locations.","part":1},{"p":"TCC consent is per user, per app, per service; MDM can pre-grant some, but camera, microphone and screen recording stay the user's decision.","part":1},{"p":"LaunchAgents run as the user at login; /Library/LaunchDaemons run as root at boot with no login needed.","part":3},{"p":"launchctl bootstrap loads a job, print shows its state and last exit code, kickstart -k restarts it, bootout unloads it.","part":3}]},"p43m3":{"big":"Troubleshoot Apple devices from the least destructive step to the most: gather evidence, try Safe Mode, reinstall, revive, and erase and restore only as a last resort.","why":"Help-desk tickets and Apple exam questions mostly ask for the next, least destructive step, and following that order means you never destroy data you could have saved.","parts":[{"t":"Startup modes","gist":"On Apple silicon, hold the power button to reach startup options: recoveryOS, Safe Mode, Apple Diagnostics and Share Disk. DFU mode lets a second Mac revive the firmware, keeping data, or restore it, erasing everything.","core":true},{"t":"Diagnostics and the unified log","gist":"Apple Diagnostics tests hardware and ends with a code; ADP000 means no issue found. Software problems leave evidence in the unified log, which you read with Console, log show or log stream, or bundle with sysdiagnose.","core":true},{"t":"Common failures and first moves","gist":"Match each symptom to its first evidence: do startup options appear, what does the panic report name, is memory under pressure, does a name fail where an IP works. Then apply the usual fix.","core":true},{"t":"Reinstall, erase, migrate, recover","gist":"Reinstalling macOS replaces the system and leaves your files alone. Erase All Content and Settings destroys the keys, so the data is unreadable within seconds. Recovery is only as good as your backups and escrowed keys.","core":true},{"t":"The support workflow and Self Service Repair","gist":"Collect a sysdiagnose before changing anything, then test, back up, fix, verify and document. Self Service Repair uses Apple's own parts and tools, with ESD protection and lithium-battery safety.","core":false}],"remember":[{"p":"On Apple silicon, hold the power button for startup options; the Intel NVRAM and SMC resets don't exist there.","part":0},{"p":"DFU revive reinstalls firmware and recoveryOS and keeps your data; DFU restore erases everything and installs current macOS.","part":0},{"p":"Apple Diagnostics code ADP000 means no issue found; log show and log stream read the unified log from the command line.","part":1},{"p":"Reinstalling macOS from recoveryOS keeps the Data volume; Erase All Content and Settings is a cryptographic erase.","part":3},{"p":"A FileVault Mac with no password and no recovery key is unrecoverable by design.","part":3}]},"p43m4":{"big":"An MDM server never connects to a device directly: it sends a push wake-up, and the device checks in and pulls its queued commands, which makes zero-touch deployment possible.","why":"MDM is the center of every Apple admin job and of this domain's exams, and it is how a laptop no one in IT has touched reaches a new hire ready to work.","parts":[{"t":"The protocol: push, check in, pull","gist":"The server sends a tiny wake-up through Apple's push service. The device then connects to the server, says Idle, and pulls queued commands one at a time, answering each. Renew the yearly push certificate with the same Apple Account.","core":true},{"t":"Enrollment types and supervision","gist":"Automated Device Enrollment makes organization-owned devices enroll themselves at setup, supervised and non-removable, and supervision unlocks stronger control. User Enrollment protects personal devices by managing only a separate work volume.","core":true},{"t":"Configuration profiles","gist":"A configuration profile is an XML property list holding payloads, one per group of settings, such as Wi-Fi or restrictions. A Mac accepts profiles only from MDM or with the user's explicit approval.","core":true},{"t":"Declarative Device Management","gist":"Instead of the server micromanaging, DDM sends JSON declarations that the device enforces itself, reporting changes through a status channel. On the 27.0 releases, software updates can be enforced only this way.","core":true},{"t":"Apps, vendors and the zero-touch walkthrough","gist":"Apps and Books licenses can go to serial numbers with no Apple Account needed. Vendors range from Jamf Pro to Intune to Apple Business's free built-in option. Zero-touch links purchases to your MDM, so devices arrive ready.","core":true}],"remember":[{"p":"APNs only tells the device to contact its server; the device keeps a connection to APNs on TCP 5223, then pulls commands over HTTPS.","part":0},{"p":"Renew the push certificate yearly from the same Apple Account; a different account changes the topic and every device must re-enroll.","part":0},{"p":"Supervision unlocks restrictions like blocking AirDrop, silent app installs, Single App Mode, Managed Lost Mode and enforced updates.","part":1},{"p":"With User Enrollment the organization never sees the serial number or personal apps and can remove only the work data.","part":1},{"p":"On the 27.0 releases, legacy software update commands no longer work; updates are enforced only through DDM declarations.","part":3}]},"p43m5":{"big":"Securing Apple fleets means layered, checkable controls, from identity and encryption to baselines, EDR and patch deadlines, that you can prove to an auditor with real numbers.","why":"It is how you show an auditor that every Mac is encrypted, patched, monitored and tied to the corporate identity, the work where Mac admins earn their pay.","parts":[{"t":"Identity: Platform SSO with Entra ID and Okta","gist":"Platform SSO lets Entra ID or Okta own the Mac login. The strongest method replaces the password with a Secure Enclave key, a hardware-bound credential that resists phishing. After login, apps and browsers sign in silently.","core":true},{"t":"FileVault escrow and the bootstrap token","gist":"MDM enforces FileVault and stores each Mac's recovery key so the help desk can unlock it. The bootstrap token is a different secret: it lets MDM grant secure tokens and authorize updates, but it does not unlock disks.","core":true},{"t":"Baselines: CIS benchmarks and mSCP","gist":"A baseline turns 'secure' into rules you can check. CIS benchmarks come in Level 1 and a stricter Level 2, and NIST's mSCP generates profiles and check scripts. Report honestly and rank the failures by risk.","core":true},{"t":"Endpoint Security, EDR and the threat picture","gist":"Security tools now run on Apple's Endpoint Security framework and are deployed with MDM profiles. The main Mac threat is infostealers that trick users into installing fake apps or pasting commands into Terminal.","core":true},{"t":"Activation Lock, Lost Mode and patch enforcement","gist":"Activation Lock keeps a wiped stolen device useless, and Lost Mode reports location only while a device is lost. For patching, a DDM declaration sets a target version and a deadline, and the Mac installs when the deadline arrives.","core":true},{"t":"Privacy law and the limits of management","gist":"Management must respect privacy law such as GDPR, CPRA and HIPAA: publish a policy, collect inventory rather than content, use location only for lost devices, and give personal devices User Enrollment.","core":false}],"remember":[{"p":"Platform SSO's Secure Enclave key is a hardware-bound, phishing-resistant credential unlocked with Touch ID.","part":0},{"p":"The bootstrap token lets MDM grant secure tokens and authorize updates on Apple silicon; it does not unlock FileVault.","part":1},{"p":"A compliance score counts rules, not risk: one failed critical control can matter more than twenty passing cosmetic ones.","part":2},{"p":"Mac infostealers such as Atomic Stealer mostly arrive through fake installers, cracked apps and 'paste this into Terminal' lures.","part":3},{"p":"In practice Apple patches the current macOS and the two before it; a DDM declaration enforces a target version by a deadline.","part":4}]},"p43m6":{"big":"Apple devices lean on multicast, peer-to-peer radio and Apple cloud services more than PCs do, and ordinary networking explains every ticket they generate.","why":"Discovery failing across VLANs, Private Relay on audited networks, roaming and update caching are tickets Apple fleets generate every week, and they reuse everything you learned in Domain 2.","parts":[{"t":"Bonjour: mDNS and DNS-SD","gist":"Bonjour lets devices announce and find services by multicast DNS on UDP 5353. Those packets never cross routers, so across VLANs you need an mDNS gateway that copies only the services you choose.","core":true},{"t":"Continuity and AirDrop","gist":"Continuity and AirDrop find nearby devices over Bluetooth LE and move data over AWDL, a peer-to-peer Wi-Fi link. To prevent data loss, MDM can turn AirDrop or Handoff off on supervised devices and Macs.","core":false},{"t":"Private Relay, encrypted DNS and content filtering","gist":"Private Relay sends browsing through two separate relays so no one party sees both who you are and where you go. A network that must audit traffic should answer NXDOMAIN for its hostnames instead of dropping packets.","core":true},{"t":"Wi-Fi roaming and content caching","gist":"iPhones look for a new access point below −70 dBm and Macs below −75 dBm, and the new one must be 8–12 dB stronger. Content caching lets one Mac download an update once and share it over the LAN.","core":true},{"t":"Remote management and Macs in Windows domains","gist":"SSH, Screen Sharing and Apple Remote Desktop manage Macs remotely, but keep them off the internet. Binding to Active Directory is the legacy path; modern fleets use Platform SSO and the Kerberos SSO extension.","core":true}],"remember":[{"p":"Bonjour uses multicast DNS on UDP 5353 to 224.0.0.251; it is link-local, so routers never forward it.","part":0},{"p":"Across VLANs, use an mDNS gateway that reflects only allowed service types such as _airplay._tcp, never everything everywhere.","part":0},{"p":"To block Private Relay, return NXDOMAIN for mask.icloud.com and mask-h2.icloud.com; silent drops make devices hang and retry.","part":2},{"p":"iPhones roam below −70 dBm and Macs below −75 dBm; the new access point must be 8 dB stronger (iPhone transmitting) or 12 dB stronger.","part":3},{"p":"Remote Login is SSH on TCP 22 and Screen Sharing is VNC on TCP 5900; reach them over VPN, never from the internet.","part":4}]},"p43m7":{"big":"Automation turns every repeated Mac admin task into a policy, script or pipeline that runs without you, and that skill is what the career path rewards.","why":"It is what separates the admin who clicks through 400 Macs from the one who runs 40,000, and it moves you from technician toward client platform or endpoint security engineer.","parts":[{"t":"Jamf Pro: policies, scripts, extension attributes, smart groups","gist":"Jamf Pro has four objects not to confuse: a profile holds state, a policy performs actions on a trigger, a smart group is a live query for scoping, and an extension attribute is a script that adds an inventory field.","core":true},{"t":"The Jamf Pro API from Python","gist":"Scripts reach Jamf through the Jamf Pro API: create a least-privilege API client, swap its ID and secret for a short-lived token, then page through inventory 100 records at a time. Keep secrets out of the source file.","core":true},{"t":"Munki and AutoPkg","gist":"AutoPkg recipes download apps, verify their signatures, package them and import them into Munki's testing catalog. Trust info stops a run when an upstream recipe changes. After a pilot, you promote to production.","core":true},{"t":"Compliance reporting and the community","gist":"A compliance report divides compliant devices by an in-scope count you can defend, leaving out retired and stale devices, with an owner for each exception. The MacAdmins community and certifications form the career ladder.","core":true},{"t":"The exams, mapped to this domain","gist":"A table matching Jamf 100 and Apple's two exams to this domain's modules. Practise naming the right object, policy, profile, declaration or smart group, before the click path.","core":false}],"remember":[{"p":"A Jamf policy performs actions on a trigger, by default the recurring check-in every 15 minutes; a configuration profile holds state.","part":0},{"p":"Exchange a least-privilege API client's ID and secret at /api/oauth/token for a bearer token; keep the secret in the Keychain or environment.","part":1},{"p":"Inventory comes 100 records per page, so 2,350 Macs need 24 requests, pages 0 to 23.","part":1},{"p":"AutoPkg trust info pins a hash of every parent recipe, so a changed upstream recipe stops the run until you review it.","part":2},{"p":"Compliance is compliant ÷ in-scope, excluding retired and stale devices, trended weekly with an owner for each exception.","part":3}]},"p44m1":{"big":"Usability is a measurable property: how well specified users reach specified goals effectively, efficiently and with satisfaction in a specified context, and bad interfaces cause real incidents.","why":"The human is part of every system you build or secure, so an engineer who can measure usability ships fewer incidents, and a tester who understands why people err writes sharper findings.","parts":[{"t":"Usability, defined so you can measure it","gist":"ISO 9241-11 defines usability as how well specified users reach specified goals in a specified context. Its three parts, effectiveness, efficiency and satisfaction, each become a number you can target, like success rate, time on task and SUS.","core":true},{"t":"The Double Diamond and design thinking, honestly assessed","gist":"The Double Diamond researches the problem before narrowing to it, then explores solutions before testing and shipping one. Design thinking names similar stages. Both help only if each stage ends in evidence, not workshop theatre.","core":true},{"t":"The cost of bad UX","gist":"Bad interfaces cause real disasters: Hawaii's false missile alert, Citibank's accidental $900 million payment, and a Navy collision linked to confusing touchscreen controls. Good design pays back too, in numbers you can estimate.","core":true},{"t":"Who does what, and where engineers fit","gist":"Researchers, designers, writers and accessibility specialists each own a piece of UX. Engineers help most by watching research sessions, reviewing designs early, demanding every state, and building from the design system.","core":true},{"t":"How this maps to the certifications","gist":"How this ground matches the first course of the Google UX Design certificate and IAAP's CPACC exam, which expects you to state the ISO definition and give a cost-of-failure example.","core":false}],"remember":[{"p":"ISO 9241-11 usability has three measurable parts: effectiveness (task success rate), efficiency (time on task) and satisfaction (a SUS score).","part":0},{"p":"Double Diamond: Discover, Define, Develop, Deliver. The first diamond finds the right problem; the second solves it right.","part":1},{"p":"Hawaii 2018: drill and live alerts were near-identical choices with the same confirmation, and the correction came 38 minutes later.","part":2},{"p":"Price a usability defect like a bug: 50,000 users a month, 3% abandoning, $40 orders is $60,000 a month.","part":2},{"p":"Push-approval prompts that trained people to tap Approve enabled MFA-fatigue attacks; number matching is a UX fix.","part":3}]},"p44m2":{"big":"Good research answers the question you actually have by watching what people do as well as hearing what they say, without letting bias fool you.","why":"Research is scoped, consented evidence-gathering, the same discipline as a penetration test, and it stops you building the wrong thing.","parts":[{"t":"Choosing a method","gist":"Pick a method from your question. Methods differ in whether they capture what people say or what they do, and why versus how many. People predict their own behavior poorly, so to learn about behavior, watch it.","core":true},{"t":"Interviews and surveys, and their biases","gist":"In interviews, ask about specific past events, never 'would you use…', and never pitch. Surveys measure how common something is; a bigger sample shrinks random error, but no size fixes a biased sample.","core":true},{"t":"Contextual inquiry and synthesis","gist":"Go where the work happens and watch like an apprentice, checking your interpretations on the spot. Every workaround is an unmet requirement. Affinity mapping turns notes into insights, then personas, job stories and journey maps.","core":true},{"t":"Structure and behavior: card sorting, tree testing, analytics","gist":"Card sorts show how people group content; tree tests check whether they can find things in a hierarchy. Analytics funnels show where people drop off but never why, so pair them with observation.","core":true},{"t":"Ethics and consent","gist":"Get informed consent in plain language: what you record, who sees it, how long it's kept, and that people may stop at any time. Recordings are personal data, so protect them and delete them on schedule.","core":true},{"t":"How this maps to the certifications","gist":"Which Google UX certificate courses cover these methods, and why CPACC's disability focus means recruiting disabled participants in every round, not in a separate study at the end.","core":false}],"remember":[{"p":"Interview about specific past behavior ('Tell me about the last time…'), never hypotheticals like 'Would you use…?'.","part":1},{"p":"A survey of 400 has a margin of about ±4.9 points, but sample size never fixes a biased sample.","part":1},{"p":"A job story reads: 'When…, I want to…, so I can…'.","part":2},{"p":"Card sorts show how people group content, tree tests whether a hierarchy is findable, funnels where people drop off but never why.","part":3},{"p":"Informed consent states the purpose, what is recorded, who sees it, how long it's kept, and that participants may stop and keep the incentive.","part":4}]},"p44m3":{"big":"Interaction design makes the next action obvious: clear structure, visible signifiers and feedback, targets sized by arithmetic, and forms whose errors say how to fix them.","why":"Like a protocol, an interface fails at the edges; these rules turn 'it feels clunky' into arithmetic and testable fixes an engineer can build.","parts":[{"t":"Information architecture and navigation","gist":"Information architecture decides how content is grouped, named, navigated and searched. Build it from a card sort, check it with a tree test, and make every screen answer: where am I, where can I go, how do I get back?","core":true},{"t":"Norman's vocabulary","gist":"Affordances are what an object lets you do; signifiers are the visible cues for where and how. Feedback answers 'did it work?'. A slip is the right goal with the wrong action; a mistake is the wrong goal or plan.","core":true},{"t":"The laws, with arithmetic","gist":"Fitts's law: big, close targets are faster to hit, so make frequent ones large and keep destructive ones far away. Hick's law: choosing among familiar options grows slowly with their number, so broad, well-labeled menus often win.","core":true},{"t":"Forms, validation and error messages","gist":"Use one column, visible labels above fields and the right input types. Validate when a person leaves a field, and again on the server. An error message sits by the field and says what happened and how to fix it.","core":true},{"t":"Feedback and microinteractions","gist":"Small moments like a toggle or 'Copied' are where quality is felt. Acknowledge input within 100 ms, show skeleton screens for slow loads, and prefer undo to confirmation dialogs for reversible actions.","core":false},{"t":"Prototyping in Figma, and the handoff","gist":"Figma builds prototypes from components, variants and auto layout, which works like CSS flexbox. Handoff is a conversation: every state, edge cases, accessibility notes, and design tokens that compile to CSS custom properties.","core":true},{"t":"How this maps to the certifications","gist":"Which Google UX certificate courses this module covers, and which WCAG criteria here, such as target size, labels and error identification, the WAS and CPACC exams test.","core":false}],"remember":[{"p":"A slip is the right goal with the wrong action (fix with spacing, constraints, undo); a mistake is the wrong goal or plan.","part":1},{"p":"Fitts's law: MT = a + b × log2(D/W + 1). Doubling a target's width lowers the difficulty and saves time on every tap.","part":2},{"p":"Hick's law, RT = a + b × log2(n + 1), applies only to familiar, equally likely options; unfamiliar lists are scanned item by item.","part":2},{"p":"WCAG 2.2 Target Size (Minimum) is 24 × 24 CSS pixels at AA; Apple asks for 44 × 44 points and Material 48 × 48 dp.","part":2},{"p":"A placeholder is not a label. Validate when a field is left, and say in text what happened and how to fix it.","part":3}]},"p44m4":{"big":"Visual design is mostly perception science and arithmetic you can check: type scales, computed contrast, spacing systems and restrained motion decide whether anyone can read what you build.","why":"Readable, high-contrast design is what WCAG requires and what decides whether anyone can actually use what you build, from a dim truck cab to a low-vision reader.","parts":[{"t":"Typography","gist":"Start body text at 16 px with line height around 1.5 and lines of 45–75 characters. Build sizes from one ratio, like 1.25, using four to six steps. Show hierarchy with two or three cues, not all at once.","core":true},{"t":"Color and contrast, computed","gist":"Contrast is computed from relative luminance. Normal text needs 4.5:1 for AA, while large text and control boundaries need 3:1. Many people can't tell red from green, so never let color carry meaning alone.","core":true},{"t":"Layout, grids, spacing and icons","gist":"Things placed close together read as a group, so spacing usually beats boxes and lines. Grids give columns and gutters, an 8-point spacing system makes every gap deliberate, and most icons need a visible label.","core":true},{"t":"Dark mode and motion","gist":"Dark mode is a second design: use dark gray such as #121212, not pure black, and re-check every contrast ratio. Motion should orient or give feedback, not decorate, and must respect prefers-reduced-motion.","core":true},{"t":"Design systems","gist":"A design system is tokens, components, patterns, guidelines and governance, with code. Material Design 3 and Apple's guidelines are the big references; start your own with tokens and the components you actually use.","core":false},{"t":"A measured critique of this app's cyberpunk theme","gist":"The app's theme mostly passes, with lecture text at 17.9:1, but faint labels at 3.74:1 and input borders at 1.66:1 fail, and quiz feedback relies on color alone. Changing two tokens and two patterns reaches AA.","core":false},{"t":"How this maps to the certifications","gist":"How this maps to Google's Course 5 and to the WCAG color and contrast criteria that CPACC tests conceptually and WAS tests by measuring and fixing.","core":false}],"remember":[{"p":"Body text 16 px or larger, line height 1.4–1.6, lines of 45–75 characters.","part":0},{"p":"Contrast ratio = (L_lighter + 0.05) / (L_darker + 0.05); AA needs 4.5:1 for normal text and 3:1 for large text.","part":1},{"p":"Component boundaries, focus indicators and meaningful chart lines need 3:1 (WCAG 1.4.11 Non-text Contrast).","part":1},{"p":"About 8% of men have a red–green color-vision deficiency, so pair color with text, icons or shape.","part":1},{"p":"Nothing may flash more than three times a second, and anything moving on its own for over five seconds needs a pause, stop or hide control.","part":3}]},"p44m5":{"big":"Accessibility means everyone, including people using assistive technology, can use what you build, and WCAG 2.2 AA, met with semantic HTML first, is the standard the law points to.","why":"Accessibility is the part of UX with a legal standard, a test method and lawsuits, and its failures stay invisible to anyone who doesn't use assistive technology.","parts":[{"t":"WCAG 2.2: how the standard is built","gist":"WCAG 2.2 rests on four principles, Perceivable, Operable, Understandable and Robust, with 86 testable success criteria at levels A, AA and AAA. Meeting all the A and AA criteria is the usual legal target.","core":true},{"t":"Semantic HTML first, ARIA second","gist":"Screen readers read the browser's accessibility tree, not your pixels. Use a native element like a real button first, and add ARIA only for patterns HTML lacks, because wrong ARIA is worse than none.","core":true},{"t":"Keyboard, focus and screen readers","gist":"Everything must work from the keyboard, in reading order, with visible focus. Move focus sensibly on route changes and when dialogs close. Test with the pairings people actually use, like NVDA with Firefox and VoiceOver with Safari.","core":true},{"t":"Media, cognition and the rest","gist":"Video needs edited captions and audio description; audio needs a transcript. Cognitive accessibility asks for plain language, consistent navigation, forgiving inputs and no puzzle tests to log in. Allowing paste also helps security.","core":true},{"t":"The law, as of October 2026","gist":"US state and local governments must meet WCAG 2.1 AA by April 2027 or 2028, federal agencies follow Section 508, and the European Accessibility Act has applied since 28 June 2025. Overlay widgets don't fix inaccessible code.","core":true},{"t":"The CPACC body of knowledge","gist":"CPACC's three domains: disabilities and assistive technologies (40%), accessibility and universal design (40%), and standards, laws and management strategies (20%), including the seven principles of universal design.","core":false}],"remember":[{"p":"WCAG 2.2 has four POUR principles, 13 guidelines and 86 success criteria; AA conformance means meeting all 55 A and AA criteria.","part":0},{"p":"First rule of ARIA: if a native HTML element already has the semantics and behavior you need, use it instead of adding ARIA.","part":1},{"p":"Accessible name order: aria-labelledby, then aria-label, then native labeling, then content; title and placeholder are weak last resorts.","part":1},{"p":"Focus order must follow reading order (2.4.3), so never use a positive tabindex.","part":2},{"p":"ADA Title II requires WCAG 2.1 AA by April 2027 or April 2028; the European Accessibility Act has applied since 28 June 2025.","part":4}]},"p44m6":{"big":"Usability testing replaces opinion with evidence: watch real people do realistic tasks, measure with honest error bars, and turn every finding into a fix someone can verify.","why":"Testing turns opinions into evidence and findings into fixed tickets, the same structure as a pentest report: scope, method, evidence, severity, remediation and retest.","parts":[{"t":"Planning a test","gist":"Give people realistic goals without interface words, define success beforehand, and let them think aloud while you prompt neutrally and never help. Five users find about 84% of common problems in one user group, so test in small rounds.","core":true},{"t":"SUS and small-sample statistics","gist":"SUS turns ten 1–5 answers into a 0–100 score, and about 68 is average; it is not a percentage. Small samples give wide error bars, so five users find problems but can't measure success rates.","core":true},{"t":"Heuristic evaluation","gist":"Three to five evaluators separately check the interface against Nielsen's ten heuristics, then merge findings and rate severity from 0 to 4. It is cheap and fast, but it doesn't replace watching real users.","core":true},{"t":"Experiments and analytics (Domain 22)","gist":"An A/B test randomly splits users between designs and compares one metric chosen in advance. It needs thousands of users per arm and tells you which design wins, never why; with little traffic, a small usability test is better.","core":false},{"t":"Accessibility audits","gist":"Automated tools like axe and Lighthouse are fast but catch only about 40–57% of barriers. The manual half covers keyboard-only use, a screen reader pass, zoom and reflow, color-only meaning and captions.","core":true},{"t":"Writing findings engineers act on","gist":"Each finding needs a title stating user impact, evidence, who is affected, a severity, a specific fix and testable acceptance criteria. Prioritize by severity times frequency, then retest after the fix.","core":true},{"t":"How this maps to the certifications","gist":"How Google's courses run a full usability study, and how WAS's domains on identifying and remediating accessibility issues match the audit and findings craft here.","core":false}],"remember":[{"p":"Five users find about 84% of common problems in one user group; a problem hitting only 10% of users is caught just 41% of the time.","part":0},{"p":"SUS: odd items score response − 1, even items 5 − response; sum × 2.5. Average is about 68.","part":1},{"p":"With 4 of 5 participants succeeding, the 95% interval runs from about 36% to 98%: five users find problems, they don't measure rates.","part":1},{"p":"Heuristic severity runs from 0 (not a problem) to 4 (catastrophe), judged by frequency, impact and persistence.","part":2},{"p":"A Lighthouse score of 100 is not conformance; automated rules catch roughly 40–57% of barriers.","part":4}]},"p44m7":{"big":"In UX the portfolio is the credential: a few deep case studies showing problem, evidence, decisions and measured outcomes, backed by the right certificates, get you hired.","why":"Hiring managers hire evidence, so a measured, accessible case study plus CPACC turns this domain into a job, and your field and security background is a mix accessibility teams struggle to hire.","parts":[{"t":"The case study","gist":"A case study is an argument with evidence: context and role, the problem with data, your process, two or three key decisions, a measured outcome, and reflection. Three deep ones beat ten thumbnails, and the portfolio itself must be accessible.","core":true},{"t":"Working with product and engineering","gist":"Good critique judges work against its goals and constraints; 'I like it' is not feedback. Design QA compares the build with the design before release. Shift accessibility left with acceptance criteria, linting and axe in CI.","core":true},{"t":"Content design and microcopy","gist":"Write what people need, in their words, in the order they need it. Buttons are verbs that say what happens, errors never blame the user, and empty states tell people what to do next.","core":true},{"t":"The Google UX Design certificate, project by project","gist":"The Google UX certificate is eight courses, about six months at ten hours a week for $49 a month, producing three portfolio projects. Run each on a real problem you can reach so yours stand out.","core":true},{"t":"CPACC, then WAS","gist":"CPACC is a broad foundational exam of 100 questions in two hours; WAS is the technical web exam, taken after hands-on remediation work. Passing both earns the CPWA, and certifications last three years.","core":true},{"t":"Career paths and pay","gist":"UX paths include designer, researcher, content designer, UX engineer, accessibility tester and usable security. Read pay figures with their source: posting medians skew high, so expect a first offer well below them.","core":false},{"t":"How this maps to the certifications","gist":"How Google's last courses build the portfolio and job search, and how CPACC's standards and management domain and WAS's remediation domain match this module's process material.","core":false}],"remember":[{"p":"A case study has six parts: context and role, problem with evidence, process, key decisions, measured outcome, reflection.","part":0},{"p":"Buttons should be verbs that say what happens, such as 'Save as-built', not 'OK'.","part":2},{"p":"Google UX certificate: eight courses, about six months at ten hours a week, $49 a month, three portfolio projects.","part":3},{"p":"CPACC: 100 questions in two hours, $410 for members and $510 for non-members; 600 on a 200–800 scale passes.","part":4},{"p":"CPACC is broad and foundational; WAS is the technical web exam. Passing both earns the CPWA.","part":4}]},"p45m1":{"big":"A project is temporary work that creates something unique, and its real target is value, outputs that become outcomes and benefits, not just scope, schedule and cost.","why":"Failure is the base rate, so understanding why projects fail is your first step from being the technician things happen to, to being the one who runs the job.","parts":[{"t":"Projects, operations and the constraints","gist":"A project is temporary and unique; operations are the ongoing work of running what projects build. Scope, schedule and cost trade against each other, but the real goal is value: output, then outcome, then benefit.","core":true},{"t":"Life cycles: predictive, adaptive, hybrid","gist":"Predictive life cycles plan everything early and suit stable work that is costly to change, like a bore under a highway. Adaptive ones plan in short iterations and suit software. Incremental hands over usable pieces; hybrid runs both together.","core":true},{"t":"How often projects fail, with sources","gist":"Every major study agrees failure is the base rate, and a few projects blow up by multiples. Only 8.5% of 16,000+ projects hit budget and schedule, and 0.5% also delivered their benefits. Read each study's limits critically.","core":true},{"t":"Roles, and the PM job in telecom and IT","gist":"The sponsor funds the project and owns the business case, the PM is accountable for delivery, and the team does the work. A RACI chart gives each deliverable exactly one Accountable person.","core":true},{"t":"The PMI Talent Triangle","gist":"PMI's Talent Triangle names three skill sets: Ways of Working, Power Skills and Business Acumen. Technicians who move up usually arrive strong on field technique and thin on the other two.","core":false},{"t":"Exam mapping","gist":"Where this module sits on the CAPM and CompTIA Project+ exams: fundamentals, life cycles, roles and the triple constraint.","core":false}],"remember":[{"p":"A project is a temporary endeavor that creates a unique product, service or result; operations are ongoing, repetitive work.","part":0},{"p":"Value chain: output (412 homes passed) → outcome (165 subscribers) → benefit (revenue and families online).","part":0},{"p":"Predictive suits stable, expensive-to-change work; adaptive suits emerging requirements; hybrid runs both, synchronized at milestones.","part":1},{"p":"Flyvbjerg and Gardner: of 16,000+ projects, 8.5% were on budget and on time, and 0.5% also delivered the promised benefits.","part":2},{"p":"RACI: Responsible, Accountable (exactly one per deliverable), Consulted, Informed.","part":3}]},"p45m2":{"big":"Planning turns a goal into a schedule crews can follow: a charter authorizes it, a WBS defines the work, estimates size it, and the critical path sets the finish.","why":"Planning decides most project outcomes, every number in the plan becomes a promise to the customer, and PERT, float and critical-path questions are near-certain on both exams.","parts":[{"t":"Business case and charter","gist":"The business case says why the work is worth doing. The charter formally authorizes the project and gives the PM authority to apply resources, with measurable objectives and the sponsor's signature. Without one, nobody agreed to pay.","core":true},{"t":"Stakeholders","gist":"List everyone who affects or is affected by the job, then sort them on a power-interest grid: manage closely, keep satisfied, keep informed, or monitor. Plan how to move each toward the engagement you need.","core":true},{"t":"Requirements, scope and the WBS","gist":"Requirements say what must be true at the end, and the scope statement lists deliverables and exclusions. The WBS breaks scope into deliverables by the 100% rule: each parent's children hold all of its work and nothing else.","core":true},{"t":"Estimating","gist":"Estimate by analogy, by rate times quantity, bottom-up, or with three points: PERT = (O + 4M + P) ÷ 6. Then check against how similar past jobs actually turned out, the outside view.","core":true},{"t":"Scheduling and the critical path","gist":"Forward and backward passes give each activity's earliest and latest start, and float = LS − ES. The zero-float chain is the critical path: slip anything on it and the finish slips. Crash or fast-track to finish sooner.","core":true},{"t":"Budget and baselines","gist":"Work-package costs plus a contingency reserve for identified risks make the cost baseline. A management reserve for unknown unknowns sits outside it and needs the sponsor. Baselines change only through change control.","core":true},{"t":"Exam mapping","gist":"Where this module sits on CAPM and CompTIA Project+; PERT, float and critical-path questions are near-certain.","core":false}],"remember":[{"p":"The charter formally authorizes the project and gives the PM authority to apply resources; the sponsor signs it.","part":0},{"p":"WBS 100% rule: a parent's children contain all of its work and nothing else. It lists deliverables, not activities.","part":2},{"p":"PERT = (O+4M+P)/6 and σ = (P−O)/6: O = 17, M = 20, P = 32 gives 21.5 days, σ = 2.5.","part":3},{"p":"Total float = LS − ES; the critical path is the longest, zero-float chain, 52 days in the Oak Hollow example.","part":4},{"p":"Contingency reserve sits inside the cost baseline; management reserve sits outside it and needs the sponsor.","part":5}]},"p45m3":{"big":"Earned value compares the work you planned, the work actually done and what it cost, so you can say in numbers whether a job is behind or over budget.","why":"Your nightly as-built already feeds earned value and, on unit-price work, the invoice. Reading it as numbers lets you tell the sponsor in one line how far the job has drifted and what you are doing about it.","parts":[{"t":"Earned value management","gist":"You compare three numbers on a status date: planned value (work scheduled by now), earned value (budgeted cost of work actually done) and actual cost. CPI = EV ÷ AC and SPI = EV ÷ PV; below 1.0 is bad.","core":true},{"t":"Daily production reporting: your as-built is the EV feed","gist":"Your as-built footage is the earned value measurement, and under a unit-price contract it is also the invoice. Compare production rate to plan every day, and never round footage up: it corrupts EV, the invoice and the next estimate.","core":true},{"t":"Change control","gist":"Every change goes down one path: request, impact analysis, a decision by the authority named in the plan, updated baselines, then communication. In the field, a redlined print becomes a priced, signed change order, before the work whenever possible.","core":true},{"t":"Quality","gist":"Quality assurance improves the process; quality control inspects the product, like testing splices against the loss spec. The cost of quality weighs prevention and inspection against failure: a bad splice costs minutes at the closure, a truck roll after turn-up.","core":true},{"t":"Procurement and contracts","gist":"Contract type decides who carries cost risk: the seller under firm fixed price, the buyer under cost plus fee, and shared under unit price, where the rate is fixed but quantity varies. Most fiber work runs on unit-price rate cards.","core":true},{"t":"Communications and status","gist":"A communications plan says who gets what, when, how and from whom, because 12 stakeholders make 66 possible channels. A status report fits on one page: red, amber or green, the numbers, top risks, and decisions needed by a date.","core":true},{"t":"Exam mapping","gist":"Where this shows up on the exams: EVM formulas are a CAPM staple, and Project+ tests change control, communication plans, contract types, quality and status reporting.","core":false}],"remember":[{"p":"CPI = EV ÷ AC and SPI = EV ÷ PV. On day 10 of the bore, CPI 0.91 and SPI 0.85 mean over cost and behind schedule.","part":0},{"p":"EAC = BAC ÷ CPI if current efficiency continues. A TCPI of 1.08 means the rest of the job must run 8% more efficiently to finish on budget.","part":0},{"p":"Spending half the budget does not mean half done: money spent is AC, while percent complete is EV ÷ BAC.","part":0},{"p":"Quality assurance improves the process; quality control inspects the product.","part":3},{"p":"Firm fixed price puts cost risk on the seller and suits a fully defined scope; cost plus fee puts it on the buyer.","part":4}]},"p45m4":{"big":"Risk management means anticipating what could hurt the job, putting numbers on it and acting early, and the same arithmetic works for field risk and cyber risk.","why":"A reserve you can defend line by line is what keeps a project solvent, and risk thinking is the skill your security training and your field job share.","parts":[{"t":"Risks, issues and how to write them","gist":"A risk is uncertain and might still happen; an issue is a problem happening now. Risks go in the risk register, issues in the issue log. Write each risk as cause, event, effect so someone can act on it.","core":true},{"t":"Qualitative analysis","gist":"Score each risk's probability and impact from 1 to 5, multiply, and plot them on a probability–impact matrix; the top right gets attention first. Define the scales in advance so 'high' means the same to everyone, and name an owner.","core":true},{"t":"Quantitative analysis","gist":"Expected monetary value (EMV) is probability times impact, added up across outcomes. Where parallel paths merge, their on-time chances multiply, and Monte Carlo simulation turns duration ranges into P50 and P80 dates you can commit to.","core":true},{"t":"Responses and reserves","gist":"Threats can be avoided, transferred, mitigated, accepted or escalated, each response with an owner and a trigger. Contingency reserve covers identified risks inside the cost baseline; management reserve covers unknown unknowns and needs the sponsor.","core":true},{"t":"Issues and lessons learned","gist":"When a risk fires it moves to the issue log with an owner and a due date, oldest items worked first. Capture lessons learned throughout the job, written so the next estimator can actually use them.","core":false},{"t":"Field safety is risk management","gist":"A job hazard analysis breaks the work into steps, lists each step's hazards and picks controls from the hierarchy: elimination, substitution, engineering, administrative, then PPE (protective gear). Never look into a fiber: invisible laser light can burn the retina.","core":true},{"t":"Cyber risk on technology projects","gist":"Security risks belong in the same register as field risks. Domain 33's ALE is simply EMV on a yearly clock, and a penetration test is its own project that needs written permission from the system owner.","core":true},{"t":"Exam mapping","gist":"Where this shows up on the exams: CAPM covers EMV, matrices, responses and risk versus issue, and Project+ tests risk strategies and the register.","core":false}],"remember":[{"p":"A risk is uncertain and in the future; an issue is a current problem. Risks go in the risk register, issues in the issue log.","part":0},{"p":"EMV = probability × impact, with threats negative: a 0.30 chance of a −$36,000 rock hit is −$10,800.","part":2},{"p":"Where two parallel paths are each 70% likely to be on time, the merge point is only 49% likely.","part":2},{"p":"Contingency reserve covers identified risks inside the cost baseline; management reserve covers unknown unknowns outside it and needs the sponsor.","part":3},{"p":"The hierarchy of controls, in order: elimination, substitution, engineering controls, administrative controls, then PPE.","part":5}]},"p45m5":{"big":"Match the method to the work: agile where requirements are uncertain and feedback is cheap, predictive plans where scope is fixed and change is expensive, often both on one project.","why":"Most real projects you'll run will be hybrid, and knowing when agile helps and when it hurts is a judgment employers pay for.","parts":[{"t":"Scrum in depth","gist":"The 2020 Scrum Guide defines three accountabilities (Product Owner, Scrum Master, Developers), five events with fixed time limits, and three artifacts, each with a commitment. The Daily Scrum is 15 minutes, for the Developers, not a status report.","core":true},{"t":"Kanban and flow","gist":"Kanban shows work on a board and caps work in progress (WIP). Little's law: average cycle time = WIP ÷ throughput, so cutting WIP from 12 to 8 at 4 a day drops cycle time from 3 days to 2.","core":true},{"t":"Velocity and forecasting","gist":"Velocity is the story points one team finishes per Sprint. Forecast with a range from recent Sprints, like 'seven to nine', and never make velocity a target, because points inflate as soon as they are rewarded.","core":true},{"t":"Scaling, honestly","gist":"SAFe, LeSS, Nexus and Scrum@Scale stretch agile across many teams, but the evidence for each is mostly case studies and vendor surveys. Remove dependencies between teams first; scale the process last.","core":false},{"t":"Hybrid: construction plus software","gist":"One project can run construction predictively, with a CPM schedule, unit prices and earned value, and software in two-week Sprints, synchronized at milestones both care about. Construction's Last Planner System adds weekly commitments, measured as percent plan complete.","core":true},{"t":"Retrospectives that change behavior","gist":"A retrospective earns its hour only if behavior changes. Open by checking the last action, and leave with one or two specific actions, each with an owner, a date and a measure.","core":true},{"t":"When agile is wrong","gist":"Agile loses when scope is fixed by physics, permits or contract and change is expensive, when no customer will look at an increment, for repetitive operations, and when 'agile' is an excuse not to plan.","core":true},{"t":"Exam mapping","gist":"Where this shows up on the exams: it is CAPM Domain 3 (20%), PSM I tests the Scrum Guide word for word, and Project+ tests agile concepts and when to choose them.","core":false}],"remember":[{"p":"A Sprint is one month or less, and the Daily Scrum is a 15-minute event for the Developers.","part":0},{"p":"Scrum's three artifacts and their commitments: Product Backlog and Product Goal, Sprint Backlog and Sprint Goal, Increment and Definition of Done.","part":0},{"p":"Little's law: average cycle time = WIP ÷ throughput. 12 items in progress finishing 4 a day means a 3-day cycle time.","part":1},{"p":"Forecast with a velocity range: 160 points at 18 to 25 per Sprint is 'seven to nine' Sprints. Never make velocity a target.","part":2},{"p":"Percent plan complete = assignments completed ÷ assignments planned, so 18 of 24 is 75%.","part":4}]},"p45m6":{"big":"Projects are delivered by people, and leadership is a learnable set of habits, practiced consistently, that earns their willingness to carry out the plan.","why":"Plans are only as good as the willingness of the people carrying them out, and the step from technician to lead is mostly a step in how you work with people.","parts":[{"t":"Leadership, management and power","gist":"Management copes with complexity and leadership copes with change, and a PM needs both, usually without much formal authority. Expert power (you know the work) and referent power (people trust you) are the ones that last.","core":true},{"t":"Situational leadership and team stages","gist":"Match your style to a person's development level on a specific task: directing, coaching, supporting or delegating. Teams move through forming, storming, norming and performing, so clarify roles early, surface conflict, then get out of the way.","core":true},{"t":"Motivation","gist":"People are driven by autonomy, competence and relatedness. Hygiene factors like pay on time and working tools don't motivate, but missing them demotivates fast, so fix those first, then make progress visible and recognize specific work.","core":true},{"t":"Meetings, one-on-ones and feedback","gist":"Every meeting needs a purpose, an agenda, a decision owner and a timebox, and ends with who, what, when. Give feedback with SBI: situation, behavior, impact, then ask and listen. Praise in public, correct in private.","core":true},{"t":"Conflict and negotiation","gist":"Each of the five conflict modes has a place: collaborate when the issue and the relationship both matter, force when safety is at stake. Negotiate on interests, not positions, and know your BATNA, your best alternative to a negotiated agreement.","core":true},{"t":"Managing up and teams you cannot see","gist":"Your sponsor is your most important stakeholder: no surprises, every problem brought with options and a recommendation, and decisions confirmed in writing. Field and remote teams need the same discipline, written down where everyone can find it.","core":false},{"t":"Psychological safety","gist":"Psychological safety means the team believes it is safe to admit a mistake, report a near miss or say 'I don't know'. It is not niceness: with high standards it produces learning. Leaders build it by admitting their own errors.","core":true},{"t":"Ethics","gist":"PMI's Code of Ethics rests on four values: responsibility, respect, fairness and honesty. In practice that means never rounding up footage, never reporting green when it is amber, and disclosing conflicts of interest.","core":true},{"t":"Exam mapping","gist":"Where this shows up on the exams: CAPM roles and stakeholder communication, the PMP's People domain, and Project+ team dynamics, conflict and communication.","core":false}],"remember":[{"p":"Management copes with complexity; leadership copes with change (Kotter).","part":0},{"p":"Situational leadership runs from directing a new hire, through coaching and supporting, to delegating to a veteran, judged task by task.","part":1},{"p":"SBI feedback: name the situation, the behavior and its impact, then ask, listen and agree the next step.","part":3},{"p":"BATNA is your best alternative to a negotiated agreement; the ZOPA lies between the two sides' walk-away points.","part":4},{"p":"PMI's four values are responsibility, respect, fairness and honesty.","part":7}]},"p45m7":{"big":"Tools only encode the method, so pick them by need; then turn what you know into the CAPM, a study plan and proof of projects you have led.","why":"It bridges knowing the material and being hired for it, turning the domain into a credential, a plan and a pay raise you can actually reach.","parts":[{"t":"The tools","gist":"Choose a tool by need: real dependencies and a critical path need a scheduling engine like Microsoft Project, P6 or ProjectLibre; team workflow wants Jira; cross-company tracking favors a shared grid. Treat AI-drafted status reports as a junior's first draft.","core":true},{"t":"Dashboards","gist":"A dashboard answers two questions at a glance: are we on track, and what needs a decision? Show CPI and SPI against thresholds set before the job started, plus top risks, open issues and safety, all on one screen.","core":true},{"t":"Programs, portfolios and the PMO","gist":"A program groups related projects for benefits none delivers alone; a portfolio is everything an organization funds. A PMO standardizes how projects run and comes in three types: supportive, controlling and directive.","core":true},{"t":"The exams","gist":"The CAPM is 150 questions in 180 minutes and needs 23 hours of PM education; Project+ is up to 90 questions in 90 minutes. Take the CAPM first, and don't under-study Business Analysis: it is 27% of the score.","core":true},{"t":"A 10-week CAPM plan","gist":"Seven hours a week for ten weeks is enough: work through the modules with their labs, then spend weeks 9 and 10 on timed mock exams, weakest domain first, before booking the exam.","core":false},{"t":"From technician to PM, and the pay","gist":"The path runs from technician through crew lead and project coordinator to project manager. Pay medians rise along it, but medians are not offers, so start building proof now: own the production report, the look-ahead and the JHA.","core":true},{"t":"Exam mapping","gist":"Where this shows up on the exams: Project+ tools, documentation and governance basics, and the CAPM's program, portfolio and PMO definitions.","core":false}],"remember":[{"p":"Real dependency logic and float need a scheduling engine (Microsoft Project, P6, ProjectLibre); team workflow wants Jira.","part":0},{"p":"Set dashboard thresholds before the job: green at 0.95 or better, amber 0.85 to 0.95, red below.","part":1},{"p":"PMI's three PMO types: supportive (templates, training), controlling (requires compliance with its methods) and directive (manages the projects itself).","part":2},{"p":"CAPM: 150 questions in 180 minutes, and Business Analysis is 27% of the score.","part":3},{"p":"Take the CAPM first: it also satisfies the PMP's 35-hour education requirement.","part":3}]},"p46m1":{"big":"Memory is a reconstruction, not a recording: you store what you attend to, sleep consolidates it, and pulling it back out strengthens it far more than rereading.","why":"Every study hour is either a retrieval that sticks or a re-read that evaporates, and knowing the difference is the biggest multiplier on a year of study.","parts":[{"t":"Encoding: attention is the gate","gist":"You only store what you pay attention to, and working memory holds only about four chunks at once. Chunking binds a pattern into one meaningful unit, which is why an engineer sees 192.168.1.0/24 as a single object.","core":true},{"t":"Consolidation: sleep is the second half of studying","gist":"During sleep the brain replays the day's learning and stabilizes it. One sleepless night cut next-day encoding by about 40 percent, so an all-nighter costs you tonight's replay and tomorrow's learning.","core":true},{"t":"Retrieval: the act that changes the memory","gist":"Recalling a fact changes the memory: the harder it is to reach right now, the more pulling it out strengthens it. That is why spacing, testing and generating answers work.","core":true},{"t":"The forgetting curve and the spacing effect","gist":"Without review, memory drops steeply and then levels off, to about a third after a day. Spaced study beats cramming, and the best first review sits at roughly 10–20 percent of the time until you need the knowledge.","core":true},{"t":"The testing effect, and why rereading feels better than it works","gist":"A week later, testing yourself beat rereading 61 to 40 percent, though students predicted the opposite. Smooth rereading feels like knowing, but that feeling is an illusion: reread only after a failed recall attempt.","core":true},{"t":"How the Recall engine implements this","gist":"The app's Recall deck is an SM-2 style scheduler: each card has an ease factor starting at 2.5. Intervals run 1 day, 3 days, then the previous interval times the ease, so each review lands near the point of forgetting.","core":true}],"remember":[{"p":"Working memory holds about four chunks (Cowan 2001); chunking binds a pattern into one unit to get around that limit.","part":0},{"p":"One sleepless night cut the next day's ability to encode new memories by about 40 percent.","part":1},{"p":"Without review, memory falls to about a third after a day; schedule the first review at roughly 10–20 percent of the time until you need it.","part":3},{"p":"A week later, repeated testing beat repeated study 61 to 40 percent (Roediger and Karpicke 2006).","part":4},{"p":"A Recall card rated Good every time returns on days 1, 4, 12, 32, 82 and 207.","part":5}]},"p46m2":{"big":"Testing yourself and spacing your practice are the high-utility techniques; rereading, highlighting and summarizing feel productive but rate low, so spend your hours on the winners.","why":"These are the techniques the app's tools implement, and using them deliberately is the difference between finishing forty-six domains and merely reading them.","parts":[{"t":"The Dunlosky scorecard","gist":"A 2013 review rated ten study techniques: practice testing and spaced practice high; elaborative interrogation, self-explanation and interleaving moderate; highlighting, rereading and summarizing low. 'Low' means weak or narrow evidence, not worthless.","core":true},{"t":"Retrieval practice, done properly","gist":"Retrieval comes in four forms: free recall, cards, practice questions and teaching back. The 3×3 rule: recall each item about three times in the first session, then relearn it in roughly three spaced sessions, always checking the answer.","core":true},{"t":"Spaced repetition: from SM-2 to FSRS","gist":"Spaced repetition software books your reviews for you. FSRS models each card's stability, the days until predicted recall falls to 90 percent, and schedules the review for when recall is predicted to hit your target.","core":true},{"t":"Interleaving","gist":"Interleaving mixes problem types in one session instead of one type at a time. It looks worse during practice but won 63 to 20 percent a week later, because exams ask which method applies before you apply it.","core":true},{"t":"Elaboration, self-explanation, dual coding, concrete examples","gist":"Ask 'why would this be true?' of each fact, explain each step of a worked example to yourself, pair words with a matching picture, and anchor abstract ideas to a concrete example.","core":true},{"t":"A weekly template","gist":"A sample week of about 75 minutes on weekdays: deck, one new module, a Mixed drill and the Mistake journal Monday to Thursday, Blurting on Friday, a lab on Saturday, the Exam simulator on Sunday.","core":false}],"remember":[{"p":"Dunlosky (2013) rated practice testing and spaced practice high utility, and highlighting, rereading and summarizing low.","part":0},{"p":"The 3×3 rule: recall each item correctly about three times in the first session, then relearn it in about three spaced sessions.","part":1},{"p":"Feynman technique: explain a concept in plain words, notice exactly where you stall or reach for jargon, then return to the source for that piece.","part":1},{"p":"In FSRS, stability is the days until predicted recall falls to 90 percent; dropping the target to 80 percent stretches the interval to 2.25S.","part":2},{"p":"Interleaved practice beat blocked practice a week later, 63 to 20 percent, even though blocking looked better during practice.","part":3}]},"p46m3":{"big":"Attention has two modes, focused and diffuse, and every task switch has a measurable cost, so build your day so structure, not willpower, protects your focus.","why":"After a ten-hour field day you may own about ninety minutes. Arranging them around how attention works is how your ninety can outlearn a full-time student's six hours.","parts":[{"t":"Focused mode, diffuse mode","gist":"Focused mode is narrow, deliberate attention; diffuse mode is the relaxed state, like walking or showering, that connects ideas. Hard learning alternates them. Stuck? Step away. Procrastinating? Commit to twenty-five minutes, not to finishing.","core":true},{"t":"Pomodoro and the Focus timer","gist":"Pomodoro is 25 minutes on one task, then 5 off, with a longer break after four. A bounded commitment beats the dread of starting, and one 50-minute block at full focus beats three distracted hours.","core":true},{"t":"Attention residue and the price of switching","gist":"When you switch tasks, part of your attention stays on the old one. Office workers needed about twenty-five minutes to fully return to an interrupted task, and even a silent phone on the desk lowered working-memory capacity.","core":true},{"t":"Phones, notifications and boredom","gist":"Notifications use the same unpredictable-reward design as slot machines, so defend yourself with structure: phone in another room, Do Not Disturb, messages batched into breaks. Then let yourself be bored; that is diffuse mode at work.","core":true},{"t":"Sleep, exercise and nutrition — the evidence, with doses","gist":"Sleep at least seven hours. Caffeine's half-life is about five hours, so a 3 p.m. coffee is still half there at 8 p.m. A brisk walk close to study time helps memory, and dehydration hurts attention.","core":true},{"t":"A field worker's study day","gist":"A sample weekday around a field job: deck at dawn, audio lessons on the commute, a drill at lunch, a walk, one 50-minute focus block, and lights out by 21:30. It adds up to roughly ten hours a week.","core":false}],"remember":[{"p":"Einstellung is being stuck on your first approach; the fix is to leave the desk for twenty minutes, not to push harder.","part":0},{"p":"Pomodoro: 25 minutes on one task, 5 minutes off, and a longer break after four.","part":1},{"p":"After an interruption, office workers needed about twenty-five minutes to fully return to the task (Mark et al. 2005).","part":2},{"p":"Even a silent, face-down phone on the desk lowered working-memory capacity compared with a phone in another room.","part":2},{"p":"Adults need at least seven hours of sleep, and caffeine's half-life is about five hours.","part":4}]},"p46m4":{"big":"Experience is not practice: skill grows from deliberate practice, with a specific goal just beyond your ability, full concentration, immediate feedback and repetition with refinement.","why":"Labs, the Mistake journal and the simulator are your coach, and using them as deliberate practice is what turns hours into a profession.","parts":[{"t":"Ericsson's deliberate practice — and what it is not","gist":"In the violinist study the hours were not the finding; the kind of hours was. Simply doing the thing again is naive practice: splicing a thousand fibers makes you fast, checking an OTDR trace after each one makes you good.","core":true},{"t":"The 10,000-hour myth, corrected","gist":"The 10,000 hours was an average at age twenty, not a mastery threshold. Practice explained about 21 percent of differences in performance in music and under 1 percent in professions: the biggest lever you control, not the only one.","core":true},{"t":"Four stages of competence and the feedback loop","gist":"Skills pass through four stages: you don't know what you don't know, you know and it hurts, you can with effort, then it's automatic. Stage two is where learning is fastest; fast, specific, frequent feedback moves you through.","core":true},{"t":"Labs, projects and the error log","gist":"A lab counts as deliberate practice only if it targets a known weakness, has a clear success check and ends with error analysis. Log mistakes as a slip, knowledge gap, misconception or reasoning error, because each needs a different fix.","core":true},{"t":"Plateaus and how to break them","gist":"A plateau comes when a skill turns automatic and you stop paying attention. Break it by pushing until errors reappear, drilling the weak sub-skill alone, changing how you represent it, measuring from outside, interleaving, or resting.","core":true},{"t":"Transfer of learning: why the fundamentals pay","gist":"Learning carries over to a new task only to the degree the two share elements, and far transfer is rare. That is why Domains 1–4 come first: bits, memory, processes, packets and functions reappear in everything after.","core":true}],"remember":[{"p":"Naive practice is doing the thing again; purposeful practice adds goals and feedback; deliberate practice is usually designed around the field's established training methods.","part":0},{"p":"Practice explained about 21 percent of performance variance in music and under 1 percent in professions (Macnamara et al. 2014).","part":1},{"p":"Conscious incompetence, the painful second stage, is where learning is fastest and where most people quit.","part":2},{"p":"Mistake types and fixes: slip, read slower; knowledge gap, lecture and a card; misconception, a correction card; reasoning error, narrate worked examples.","part":3},{"p":"Transfer happens to the degree two tasks share elements; far transfer, to a new domain and format, is rare.","part":5}]},"p46m5":{"big":"Better thinking tools, not more facts, are what let an expert find a fault fast: a small set of mental models used together, from first principles to Bayesian updating.","why":"Penetration testers, network engineers and reverse engineers are paid for exactly this kind of reasoning; the facts are only its raw material.","parts":[{"t":"What a mental model is","gist":"A mental model is a compressed, predictive picture of how something works. Facts are what you know; models are how you use what you know. A good one can be proven wrong and has known limits.","core":true},{"t":"The toolkit","gist":"Twelve thinking tools for technical work, such as: rebuild from verified facts, ask how it fails, among explanations that fit prefer the one with fewer assumptions, update beliefs with evidence, and learn why a rule exists before removing it.","core":true},{"t":"Case 1 — debugging a network with Bayes and Occam","gist":"Start with odds for each suspect, then pick tests the suspects predict differently. A ping by IP succeeding and a name lookup failing took DNS from 40 percent to about 97 percent in two cheap tests.","core":true},{"t":"Case 2 — designing a system with the toolkit","gist":"Designing a home lab for authorized security practice: list what it must do, then imagine how it fails, like a scan leaking toward the ISP. Each failure becomes a control, and you test the block instead of trusting the diagram.","core":false},{"t":"Pólya and the ladder in daily study","gist":"Use Pólya's four steps on every problem: understand, plan, carry out, then look back, the step everyone skips. When you explain something, climb at least two rungs: the principle, the mechanism, and one concrete instance with numbers.","core":true}],"remember":[{"p":"Occam's razor is a tie-breaker: among explanations that already fit the evidence, prefer the one with fewer assumptions.","part":1},{"p":"Chesterton's fence: don't remove a rule until you know why it was put there.","part":1},{"p":"Hanlon's razor: assume error before malice, but in security check the logs and verify before assuming either.","part":1},{"p":"Bayesian updating: prior × likelihood of the evidence gives the posterior, so choose the test the hypotheses predict most differently.","part":2},{"p":"Pólya's four steps: understand, plan, carry out, look back.","part":4}]},"p46m6":{"big":"Notes work through processing, not copying: select, compress and rephrase in your own words, and build an external memory you can find and understand in thirty seconds.","why":"You will meet several thousand facts in this curriculum. Your notes, lab notebook and questions are what let you keep and reuse them, and they are the habits of every good engineer.","parts":[{"t":"Why notes work, and when they do not","gist":"Producing information beats receiving it. The famous pen-beats-laptop result shrank or vanished when repeated; what matters is selecting, compressing and rephrasing. Rule: never write a sentence you could have copied.","core":true},{"t":"Cornell notes","gist":"Split the page into a narrow cue column, a wide notes column and a summary strip. Take notes on the right, write questions in the cue column afterwards, then cover the notes and answer the cues: built-in retrieval practice.","core":true},{"t":"Zettelkasten and progressive summarization","gist":"A Zettelkasten, or slip box, keeps one idea per note, in your own words, linked to other notes with a sentence saying why. Progressive summarization adds a layer of processing, bold, highlight, summary, only when you revisit a note.","core":true},{"t":"A personal wiki and documentation as learning","gist":"Keep a wiki alongside the notes: a page per tool or concept, a 'today I learned' log, and runbooks for anything done twice. Documentation comes in four kinds, tutorials, how-to guides, reference and explanation; know which you are writing.","core":false},{"t":"Reading books, papers, RFCs and man pages","gist":"Read textbooks with SQ3R: survey, question, read, recite, review. Read papers in up to three passes, and most deserve only the first five-to-ten-minute skim. Man pages are reference, not tutorials.","core":true},{"t":"The lab notebook, source code and good questions","gist":"A lab notebook entry records goal, environment, exact commands and outputs, what broke, what fixed it, and the next step. When asking for help, state your real goal, what you tried and the exact error text.","core":true}],"remember":[{"p":"Never write a sentence you could have copied: the processing, not the medium, does the work.","part":0},{"p":"Cornell notes: cue column on the left, notes on the right, summary at the bottom; review by covering the notes and answering the cues.","part":1},{"p":"A permanent Zettelkasten note holds one idea, in your own words, linked to other notes with a sentence saying why they connect.","part":2},{"p":"SQ3R stands for Survey, Question, Read, Recite, Review.","part":4},{"p":"The XY problem: you want X but ask about your attempted method Y, so everyone answers Y. State X.","part":5}]},"p46m7":{"big":"Your feelings about what you know are unreliable, so measure instead: predict your scores, test, compare, and steer a year of study by evidence rather than by feel.","why":"This is what keeps you going in month seven, when the novelty is gone and the certifications are still ahead.","parts":[{"t":"Metacognition and calibration","gist":"Calibration is how well your confidence matches your accuracy. Rereading tends to make you overconfident, so predict your score before every quiz and compare afterwards; a Brier score of 0 is perfect and 0.25 is coin-flipping.","core":true},{"t":"The Dunning–Kruger effect, stated correctly","gist":"The real finding is not that beginners are most confident: everyone rated themselves around the 60th to 70th percentile, whatever their skill, and critics call much of it a statistical artefact. The lesson: measure, don't feel.","core":true},{"t":"Impostor syndrome and growth mindset: the honest evidence","gist":"Impostor feelings are common when entering a new field; answer them with evidence, like passed simulators. Growth mindset helps only a little, its effect near d = 0.08, while practices like retrieval and spacing matter a lot.","core":true},{"t":"Motivation, habits and burnout","gist":"Motivation grows with autonomy, competence and relatedness. Habits took a median of 66 days, and an if-then plan ('when X, I do Y') beats a plain goal. Burnout looks like exhaustion, cynicism and inefficacy.","core":true},{"t":"Tracking progress: leading and lagging indicators","gist":"Leading indicators are inputs you control this week, like focus blocks and cards reviewed; lagging indicators are outcomes, like simulator scores. Inputs predict outcomes with a delay of weeks, and a 15-minute Sunday review closes the loop.","core":true},{"t":"The 12-month master plan","gist":"About 550 hours over twelve months, roughly 10.5 a week: fundamentals first, booked certification exams as deadlines with six to eight weeks of preparation each, spaced repetition to keep finished domains alive, and a 15 percent buffer.","core":false}],"remember":[{"p":"Calibration is how well confidence matches accuracy; measure it by predicting your score before each quiz and comparing.","part":0},{"p":"In the 1999 Dunning–Kruger study everyone rated themselves around the 60th to 70th percentile; self-assessment barely tracked skill.","part":1},{"p":"Growth mindset's effect is small, near d = 0.08; retrieval practice and spacing are an order of magnitude larger.","part":2},{"p":"Habits took a median of 66 days to become automatic, and missing one day did no measurable harm.","part":3},{"p":"A streak is a leading indicator: it measures showing up. Learning shows in lagging indicators like simulator scores.","part":4}]},"p47m1":{"big":"The deep web is everything search engines haven't indexed and is mostly legal; the dark web is a tiny slice, reachable only with special software like Tor.","why":"You will be asked to monitor the dark web for a living, and getting the definitions and numbers right is the difference between intelligence and rumor.","parts":[{"t":"Three layers, defined so you can test them","gist":"The surface web is what search engines have indexed. The deep web is everything else on the ordinary internet, like pages behind logins, mostly legal. The dark web is content on darknets, networks needing special software like Tor.","core":true},{"t":"The iceberg, measured","gist":"The famous '96%' iceberg misreads a 2001 deep-web estimate about databases and logins. Live onion sites number in the tens of thousands, a large share criminal, and at least four-fifths of Tor's traffic goes to ordinary websites.","core":true},{"t":"\"Darknet\" predates the crime","gist":"'Darknet' first named isolated ARPANET hosts in the 1970s, and Microsoft engineers used it in 2002 for content-sharing networks; the link to crime came later. The darknet is the network; the dark web is the content on it.","core":false},{"t":"History: a government lab, then a nonprofit","gist":"Onion routing was designed at the U.S. Naval Research Laboratory in 1995–96 and had to be opened to everyone, because anonymity needs a crowd. Tor was released in 2002 and became a nonprofit in 2006.","core":true},{"t":"Who actually uses Tor (Tor Metrics, October 2026)","gist":"About 3.2 million users connect to Tor directly each day, plus about 220,000 through bridges, over half of those from Russia and Iran. Legitimate uses are the majority: journalists, activists, privacy-seekers, researchers and censored readers.","core":true},{"t":"How this maps to the exam","gist":"Where this shows up: CTIA defines surface, deep and dark web much as here and likes to test the deep-web versus dark-web distinction; GCTI expects you to describe your collection sources.","core":false}],"remember":[{"p":"Deep web means not indexed by search engines (logins, databases, paywalls); dark web means web content on darknets that need special software.","part":0},{"p":"Bergman's estimate put the deep web at 400 to 550 times the surface web, and it describes databases and logins, not hidden criminal sites.","part":1},{"p":"At least four-fifths of the bytes moving through Tor go to ordinary websites, not onion services.","part":1},{"p":"Onion routing came from the U.S. Naval Research Laboratory; Tor was released in 2002 and The Tor Project became a nonprofit in 2006.","part":3},{"p":"Tor Metrics, October 2026: about 3.2 million direct daily users, plus about 220,000 through bridges.","part":4}]},"p47m2":{"big":"Tor wraps your traffic in three layers of encryption across three relays, so the guard sees your IP, the exit sees the destination, and no single relay sees both.","why":"Every dark-web safety rule, and every criminal's downfall, follows from knowing exactly who sees what at each hop.","parts":[{"t":"Layered encryption, one hop at a time","gist":"Your client builds a circuit through a guard, a middle and an exit relay, sharing a separate key with each. Messages go in cells wrapped in three layers of encryption; each relay peels one and learns only the next hop.","core":true},{"t":"Directory authorities, consensus, flags and families","gist":"Nine directory authorities vote every hour on a signed consensus listing every public relay, so no single one can fake the network. Flags assign roles like Guard and Exit, and a circuit never uses two relays from one family.","core":true},{"t":"Bridges and pluggable transports","gist":"Because the relay list is public, a censor can block every address on it. Bridges are unlisted entry relays, and pluggable transports such as obfs4, Snowflake and WebTunnel disguise the Tor traffic from censors.","core":true},{"t":"What Tor does and does not protect against","gist":"Tor hides where you are, not who you say you are. The exit can read plain HTTP, an adversary watching both ends can match timing, a customized browser stands out, and logging into your own account identifies you.","core":true},{"t":"Tor versus VPN versus proxy","gist":"A VPN moves your trust from your ISP to one company that can see both ends; Tor splits it across three volunteer relays so no single party sees both. Stacking them is not automatically better.","core":true},{"t":"Performance realities","gist":"Three encrypted hops each way add hundreds of milliseconds per round trip, and onion services double the hops. Tor carries only TCP, so most video calls fail; those are physics and economics, not bugs.","core":false},{"t":"How this maps to the exam","gist":"Where this shows up: CTIA asks what an exit relay can see, why HTTPS still matters inside Tor and how bridges differ from relays; GCTI expects you to describe your collection path's anonymity.","core":false}],"remember":[{"p":"The guard sees your IP but not the destination; the exit sees the destination but not your IP; the middle sees neither.","part":0},{"p":"Tor pins guards for weeks to months on purpose: rotating them raises the chance of eventually picking a malicious one.","part":1},{"p":"Bridges are unlisted entry relays; pluggable transports like obfs4, Snowflake and WebTunnel disguise the traffic from censors.","part":2},{"p":"Plain HTTP is readable at the exit; with HTTPS the exit still sees the hostname (SNI) but not the page or your password.","part":3},{"p":"Tor over a VPN hides Tor use from your ISP but hands your identity to the VPN provider.","part":4}]},"p47m3":{"big":"A .onion address is the service's own public key, so it needs no DNS or certificate authority, and because the service only makes outbound circuits, its location stays hidden.","why":"It lets you explain, in an interview or a report, why a seized site was located by human error rather than by a Tor flaw.","parts":[{"t":"What a .onion name is","gist":"A v3 address is 56 characters encoding 35 bytes: a 32-byte public key, a 2-byte checksum and a version byte. Because the name is the key, it authenticates itself. Old 16-character v2 addresses are dead.","core":true},{"t":"The protocol: introduction points and the rendezvous","gist":"The service keeps circuits open to a few go-between relays, its introduction points. A client picks a meeting relay, the rendezvous point, and asks the service through a go-between to meet there: six hops, nobody's IP revealed.","core":true},{"t":"The hidden-service directory","gist":"The service's signed descriptor is stored on eight directory relays picked by hashing. It is filed under a blinded key, changed every period, so those relays cannot link it to any address or harvest onion names.","core":false},{"t":"Client authorization and the operational extras","gist":"Optional extras: client authorization hides the service from anyone without a listed key, single-onion mode trades service anonymity for speed, OnionBalance spreads one address across servers, and the Onion-Location header points visitors to a real mirror.","core":false},{"t":"Why the location is hidden, and how services still get found","gist":"The service only makes outbound connections through its own guard, so its location stays hidden. Services get found through misconfiguration, buggy apps, reused usernames and hosting records, never by breaking the math.","core":true},{"t":"Legitimate onion services, and the only safe way to find them","gist":"Real organizations, from the BBC to ProPublica, run onion mirrors. Take an address only from the organization's own clearnet (ordinary web) page or Onion-Location header: phishing clones are common, and 56 characters can't be checked by eye.","core":true},{"t":"Your lawful lab: an onion service for a page on your own machine","gist":"Two lines in Tor's config file (torrc) turn a page served on your own machine into an onion service, legally, with nothing exposed to the internet. Binding the web server to 127.0.0.1 keeps the ordinary internet from reaching it directly.","core":true},{"t":"How this maps to the exam","gist":"Where this shows up: CTIA expects that onion services are reached by name not IP and that v3 names are 56 characters; GCTI expects you to explain how seized sites were really found.","core":false}],"remember":[{"p":"A v3 .onion address is 56 base32 characters: 35 bytes of key, checksum and version, and 35 × 8 ÷ 5 = 56.","part":0},{"p":"The name is the public key, so it is self-authenticating: no DNS lookup and no certificate authority needed.","part":0},{"p":"Client and service meet at a rendezvous point arranged through an introduction point: a six-hop path where neither learns the other's IP.","part":1},{"p":"Onion services get located through misconfiguration, operator mistakes and hosting records, not by breaking Tor's cryptography.","part":4},{"p":"Take an onion address only from the organization's own clearnet page or its Onion-Location header, never from a link list.","part":5}]},"p47m4":{"big":"Safe dark-web research is a discipline: verified software, a contained environment, the Safest settings, a clear grasp of what the law forbids, and a written procedure every time.","why":"This procedure keeps a researcher from becoming a victim, a suspect or a liability to an employer.","parts":[{"t":"Get it from torproject.org and prove it","gist":"Fake Tor Browser downloads are a classic way to spread malware. Download only from torproject.org, then verify its OpenPGP signature to prove it came from the Tor Browser developers; a 'BAD signature' means delete the file.","core":true},{"t":"Security levels and the settings that matter","gist":"People get unmasked by browser exploits, not Tor weaknesses, so use the Safest level, which turns JavaScript off everywhere, for any site you don't control. Add no extensions, never torrent, never open downloads online, never log into personal accounts.","core":true},{"t":"Tails and Whonix compared","gist":"Tails runs from a USB stick in memory only and leaves nothing on the computer. Whonix splits into a Tor-only Gateway and a Workstation with no direct network, so even malware can't learn your IP. Pick by threat.","core":true},{"t":"The law, plainly","gist":"In the U.S., using Tor and visiting onion sites is legal; some countries block it. Drugs, weapons, stolen data and unauthorized access are illegal on every network. If you meet child sexual abuse material, leave immediately and report it.","core":true},{"t":"The real risks and the counter to each","gist":"The real risks are phishing clones, malware downloads, tampering with plain-HTTP downloads, accidental exposure to illegal content, habits that reveal you, and legal trouble from stolen data. Each has a counter, from official addresses to legal review first.","core":true},{"t":"A researcher's checklist","gist":"Start with a written purpose and authorization, a dedicated machine or VM with a clean snapshot, and verified Tor Browser at Safest. Never buy, download outside a sandbox or message anyone; afterwards revert the snapshot and write the log.","core":true},{"t":"How this maps to the exam","gist":"Where this shows up: CTIA names Tor Browser and Tails and expects these operational-security basics and the legal limits; GCTI expects the same of a collector.","core":false}],"remember":[{"p":"Download Tor Browser only from torproject.org and verify its OpenPGP signature; a 'BAD signature' means delete the file.","part":0},{"p":"Use the Safest level, JavaScript off everywhere, for any onion site you don't control: browser exploits, not Tor flaws, unmask people.","part":1},{"p":"Tails is amnesic and leaves no trace on the host; Whonix isolates, so a compromised Workstation still cannot learn your IP.","part":2},{"p":"Knowingly accessing or possessing CSAM is a serious federal crime, and research is no defense: leave and report to NCMEC's CyberTipline or tips.fbi.gov.","part":3},{"p":"Viewing a leak post differs from downloading and testing stolen credentials, which is a crime; get legal review first.","part":4}]},"p47m5":{"big":"Each alternative network trades away speed, convenience or size to answer one weakness in Tor's threat model, such as timing correlation, choke points or seized servers.","why":"Knowing the alternatives lets you pick the right tool for a mission and recognize that the dark web that matters to your job still lives mostly on Tor and, increasingly, on Telegram.","parts":[{"t":"I2P: the internal network","gist":"I2P is a network for services inside itself, with no exits to the ordinary web by default. It bundles messages together (garlic routing), uses separate one-way tunnels in and out, and every user also routes for others.","core":true},{"t":"Hyphanet: a datastore, not a pipe","gist":"Hyphanet doesn't connect you to servers; it stores encrypted pieces of files on other users' disks. The publisher can go offline and the content survives, but retrieval is slow and not guaranteed.","core":true},{"t":"ZeroNet, Lokinet, Yggdrasil, GNUnet","gist":"Smaller projects worth placing correctly: ZeroNet is abandoned, Lokinet was renamed Session Router, Yggdrasil gives a mesh without anonymity, and GNUnet is a research framework. None of them is where the dark web lives.","core":false},{"t":"Mixnets: the research frontier","gist":"A mixnet beats someone watching both ends by delaying, reordering and batching messages and adding dummy traffic. The cost is latency, so it suits messaging, not browsing; Nym's NymVPN is the live example.","core":true},{"t":"Comparison","gist":"Pick by threat: Tor for anonymous browsing against a local adversary, I2P for internal sites, Hyphanet for documents that must survive, a mixnet against a global observer, and a VPN when you are willing to trust one company.","core":true},{"t":"What mesh and off-grid networks share with them","gist":"Mesh networks and anonymity networks are all overlays built from public-key identities, peer discovery, relays and store-and-forward. Meshes fight for connectivity when infrastructure is absent; anonymity networks fight for unlinkability when it is hostile.","core":false},{"t":"How this maps to the exam","gist":"Where this shows up: CTIA expects you to name I2P and Freenet/Hyphanet as dark-web sources reached with their own software; GCTI expects you to choose a collection channel by its anonymity properties.","core":false}],"remember":[{"p":"I2P has no exits by default: it is for services inside the network, such as I2P sites, mail and torrents.","part":0},{"p":"I2P's tunnels are one-way, a separate inbound and outbound tunnel, so an attacker must compromise two paths instead of one.","part":0},{"p":"Hyphanet is a distributed encrypted datastore: content stays available after the publisher goes offline.","part":1},{"p":"A mixnet resists timing correlation with delays, batching and cover traffic, at the price of latency that makes web browsing impractical.","part":3},{"p":"Mesh networks buy connectivity; anonymity networks buy unlinkability.","part":5}]},"p47m6":{"big":"Dark-web markets fall not because Tor gets broken but because operators leave traces in ordinary places: reused handles, personal emails, leaking servers, hosting records and traceable cryptocurrency.","why":"These failure patterns are exactly what a threat-intelligence analyst hunts for, and they prove that anonymity tools do not make crime safe.","parts":[{"t":"How a market works, conceptually","gist":"A market holds buyers' money in escrow, a holding account, until delivery is confirmed. Whoever holds that escrow can vanish with it, so exit scams are built in. For investigators, every Bitcoin escrow payment is a permanent public record.","core":true},{"t":"Case 1: Silk Road (2011–2013)","gist":"Silk Road's operator was traced through ordinary threads: an 'altoid' forum post leading to a Gmail address with his full name, a Stack Overflow question under his real name, a leaking server and intercepted fake IDs.","core":true},{"t":"Case 2: Operation Bayonet (2017)","gist":"AlphaBay's admin was undone by his personal Hotmail address in the site's welcome emails and an unencrypted laptop. Meanwhile Dutch police secretly ran the rival market Hansa for a month, logging passwords and about 27,000 transactions.","core":true},{"t":"Case 3: Hydra (2015–2022)","gist":"Hydra, a Russian-language market with about 17 million customer accounts, fell when German police seized its servers in Germany and 543 bitcoin. The weak point: infrastructure in a cooperating country and a real company on the hosting contract.","core":true},{"t":"Case 4: Genesis Market (2018–2023)","gist":"Genesis sold 'bots': cookies, saved logins and browser fingerprints stolen by malware, letting buyers impersonate victims. A 17-country operation made 119 arrests. The lesson for defenders: a session cookie is a credential.","core":true},{"t":"The investigators' toolbox","gist":"Bitcoin's ledger is public, so investigators group addresses into wallets and follow the funds to an exchange whose customer records answer a subpoena. Add operator mistakes, platform takeovers and international cooperation through Europol, the FBI and the NCA.","core":true},{"t":"How this maps to the exam","gist":"Where this shows up: CTIA draws on these cases for actor motivation and attribution, and GCTI's attribution and pivoting objectives are these investigations: handle to email to server to wallet.","core":false}],"remember":[{"p":"Markets hold buyers' money in escrow, so the operator can vanish with it: exit scams are structural.","part":0},{"p":"Silk Road fell through a reused 'altoid' handle tied to a Gmail address, a leaking server and intercepted fake IDs.","part":1},{"p":"In Operation Bayonet, Dutch police secretly ran Hansa for a month, while AlphaBay's admin was traced through a personal Hotmail address.","part":2},{"p":"Common-input-ownership heuristic: all inputs of one Bitcoin transaction are treated as belonging to one wallet.","part":5},{"p":"Bitcoin is pseudonymous and permanently public; address clustering plus exchange records unravel most dark-web finances.","part":5}]},"p47m7":{"big":"Dark-web monitoring is one collection discipline inside threat intelligence: you collect safely under strict rules, then turn what you find into a graded, actionable report for someone who must decide.","why":"This is the job the whole phase leads to, and it feeds directly into Domain 21 blue-team work and Domain 8 penetration-testing OSINT.","parts":[{"t":"The intelligence cycle, and where the dark web sits","gist":"Intelligence work runs in a cycle: requirements, collection, processing, analysis, dissemination, feedback. Dark-web findings serve strategic, operational and tactical levels, and an indicator is only data until analysis adds meaning and a recommended action.","core":true},{"t":"What you monitor","gist":"You watch for leaked credentials, mentions of your brand and executives, 'data for sale' posts that often come days before a breach is public, ransomware leak sites and access offers, on Tor but also Telegram and clearnet forums.","core":true},{"t":"Tools","gist":"Commercial platforms like Recorded Future and Flashpoint collect at scale so analysts aren't exposed to raw forums all day; open tools include Ahmia and Have I Been Pwned. Capture evidence with UTC timestamps and SHA-256 hashes.","core":false},{"t":"Rules of engagement (non-negotiable)","gist":"Never buy anything, never download outside a sandbox, never test stolen credentials, and never engage with actors without legal approval. Get legal review, document everything, and stop and report if you meet child sexual abuse material.","core":true},{"t":"Writing the report","gist":"Lead with the bottom line. State judgments with calibrated confidence words like 'likely' (55–80%), grade your sources, add impact, actions with owners and machine-readable indicators, and mark who may see it with TLP.","core":true},{"t":"How it feeds Domain 21 and Domain 8 careers","gist":"For the blue team, findings become detections and triggers, like a credential dump driving a password reset. For pentesters, leaked credentials are in scope only when the rules of engagement say so.","core":false},{"t":"CTIA mapping","gist":"How CTIA's modules map to this domain, plus the exam facts: CTIA is 50 questions in two hours at 70% passing; GCTI is 82 questions in three hours at 71%.","core":false}],"remember":[{"p":"The intelligence cycle: requirements, collection, processing, analysis, dissemination, feedback.","part":0},{"p":"Rules of engagement: never purchase, never download outside a sandbox, never test credentials, never engage actors without legal approval.","part":3},{"p":"ICD 203 confidence bands: 'likely' is 55–80%, 'very likely' 80–95%, 'almost certain' 95–99%.","part":4},{"p":"Admiralty grading pairs source reliability A to F with information credibility 1 to 6; an unknown poster confirmed by your logs might be F2.","part":4},{"p":"There is no dark-web certification: monitoring is a CTI skill, certified through EC-Council's CTIA and GIAC's GCTI.","part":6}]},"p48m1":{"big":"Number fluency means carrying units through every step and estimating roughly first, so you can tell within ten seconds whether a download time, bill or drive size is plausible.","why":"Every bandwidth plan, storage order, power budget and cloud bill you check depends on these moves, and a misplaced factor of 8 or 1,000 is the most common mistake in engineering reports.","parts":[{"t":"Fractions, ratios and percentages","gist":"Fractions add over a common denominator, multiply straight across, and divide by flipping the second one. Successive percentage changes multiply rather than add, and 40% to 50% is 10 percentage points but a 25% relative rise.","core":true},{"t":"Powers of ten and powers of two","gist":"Exponent laws run two prefix families: SI counts in powers of ten, binary in powers of two (KiB = 1,024 bytes). The gap grows to 10% at tera, which is why a 1 TB drive shows 931 GB in Windows.","core":true},{"t":"Scientific notation and significant figures","gist":"Scientific notation writes a number as m × 10 to a power; to multiply, multiply the front parts (mantissas) and add the exponents. Report only digits you actually measured: round answers to your least precise input.","core":true},{"t":"Units and dimensional analysis","gist":"Treat units like algebra: multiply by conversion factors equal to 1, like 8 bits per byte, until only the wanted unit is left. If the units don't cancel, the formula is wrong. Watts are a rate; watt-hours are an amount.","core":true},{"t":"Estimation and sanity checks","gist":"Round each input to one significant figure, multiply and track the powers of ten; that gets the order of magnitude in seconds. Then check units, magnitude, direction and extremes before trusting any answer.","core":true}],"remember":[{"p":"Successive percentage changes multiply: up 50% then down 50% leaves 0.75 of the start, a net loss of 25%.","part":0},{"p":"A percentage point is an absolute difference: 40% to 50% is 10 points but a 25% relative increase.","part":0},{"p":"1 KiB = 1,024 bytes; a 1 TB drive is 931 GiB, which Windows labels GB. Nothing is missing.","part":1},{"p":"Lowercase b is bits, uppercase B is bytes: a 1,000 Mb/s link moves at most 125 MB/s.","part":3},{"p":"Sanity-check every answer in order: units, magnitude, sign and direction, then extremes.","part":4}]},"p48m2":{"big":"An equation stays true if you do the same thing to both sides, which lets you run a formula backwards to find the input you need.","why":"Real work gives you the target and asks for the input, from resistor values to TCP windows to which cloud plan is cheaper, and rearranging formulas is exactly that.","parts":[{"t":"Expressions: the grammar","gist":"Combine like terms, simplify with the distributive law, and factor by running it backwards. Watch the order of operations: in Python -2**2 is -4. And (a + b)² is a² + 2ab + b², not a² + b².","core":true},{"t":"Linear equations: the balance","gist":"Solve a linear equation by undoing operations in reverse order, doing each step to both sides, then plug the answer back in to check. With fractions, multiply both sides by the common denominator first.","core":true},{"t":"Rearranging formulas","gist":"Rearranging uses the same moves to solve for a different letter. Ohm's law V = IR gives R = V/I, so an LED dropping 2 V on a 5 V supply at 20 mA needs a 150 Ω resistor.","core":true},{"t":"Inequalities and cost models","gist":"Inequalities solve like equations, with one extra rule: multiplying or dividing by a negative flips the direction. They express budgets and tolerances, like when renting a GPU at $0.80 an hour stops being cheaper than buying one for $1,600.","core":true},{"t":"Quadratic equations","gist":"Solve a quadratic by factoring, or with the quadratic formula when it won't factor. The discriminant b² − 4ac tells you in advance: positive means two real roots, zero one, negative none. Keep only roots that make physical sense.","core":true},{"t":"Systems of equations","gist":"A system asks for values that satisfy several equations at once; solve by substitution or elimination. Setting two price plans equal finds the break-even point, 100 GB here, and parallel lines mean no solution.","core":true}],"remember":[{"p":"In Python, -2**2 is -4 because exponentiation binds tighter than the minus; write (-2)**2 when you mean 4.","part":0},{"p":"(a + b)² = a² + 2ab + b², not a² + b².","part":0},{"p":"Bandwidth-delay product: window = throughput × RTT, so filling 1 Gb/s over 50 ms needs 6.25 MB in flight.","part":2},{"p":"Multiplying or dividing an inequality by a negative number reverses its direction.","part":3},{"p":"The discriminant b² − 4ac: positive gives two real roots, zero one repeated root, negative none on the real line.","part":4}]},"p48m3":{"big":"A function maps each input to exactly one output, and the shape of its growth, linear, polynomial, exponential or logarithmic, tells you whether a system or algorithm will scale.","why":"It lets you read a performance chart or an algorithm's running time and know what growth you face, and decibels are the language of every fiber, Wi-Fi and RF measurement you take.","parts":[{"t":"Functions: domain, range, composition, inverse","gist":"A function gives exactly one output per input. The domain is the allowed inputs, the range the outputs that occur. Composing functions chains them, and order matters; an inverse undoes a function, and only one-to-one functions have one.","core":true},{"t":"Linear functions and slope","gist":"A line y = mx + b changes at a constant rate: slope m is rise over run, and b is the value at x = 0. In a bill, slope is the rate and the intercept the fixed cost.","core":true},{"t":"Quadratics and polynomials","gist":"A quadratic graphs as a parabola with its vertex at x = −b/(2a). For a polynomial, the highest-power term dominates for large inputs, so 3n² + 100n + 7 behaves like 3n²: the seed of Big-O.","core":true},{"t":"Exponential functions","gist":"An exponential multiplies by the same factor at every step and eventually beats every polynomial: 2ⁿ passes n³ at n = 10. Growth at 7% a year nearly doubles in 10 years, which is the 'rule of 72'.","core":true},{"t":"Logarithms","gist":"A logarithm counts how many times you multiply: log₂ 1,024 = 10. Logs turn multiplication into addition. Binary search over a million items takes about 20 halvings, and numbering N things needs log₂ N bits, rounded up.","core":true},{"t":"Log scales and decibels","gist":"A decibel is 10 log₁₀ of a power ratio: +3 dB doubles, +10 dB is ten times, and dBm is relative to 1 mW. The payoff is that multiplied losses become added decibels, so a fiber link's losses simply sum.","core":true},{"t":"Growth rates and algorithms","gist":"At a billion operations a second, n log n sorts a million items in about 20 ms while n² takes about 17 minutes. A computer ten times faster barely helps a bad algorithm, so shape matters more than constants.","core":true}],"remember":[{"p":"Subtracting inside, f(x − 3), shifts the graph right by 3, not left.","part":0},{"p":"log(ab) = log a + log b; there is no simple rule for log(a + b).","part":4},{"p":"Numbering N things needs ⌈log₂ N⌉ bits: 5,000 hosts need 13 bits, because 12 bits give only 4,096.","part":4},{"p":"Decibels: +3 dB doubles power, +10 dB is ×10, and 0 dBm = 1 mW; losses in dB add.","part":5},{"p":"At 10⁹ operations per second, a million items take about 20 ms with n log n but about 17 minutes with n².","part":6}]},"p48m4":{"big":"Trigonometry and vectors describe direction, rotation and repetition with numbers: sine waves carry signals, rotations move graphics, and the dot product measures how closely two vectors point the same way.","why":"Waves carry every wireless signal you will analyze, rotations draw every game frame, and cosine similarity is how retrieval in local AI systems finds the right document.","parts":[{"t":"Angles, radians and right triangles","gist":"Programming languages measure angles in radians: a full turn is 2π, so π rad = 180°, and math.sin(90) is not 1. In a right triangle, a² + b² = c², and sine, cosine and tangent are ratios of its sides.","core":true},{"t":"The unit circle","gist":"Walk angle θ around a circle of radius 1 and you land at (cos θ, sin θ), so sin²θ + cos²θ = 1. To turn coordinates back into an angle, use atan2(y, x), which gets the quadrant right.","core":true},{"t":"Sine waves","gist":"A sine wave is described by amplitude, frequency, period (1/f) and phase. Wavelength is speed ÷ frequency, so 2.4 GHz Wi-Fi has a 12.5 cm wavelength. To capture a wave digitally, sample faster than twice its highest frequency.","core":true},{"t":"Rotation","gist":"Rotating a point uses two formulas built from cos θ and sin θ; rotating (1, 0) by 90° gives (0, 1). Written as a 2 × 2 matrix, it is why graphics hardware is built to multiply matrices fast.","core":true},{"t":"Vectors","gist":"A vector has magnitude and direction, written as components like (3, 4), whose length is 5. Add vectors component by component, and divide by the length to get a unit vector. An AI embedding is just a long vector.","core":true},{"t":"The dot product","gist":"Multiply matching components and add: a · b = |a||b| cos θ. Positive means roughly the same direction, zero perpendicular, negative opposed. Divided by both lengths it becomes cosine similarity, which AI search uses to rank documents.","core":true}],"remember":[{"p":"Math libraries use radians: π rad = 180°, so write math.sin(math.radians(90)) to get 1.","part":0},{"p":"Use atan2(y, x), not atan(y/x): it returns the correct quadrant and does not fail at x = 0.","part":1},{"p":"Wavelength = speed ÷ frequency: 2.4 GHz Wi-Fi is 12.5 cm, so a quarter-wave antenna element is about 3.1 cm.","part":2},{"p":"Rotation by θ: x′ = x cos θ − y sin θ, and y′ = x sin θ + y cos θ.","part":3},{"p":"Cosine similarity = a · b ÷ (|a||b|), from −1 to 1; a dot product of zero means the vectors are perpendicular.","part":5}]},"p48m5":{"big":"The derivative measures how fast something is changing right now: the slope of the tangent line, found as the limit of average rates over ever-shorter intervals.","why":"Every neural network in Domain 9 learns by computing derivatives with the chain rule and stepping against the gradient, and engineers use derivatives to linearize sensors, circuits and control loops.","parts":[{"t":"From average rate to instantaneous rate","gist":"An average rate is the slope between two points; shrink the gap toward zero and you get the instantaneous rate, the derivative. Worked by hand, the derivative of x² is 2x, so its slope at x = 3 is 6.","core":true},{"t":"Limits: the idea underneath","gist":"A limit asks what value f(x) approaches, even where f itself is undefined. Limits at infinity are what Big-O means by dropping lower-order terms, and L'Hôpital's rule handles 0/0 by differentiating top and bottom.","core":true},{"t":"The rules","gist":"A dozen rules replace the limit: power, product, quotient and chain, plus eˣ → eˣ, ln x → 1/x and sin → cos. The chain rule multiplies rates along a chain, and it is backpropagation in miniature.","core":true},{"t":"What the derivative says about a graph","gist":"Where f′ is positive the function rises, where negative it falls, where zero it is momentarily flat. f″ shows curvature, cup or cap. The tangent line gives a quick approximation: f(x + Δx) ≈ f(x) + f′(x)Δx.","core":true},{"t":"Partial derivatives and gradients","gist":"A partial derivative changes one input while holding the others constant; stacking them gives the gradient, which points steepest uphill. Stepping against it is training: one step cut a squared-error loss from 25 to 6.25.","core":true},{"t":"Numerical derivatives","gist":"When you can't differentiate by hand, approximate: the central difference [f(x + h) − f(x − h)]/(2h) is far more accurate than the forward one. But don't make h tiny, or subtracting nearly equal numbers destroys the digits.","core":true}],"remember":[{"p":"f′(x) = lim (h→0) [f(x + h) − f(x)]/h; the derivative of x² is 2x.","part":0},{"p":"Chain rule: d/dx f(g(x)) = f′(g(x)) · g′(x); always multiply by the inside's derivative.","part":2},{"p":"Product rule: (fg)′ = f′g + fg′, not the product of the derivatives.","part":2},{"p":"The gradient points in the direction of steepest increase; gradient descent steps the opposite way.","part":4},{"p":"For a numerical derivative, use a central difference with h ≈ 10⁻⁵ to 10⁻⁶; a much smaller h makes the error grow again.","part":5}]},"p48m6":{"big":"An integral adds up a rate over time to give the total, and the fundamental theorem says integrating is differentiating run backwards.","why":"Turning watts into kilowatt-hours, throughput into bytes and latency densities into probabilities are everyday engineering tasks, and the same integrals sit under probability and alerting.","parts":[{"t":"Accumulation and Riemann sums","gist":"When a rate changes, slice time thin, treat the rate as constant in each slice, and add up the rectangles. As the slices get thinner, the sum converges to the definite integral, the total amount.","core":true},{"t":"Antiderivatives","gist":"An antiderivative runs a derivative rule backwards, like ∫ xⁿ dx = xⁿ⁺¹/(n + 1) + C. The + C is unknown until a starting value pins it down. Check any answer by differentiating it.","core":true},{"t":"The fundamental theorem","gist":"To find a definite integral, take any antiderivative F and compute F(b) − F(a). The same idea gives the area between two curves, top minus bottom, and the average value of a function over an interval.","core":true},{"t":"Growth and decay: y′ = ky","gist":"When something changes at a rate proportional to its size, y′ = ky gives y = y₀eᵏᵗ. That covers growth, decay and a capacitor discharging: after one RC time constant it holds about 37% of its starting voltage.","core":true},{"t":"Probability densities","gist":"For a continuous quantity like latency, probabilities are areas under a density curve whose total area is 1. For random arrivals, the chance of waiting longer than t is e^(−λt).","core":true},{"t":"Numerical integration","gist":"When no formula exists, or you only have samples, integrate numerically. The trapezoid rule's error shrinks like h², Simpson's rule like h⁴, and NumPy and SciPy do both in one line.","core":true}],"remember":[{"p":"∫ xⁿ dx = xⁿ⁺¹/(n + 1) + C for n ≠ −1, and ∫ 1/x dx = ln|x| + C.","part":1},{"p":"Fundamental theorem: ∫ₐᵇ f(x) dx = F(b) − F(a) for any antiderivative F.","part":2},{"p":"y′ = ky gives y = y₀eᵏᵗ; doubling time is ln 2 / k and half-life ln 2 / |k|.","part":3},{"p":"A density can exceed 1; only areas under it are probabilities, and its total area is 1.","part":4},{"p":"Trapezoid rule error shrinks like h², Simpson's like h⁴, and Simpson's rule is exact for polynomials up to degree 3.","part":5}]},"p48m7":{"big":"Computers optimize and solve equations by repeated steps on finite-precision numbers, so step size, algorithm choice and stable formulas decide whether the answer is right.","why":"Get this right and models train, simulations stay stable and money reconciles; get it wrong and you reproduce famous disasters.","parts":[{"t":"Minima and maxima","gist":"Look for critical points where f′ = 0, then classify them: f″ > 0 is a minimum, f″ < 0 a maximum. On a closed interval, also check the endpoints. At the batch-size optimum, the two competing costs are equal.","core":true},{"t":"Gradient descent","gist":"Gradient descent steps downhill, x ← x − η f′(x), with η the learning rate. On (x − 3)² each step multiplies the error by 1 − 2η: η = 0.1 converges, 1.0 oscillates, 1.1 diverges.","core":true},{"t":"Newton's method and bisection","gist":"Newton's method jumps to where the tangent line crosses zero, and the correct digits roughly double each step. Bisection halves an interval that brackets a root: slower, but guaranteed if the function is continuous.","core":true},{"t":"Floating point: IEEE 754","gist":"Computers store real numbers as binary scientific notation. A double has 52 fraction bits and machine epsilon about 2.22 × 10⁻¹⁶. Most decimals, like 0.1, aren't exact, so compare approximate results with a tolerance and keep money in integer cents.","core":true},{"t":"Rounding error and catastrophic cancellation","gist":"Subtracting two nearly equal numbers keeps only their differing digits, so the relative error explodes. The cure is a stable formula that avoids the subtraction, not just more digits. Errors also pile up in long loops.","core":true},{"t":"Iterative solvers and stopping rules","gist":"Huge systems are often solved by repeating an update until it settles, like the Jacobi method. Always set a stopping rule: stop when the change or residual falls below a tolerance, and cap the number of iterations.","core":false}],"remember":[{"p":"At a critical point f′ = 0; f″ > 0 means a minimum and f″ < 0 a maximum. On a closed interval, check the endpoints too.","part":0},{"p":"Newton's method converges quadratically, roughly doubling the correct digits each step; gradient descent converges linearly.","part":2},{"p":"A double has machine epsilon about 2.22 × 10⁻¹⁶ and stores integers exactly only up to 2⁵³.","part":3},{"p":"0.1 + 0.2 shows as 0.30000000000000004: compare approximate results with a tolerance like math.isclose, and keep money in integer cents or Decimal.","part":3},{"p":"Subtracting nearly equal numbers destroys digits (catastrophic cancellation); rewrite the formula to avoid the subtraction.","part":4}]},"p49m1":{"big":"C++ keeps C's machine model and adds one key idea: an object's cleanup runs automatically when it goes out of scope, so resources are released on every exit path.","why":"Most large binaries you will reverse engineer in Domain 11 and audit in Domain 8 are C++, and classes and namespaces are 29% of the CPA exam.","parts":[{"t":"What C++ adds, and the zero-overhead rule","gist":"C++'s rule is zero overhead: what you don't use costs nothing, and what you do use you couldn't hand-code better. A class without virtual functions is laid out like a C struct. C++ is not a strict superset of C.","core":true},{"t":"References: a second name, not an address you manage","gist":"A reference is another name for an existing object: it must be initialized, can't be re-seated and isn't null in a correct program. Passing const references avoids copies, but a reference to a local still dangles.","core":true},{"t":"Classes, constructors and destructors","gist":"A constructor sets up an object; its destructor runs automatically when the object's lifetime ends. Locals are destroyed in reverse order of construction. A virtual function adds a hidden 8-byte pointer to a table of functions.","core":true},{"t":"RAII: the idea that makes C++ C++","gist":"RAII means acquire a resource in a constructor and release it in the destructor, so the compiler cleans up on every exit path, including exceptions. It replaces Domain 15's goto cleanup, and the whole standard library works this way.","core":true},{"t":"Const-correctness and compile-time evaluation","gist":"Mark everything that shouldn't change as const and the compiler enforces it; read declarations right to left. constexpr runs code at compile time, so a static_assert can fail the build instead of the field test.","core":true},{"t":"Namespaces, overloading and name mangling","gist":"Namespaces keep names from colliding, and overloading means the compiler encodes each function's signature into its symbol: int add(int, int) becomes _Z3addii. extern \"C\" turns mangling off, the doorway to C and Rust.","core":true},{"t":"The compilation model: headers, translation units and the ODR","gist":"Each .cpp file plus all it includes is one translation unit, compiled separately; including iostream alone pasted in 43,165 lines with GCC 13. The one definition rule: each non-inline function is defined exactly once, inline ones identically everywhere.","core":true}],"remember":[{"p":"Destructors run automatically at the end of an object's lifetime, and locals are destroyed in reverse order of construction.","part":2},{"p":"Adding a virtual function adds a hidden 8-byte vtable pointer: sizeof(Link) grows from 16 to 24.","part":2},{"p":"RAII: acquire in the constructor, release in the destructor, and cleanup runs on every exit path, including exceptions.","part":3},{"p":"Read const declarations right to left: const char* p points to const char; char* const p is a const pointer to char.","part":4},{"p":"Itanium name mangling encodes the signature: int add(int, int) becomes _Z3addii, and extern \"C\" switches mangling off.","part":5}]},"p49m2":{"big":"Templates let the compiler stamp out a specialized copy of generic code for each type, and iterators let one set of algorithms work across every container at hand-written speed.","why":"The CPP exam is almost entirely containers, algorithms and templates, and choosing the right container with numbers is a standard performance-interview question.","parts":[{"t":"Templates: code that writes code","gist":"A template is a pattern the compiler stamps out per type, so std::sort gets a specialized, inlined comparison. It sorted 10 million ints in 1,014 ms against qsort's 2,066 ms. The cost is code size and compile time.","core":true},{"t":"Containers and their guarantees","gist":"The standard promises each container's complexity: vector push_back is amortized O(1) because capacity doubles, map lookups are O(log n), unordered_map O(1) on average. Cache behavior matters too: summing a vector was nine times faster than a list.","core":true},{"t":"Iterators: the glue","gist":"An iterator is a generalized pointer, and two of them mark a half-open range [begin, end). Algorithms state which iterator category they need. A vector push_back that reallocates invalidates every iterator into it.","core":true},{"t":"Algorithms, lambdas and ranges","gist":"The standard library has more than a hundred tested algorithms, like sort, lower_bound and nth_element, so you rarely need hand-written loops. Lambdas supply the behavior inline, and C++20 ranges compose whole-container steps lazily.","core":true},{"t":"Concepts: saying what a template needs","gist":"A concept is a named compile-time check on a type, tested at the call site. Instead of a hundred-line error from deep inside a template, you get a short message naming the requirement and the type that failed it.","core":true},{"t":"Generic programming and what it costs","gist":"Generic code costs nothing at run time, but name the real costs in reviews: binary size, compile time and long diagnostics. When you need one function for many types, type erasure like std::function trades speed for size.","core":false}],"remember":[{"p":"std::sort beats qsort because the comparator is inlined, not called through a function pointer for every comparison.","part":0},{"p":"vector push_back is amortized O(1): capacity doubles, so a million pushes took only 21 reallocations.","part":1},{"p":"Same Big-O, different speed: summing 10 million ints took 6.3 ms in a vector and 57 ms in a list, because contiguous memory streams through the cache.","part":1},{"p":"map is a red-black tree with O(log n) lookups; unordered_map is a hash table, O(1) on average but O(n) in the worst case.","part":1},{"p":"A vector push_back that reallocates invalidates every iterator, pointer and reference into the vector.","part":2}]},"p49m3":{"big":"C++ writes ownership into types, with moves that hand resources over instead of copying, but it still compiles memory bugs, so sanitizers must catch what the type system misses.","why":"Ownership bugs are the memory-safety CVEs that Domains 7, 8 and 11 hunt, and reading a sanitizer report is a daily skill for any C++ job.","parts":[{"t":"Copies are expensive; moves are theft","gist":"Copying a 10-million-int vector took about 22–26 ms; moving it took about 50 ns, because a move just takes the source's three pointers and leaves it empty. Move when the source is about to die anyway.","core":true},{"t":"Value categories without the headache","gist":"Every expression is an lvalue (named and lasting), a prvalue (a nameless temporary) or an xvalue (something you said you no longer need). std::move moves nothing: it is just a cast that makes the move overload eligible.","core":true},{"t":"The rule of zero, three and five","gist":"Best is the rule of zero: build classes from self-managing members and write none of the five special members (destructor, copies, moves). If you write one, think about all five, and make the move constructor noexcept.","core":true},{"t":"Smart pointers: ownership you can read in the type","gist":"unique_ptr means sole ownership, costs nothing over a raw pointer and can only be moved. shared_ptr shares ownership by counting references, but cycles never reach zero and leak; weak_ptr observes without owning and breaks them.","core":true},{"t":"The bugs C++ still lets you write","gist":"Dangling references after a vector reallocates, views of destroyed strings, use-after-free, use after move and signed overflow all compile cleanly with -Wall -Wextra. Compiling without warnings is not proof of safety.","core":true},{"t":"Sanitizers in practice","gist":"AddressSanitizer, about 2× slower, catches use-after-free and shows where memory was freed and where it was read; UBSan catches undefined behavior like signed overflow. Sanitizers only see the paths your tests run: a net, not a proof.","core":true}],"remember":[{"p":"std::move is only a cast to T&&; the actual move happens in the move constructor or move assignment it makes eligible.","part":1},{"p":"A moved-from standard object is valid but unspecified: you may destroy it or assign to it, but don't read it expecting data.","part":1},{"p":"vector moves elements during growth only if their move constructor is noexcept; otherwise it copies them.","part":2},{"p":"Prefer unique_ptr (8 bytes, sole owner); use shared_ptr only for truly shared lifetimes, and break cycles with weak_ptr.","part":3},{"p":"AddressSanitizer: compile with -fsanitize=address -fno-omit-frame-pointer -g; it catches heap-use-after-free on the paths your tests run.","part":5}]},"p49m4":{"big":"A professional C++ project is the language plus a toolchain: features that pay for themselves, threads that are correct rather than lucky, CMake builds, tests, analyzers and a debugger.","why":"Every C++ job and every open-source project you will audit uses this toolchain, and race and deadlock diagnosis is a standard systems interview question.","parts":[{"t":"The features worth knowing","gist":"C++20 and C++23 add concepts, ranges, span, format, jthread, expected and print; C++26 adds reflection, contracts and a hardened library. Compiler support lags the standard, so check before you depend on a feature.","core":false},{"t":"Threads, done correctly","gist":"A std::thread must be joined or detached before it is destroyed; jthread joins itself. Shared data needs a lock or an atomic. Two threads adding a million each to a shared counter never reached 2,000,000; ThreadSanitizer named the race.","core":true},{"t":"The memory model, intuitively","gist":"If every conflicting access is ordered by locks or atomics, your program behaves as if all operations happened in one global order. A release store and an acquire load publish data between threads. volatile is not synchronization.","core":true},{"t":"CMake: the build you will actually meet","gist":"Modern CMake is target-based: declare targets and attach requirements to them. PUBLIC requirements flow to whatever links the target and PRIVATE ones don't; that distinction is most of what 'modern CMake' means.","core":true},{"t":"Testing and static analysis","gist":"Use many fast unit tests with GoogleTest or Catch2, run by ctest, plus a sanitizer build of the whole suite on every commit. Fuzz parsers, treat warnings as errors in CI, and run clang-tidy.","core":true},{"t":"Debugging with gdb and lldb","gist":"Build with -g -Og, then use gdb or lldb to set breakpoints, read backtraces and watch a variable change. For a hang, attach and read every thread's stack: two threads each waiting on the other's lock is a deadlock.","core":true}],"remember":[{"p":"Destroying a joinable std::thread calls std::terminate; std::jthread joins in its destructor.","part":1},{"p":"ThreadSanitizer (-fsanitize=thread) names a data race and the variable involved on the first run.","part":1},{"p":"Race fixes, slowest to fastest: a mutex around the update, an atomic fetch_add, per-thread counters summed after join.","part":1},{"p":"relaxed gives atomicity only; acquire/release give one-way publication; seq_cst, the default, gives one global order.","part":2},{"p":"In CMake, PUBLIC requirements flow to consumers of a target and PRIVATE ones do not.","part":3}]},"p49m5":{"big":"In safe Rust, the compiler enforces one owner and either many readers or one writer, turning dangling references and iterator invalidation into compile errors and making double frees impossible.","why":"Memory-safe languages are now what CISA and the NSA recommend for new code, and reading borrow-checker errors fluently is the first skill every Rust employer screens for.","parts":[{"t":"Ownership and moves","gist":"Every value has exactly one owner and is dropped when that owner leaves scope. Assigning a String moves it, and using the old name is error E0382. Plain data like i32 is copied; anything else needs an explicit .clone().","core":true},{"t":"Borrowing: shared XOR mutable","gist":"At any moment you may have many shared references or exactly one mutable one, never both. So a reference into a vector followed by a push is rejected with E0502, the same bug AddressSanitizer caught at run time in C++.","core":true},{"t":"Lifetimes","gist":"A lifetime is the region where a borrow must stay valid, checked at compile time. The compiler rejects any reference that would outlive its data; when it can't infer which input a result borrows from, you add a named lifetime.","core":true},{"t":"Slices, strings and checked arithmetic","gist":"A slice is a borrowed view, a 16-byte pointer plus length. Indexing past the end panics instead of reading stray memory, and integer overflow is defined: it panics in debug builds and wraps in release builds.","core":true},{"t":"What the borrow checker buys, and what it costs","gist":"Safe Rust turns use-after-free, iterator invalidation and data races into compile errors and buffer overflows into panics. It does not prevent leaks, deadlocks or logic bugs, and some correct programs, like doubly linked lists, get rejected.","core":true}],"remember":[{"p":"Rust's ownership rules: each value has one owner, one owner at a time, and the value is dropped when its owner goes out of scope.","part":0},{"p":"Using a value after it moved is error E0382; a Rust move leaves no moved-from state to misuse.","part":0},{"p":"Shared XOR mutable: many &T or exactly one &mut T at a time, never both.","part":1},{"p":"Integer overflow panics in debug builds and wraps in release builds: defined, never undefined.","part":3},{"p":"The borrow checker does not prevent memory leaks: an Rc cycle leaks, and std::mem::forget is a safe function.","part":4}]},"p49m6":{"big":"Rust's types let you make illegal states impossible to represent: enums carry data and must be matched exhaustively, and errors are values the compiler won't let you ignore.","why":"The compiler flags every place a new case must be handled and every unchecked error. And with no recognized Rust exam, a clean crate with typed errors, tests and passing Clippy is what employers read.","parts":[{"t":"Structs and impl blocks","gist":"A struct holds the fields and a separate impl block holds the methods. There are no constructors, and every field must be set, so a half-built value can't exist. Fields are private unless marked pub.","core":true},{"t":"Enums are sum types; match is exhaustive","gist":"Each variant of a Rust enum can carry its own data, and a match must handle every variant or the code won't compile (error E0004). Add a new case and the compiler finds every place that must handle it.","core":true},{"t":"Option and Result: errors are values","gist":"Rust has no null and no exceptions. Option means 'maybe a value', Result means 'a value or an error', and you must handle both cases. The ? operator returns errors to the caller with almost no typing.","core":true},{"t":"Traits and generics","gist":"A trait is an interface: behavior that types implement. Generic functions get one compiled copy per type, at zero run-time cost (static dispatch). dyn trait objects let mixed types share one collection, with calls through a lookup table (dynamic dispatch).","core":true},{"t":"Iterators and closures","gist":"Iterator chains like filter and map read like Python but compile to the same machine code as a hand-written loop. Closures, small unnamed functions, capture variables by borrow or by move.","core":false},{"t":"Cargo, crates and Clippy","gist":"Cargo builds, tests, documents and formats your project and manages its dependencies. Clippy, the official linter, flags unidiomatic code; run it in CI so warnings fail the build, and check dependencies with cargo audit.","core":true}],"remember":[{"p":"Methods take &self to read, &mut self to modify, or self to consume the value: the ownership rules applied to the receiver.","part":0},{"p":"A match on an enum must cover every variant; miss one and rustc refuses to compile with error E0004.","part":1},{"p":"Return Result for failures a caller can handle and use ? to pass them up; panic only for bugs, so unwrap() in a library is a code smell.","part":2},{"p":"Generics are monomorphized, one copy per type with static dispatch; only dyn Trait objects use a vtable, through a 16-byte fat pointer.","part":3},{"p":"Run cargo clippy -- -D warnings in CI so lints fail the build; cargo audit checks dependencies against the RustSec database.","part":5}]},"p49m7":{"big":"Rust's type system rules out data races in safe code through Send and Sync; unsafe and FFI are small, documented escape hatches, and choosing a language should rest on evidence.","why":"Employers and government guidance now ask 'C++ or Rust for this?' directly. Answering with evidence and honest caveats, not loyalty, is what separates an engineer from a fan.","parts":[{"t":"Send and Sync: thread safety in the type system","gist":"Send means a value can move to another thread, Sync that it can be shared. Rc is neither, so sending one won't compile (E0277). Arc plus Mutex works, and the Mutex owns its data, so nothing touches it unlocked.","core":true},{"t":"Async, at an intuitive level","gist":"An async function compiles into a state machine, a Future, that can pause at each .await and is driven by a runtime such as Tokio. It suits many mostly idle connections; CPU-bound work belongs on threads.","core":false},{"t":"Unsafe Rust: five superpowers, no off switch","gist":"An unsafe block allows exactly five extra things, such as following raw pointers and calling C, but the borrow checker stays on. Keep unsafe small, explain each block in a SAFETY comment, and check it with Miri.","core":true},{"t":"FFI: calling C, and being called by it","gist":"Rust talks to C through the C ABI, the shared rules for calling functions and laying out data. Calls into C go inside unsafe. The hard part is ownership: memory must go back to the allocator that made it.","core":true},{"t":"What governments and industry now say","gist":"About 70% of the vulnerabilities (CVEs) Microsoft assigns are memory-safety bugs. CISA, the NSA and the FBI urge memory-safe languages for new code and roadmaps for existing code. It is guidance, not law, and doesn't require rewrites.","core":true},{"t":"Performance, honestly","gist":"With the same algorithm and data layout, C, C++ and Rust land close together, and the winner flips with the workload. Rust's real costs include binary size and compile time. Benchmarks suggest; profiles of your own workload decide.","core":true},{"t":"Choosing a language","gist":"The usual picks: Rust for new network-facing code and parsers, C++ with hardening for big existing codebases, and C or supported embedded Rust on microcontrollers. Rust has no widely recognized certification, so a portfolio is your proof.","core":true}],"remember":[{"p":"Send means a value can move to another thread; Sync means it can be shared. Rc is neither, so sending one gives error E0277.","part":0},{"p":"Safe Rust rules out data races, but deadlocks and logic-level race conditions are still possible.","part":0},{"p":"unsafe adds exactly five permissions, like dereferencing raw pointers and calling foreign functions; the borrow checker and type checker stay on.","part":2},{"p":"Across the FFI, memory goes back to the allocator that made it: a Rust buffer is freed by an exported Rust function, never by C's free.","part":3},{"p":"The CISA, NSA and FBI memory-safety publications are voluntary guidance: memory-safe languages for new code, roadmaps for existing code, hardening for what can't move.","part":4}]},"p50m1":{"big":"TypeScript checks values by their shape before the program runs; then all types are erased, so nothing is checked while it runs.","why":"Knowing exactly what the checker does and does not prove is how you write safe code and find bugs in others' code during an authorized review.","parts":[{"t":"Types are sets; compatibility is shape","gist":"Think of a type as the set of values it allows. TypeScript decides fit by shape, not name: any value with the required properties fits. A branded type keeps look-alikes apart, like feet and meters, at zero run-time cost.","core":true},{"t":"Narrowing and type guards","gist":"The checker follows if, switch and return and shrinks a variable's type on each path after checks like typeof, in or ===, or your own type guard. Use unknown, not any, for uninspected data.","core":true},{"t":"Discriminated unions and exhaustiveness","gist":"A discriminated union is a union of object types sharing one literal tag, like kind. Switch on the tag and each branch knows its variant; assign the leftover to never and a forgotten case becomes a compile error.","core":true},{"t":"Generics, conditional and mapped types","gist":"Generics are type parameters filled in at each call, so what goes in decides the related type that comes out. Conditional and mapped types compute new types, and utility types like Partial, Pick and Omit cover daily needs.","core":true},{"t":"What erases, and the soundness holes","gist":"All types are deleted before the program runs, so JSON.parse data is unchecked. A few old features, like enum, emit code, and Node's type stripping rejects them. any, as and ! are holes where the checker believes you without evidence.","core":true}],"remember":[{"p":"Assignability is by shape: a stored value may carry extra properties, but a fresh object literal with an unknown property fails the excess property check.","part":0},{"p":"any switches checking off and spreads; unknown accepts every value but allows nothing until you narrow it.","part":1},{"p":"Assign the leftover case to never to make a switch exhaustive: add a variant and tsc names it in a TS2322 error at the exact line.","part":2},{"p":"In K extends keyof T, keyof T is the union of T's keys, and the indexed access T[K] is the type stored at that key.","part":3},{"p":"enum, value namespaces and constructor parameter properties generate code, so Node's type stripping rejects them; use a frozen object plus a derived union.","part":4}]},"p50m2":{"big":"A trustworthy TypeScript project runs a strict checker as its own CI step and validates untrusted data at the boundary, because a cast proves nothing.","why":"Real projects fail at the boundaries, like bad data from another team's API or a missing CI check. These habits make a TypeScript codebase trustworthy rather than merely typed.","parts":[{"t":"tsconfig.json and strict mode","gist":"tsconfig.json says which files make up the project and how hard to check them. strict switches on a family of checks, each aimed at one kind of bug, and extra flags like noUncheckedIndexedAccess close more holes.","core":true},{"t":"Running TypeScript: tsc, type stripping, bundlers","gist":"Something must remove types before code runs: tsc compiles, Node.js strips them and runs .ts directly, and bundlers like Vite strip them for the browser. Only tsc checks, so run tsc --noEmit as its own CI step.","core":true},{"t":"Typing an API client end to end","gist":"res.json() returns any, and casting it with as Reel[] compiles but checks nothing, so bad data slips through as a wrong number. Add a timeout, check the status, treat parsed data as unknown and validate it.","core":true},{"t":"Runtime validation at the boundary","gist":"Because types erase, check untrusted data where it enters: HTTP bodies, files, environment variables. Parse it once into a typed value at the edge. Zod's z.infer derives the static type from the schema, so the two can't drift.","core":true},{"t":"Migrating a JavaScript file","gist":"Move JavaScript over in small steps that each keep the build green: add // @ts-check and JSDoc types first, then rename to .ts, then tighten one flag at a time while tracking the error count.","core":true}],"remember":[{"p":"Node.js type stripping runs .ts files without checking types, so a file full of type errors still runs; run tsc --noEmit in CI.","part":1},{"p":"A type assertion like as Reel[] is a promise the compiler believes without evidence; only runtime validation checks what actually arrived.","part":2},{"p":"fetch does not throw on HTTP 500, so check res.ok yourself; add AbortSignal.timeout so a dead server can't hang you.","part":2},{"p":"In Zod, z.infer derives the static type from the schema; safeParse returns success with data or an error, while parse throws.","part":3},{"p":"Prefer @ts-expect-error over @ts-ignore: it becomes an error itself once the problem is fixed, so it can't rot.","part":4}]},"p50m3":{"big":"Write most code as pure functions, same input giving same output with no side effects, and push effects like files and the clock to a thin outer shell.","why":"Pure, immutable code is the easiest to test and the safest under concurrency, and the same ideas run React, Spark and the data tools in later domains.","parts":[{"t":"Pure functions and side effects","gist":"A pure function gives the same output for the same input and has no side effects, like writing a file. That makes it easy to test and cache. Keep effects in a thin shell around a pure core.","core":true},{"t":"Immutability, honestly","gist":"Immutability means building a new value instead of changing the old one. const doesn't give it and Object.freeze is shallow; copy with spread or toSorted, or use Python's frozen dataclasses. Copying has a cost, which structural sharing mostly removes.","core":true},{"t":"Functions as values: higher-order functions and closures","gist":"Functions are values you can pass and return. A closure keeps the actual variables from where it was made, not copies, which explains the var-in-loop and Python late-binding bugs.","core":true},{"t":"map, filter and reduce in two languages","gist":"map transforms each item, filter keeps those that pass a test, and reduce combines them into one value. Always give reduce an initial value, because the empty case always arrives. Python prefers comprehensions and built-ins like sum.","core":true},{"t":"Recursion, the stack and memoization","gist":"Each recursive call uses a stack frame, and the stack is small: Python's default limit is 1,000 frames. Recurse over trees, loop over long lists. Memoization caches pure results, cutting fib(30) from 2,692,537 calls to 31.","core":true}],"remember":[{"p":"const stops you reassigning the variable, not changing the object; Object.freeze is shallow, so nested objects stay writable.","part":1},{"p":"A Python mutable default like batch=[] is created once, when def runs, so every call shares the same list.","part":2},{"p":"Always give reduce an initial value: in JavaScript, reducing an empty array without one throws a TypeError.","part":3},{"p":"Python's default recursion limit is 1,000 frames, and neither V8 nor CPython eliminates tail calls, so deep recursion fails.","part":4},{"p":"Memoization is correct only for pure functions; memoize something that reads the clock and you cache a lie.","part":4}]},"p50m4":{"big":"Design data with sum types so impossible states can't be written, and put absence and failure in the type with Option and Result so callers can't forget them.","why":"These techniques turn whole classes of runtime bugs into compile errors and make error handling impossible to forget, so code is correct by construction, not merely tested.","parts":[{"t":"Algebraic data types: counting states","gist":"A record (product type) multiplies its states and a tagged union (sum type) adds them. So a loading flag plus optional error and data has 8 shapes, only 3 meaningful; as a three-variant union, the other 5 can't exist.","core":true},{"t":"Pattern matching","gist":"Pattern matching checks a value's shape and pulls out its parts in one step. Python has match/case since 3.10; TypeScript uses a switch on the tag plus the never check. Cases are tried top to bottom, so order matters.","core":true},{"t":"Option and Result instead of null and exceptions","gist":"Option means 'some value or none' and Result means 'success or failure', so failure shows in the signature. Return a Result for expected failures, throw for bugs, and chain steps with andThen, which continues only on success.","core":true},{"t":"Composition, currying and pipelines","gist":"Composition builds one function out of others, and pipe runs them left to right, like a Unix pipe. Currying turns a two-argument function into one that takes the first and returns a function waiting for the second.","core":true},{"t":"Lazy evaluation","gist":"Lazy evaluation computes a value only when it is demanded. Generators work one element at a time: a million-element Python list peaks near 40 MB, its generator about 200 bytes. But a generator can be used only once.","core":true},{"t":"A taste of Haskell","gist":"In Haskell all of this is the default: pure functions, immutable data, currying, lazy evaluation and algebraic data types. A short program shows each idea in its native form, next to the TypeScript you already know.","core":false}],"remember":[{"p":"A product type's state count multiplies its fields' counts; a sum type's adds its variants': 2 × 3 = 6 versus 2 + 3 = 5.","part":0},{"p":"Match cases are tried top to bottom, so a guarded special case, like the square, must come before the general rectangle.","part":1},{"p":"Return a Result for expected failures like bad input or not found; throw exceptions for bugs and unrecoverable conditions.","part":2},{"p":"andThen, also called flatMap or bind, runs the next step only on success; an error flows through untouched.","part":2},{"p":"Lazy work runs when values are demanded, so errors surface where values are used, and a generator is consumed only once.","part":4}]},"p50m5":{"big":"A design pattern earns its place only by absorbing real variation; with first-class functions many become tiny, and one that absorbs none should be deleted.","why":"Interviewers and code reviewers speak in pattern names, and knowing when to delete a pattern is as valuable as knowing how to write one.","parts":[{"t":"SOLID, honestly","gist":"SOLID is five design principles, each with a real insight and a common misuse. Liskov is the precise one: a subtype must work anywhere its base type does. Dependency inversion is what makes code testable. Prefer composition over inheritance.","core":true},{"t":"Behavioral patterns: strategy, observer, command","gist":"Strategy swaps an algorithm, and here it's just a function parameter. Observer lets subscribers react to events, with an unsubscribe so they don't leak. Command turns an action into an object you can queue, log or undo.","core":true},{"t":"Structural and creational patterns","gist":"A decorator wraps a function to add behavior, like retries, without changing it. An adapter translates a vendor's interface into the one your code expects. A singleton is usually a disguised global: create one instance and pass it in.","core":true},{"t":"When a pattern is over-engineering","gist":"Every pattern costs readers another file and jump, so it must absorb real variation. Don't build an extension point before the second variation exists (YAGNI), extract shared code the third time (rule of three), and delete unjustified patterns.","core":true}],"remember":[{"p":"Liskov test: anywhere the base type works, the subtype must too, with no strengthened preconditions or weakened postconditions. Square extends Rectangle fails it.","part":0},{"p":"Prefer composition over inheritance, because inheritance couples a subclass to every implementation detail of its parent.","part":0},{"p":"In TypeScript and Python a strategy is just a function parameter: sorted(reels, key=used) is the strategy pattern in one argument.","part":1},{"p":"A singleton hides dependencies and makes tests order-dependent; create one instance in one place and pass it in.","part":2},{"p":"Rule of three: tolerate duplication twice and extract the abstraction the third time, when you can see what actually varies.","part":3}]},"p50m6":{"big":"Let the core business rules own their interfaces, called ports, and plug in the database, web and clock as swappable adapters, with all source dependencies pointing inward.","why":"This structure decides whether your tests take seconds or minutes and whether a database change touches one file or fifty.","parts":[{"t":"Layered architecture and its failure mode","gist":"Layered architecture stacks presentation, application, domain and data access, each calling the one below. Its flaw: dependencies point down to the database, so testing a rule needs one, and you often get an anemic domain model: bare data classes.","core":true},{"t":"Hexagonal: ports and adapters","gist":"The application sits in the middle and owns its ports, interfaces in its own words: driving ports it offers, driven ports it needs. Adapters outside translate, and the core imports nothing from them. The hexagon's six sides mean nothing.","core":true},{"t":"Dependency injection and the composition root","gist":"Dependency injection means a component is handed its collaborators instead of building them. The wiring lives in one place, the composition root, so swapping in SQLite changes one line. You don't need a framework for it.","core":true},{"t":"Testing seams","gist":"A seam is a place to change behavior without editing the code there, and every port is one. Use fakes, spies and stubs, like a fixed clock, so tests run in milliseconds; contract tests prove fakes match real adapters.","core":true},{"t":"Domain events, inside one process","gist":"Instead of calling each reaction directly, the core can publish a domain event, like ReelLow, to an in-process bus, so a new reaction touches no existing code. Once an event must leave the process, you need Domain 37's outbox.","core":true}],"remember":[{"p":"In hexagonal architecture, source-code dependencies point inward: the core imports nothing from the adapters, no framework and no database driver.","part":1},{"p":"Driving ports are what the application offers, like 'record an install'; driven ports are what it needs, like a repository, notifier or clock.","part":1},{"p":"The service locator, code that reaches into a global registry for its dependencies, hides exactly what injection makes visible.","part":2},{"p":"A fake is a real in-memory implementation, a spy records calls, and a stub returns a constant, like a clock fixed at one time.","part":3},{"p":"Hexagonal pays off when real domain rules meet two or more kinds of I/O, or tests must be fast and many; a small script needs none of it.","part":4}]},"p50m7":{"big":"The shell rewrites every line through fixed expansions, so unquoted variables split and glob; quote every expansion, handle important failures explicitly, and switch to Python when scripts grow.","why":"One unquoted variable can split a filename into three arguments or turn an empty value into /, which is how Steam's rm -rf erased users' files. Careful quoting and failure handling make a script a tool, not a disaster.","parts":[{"t":"How the shell reads a line","gist":"Before running a command, Bash expands it in a fixed order, and unquoted results are split at whitespace and have their wildcards expanded. So a filename with spaces can become three arguments. Quote every expansion and use arrays for lists.","core":true},{"t":"Parameter expansion toolkit","gist":"Parameter expansion does string work, like getting a basename or stripping an extension, without starting a process. ${var:?} aborts if a variable is empty, so rm -rf can't expand to /*, the Steam bug.","core":true},{"t":"set -euo pipefail, and its caveats","gist":"set -euo pipefail is a useful safety net, not a guarantee. The -e part is suspended inside if, while, && and ||, and fires on harmless statuses like grep finding nothing. Handle failures that matter yourself.","core":true},{"t":"Functions, exit codes and traps","gist":"Exit status 0 means success; 127 means command not found, and 128 + n means killed by signal n. Functions print data and return a status. trap cleanup EXIT runs on every exit, even Ctrl-C.","core":true},{"t":"ShellCheck, POSIX sh and portability","gist":"ShellCheck reads your script and flags bugs before it runs; it is not optional. Test #!/bin/sh scripts with dash, which can silently mishandle bash-isms. Past about 100 lines, or when you need data structures, switch to Python.","core":true}],"remember":[{"p":"Bash expands in this order: brace, tilde, parameter/arithmetic/command substitution, word splitting, globbing, then quote removal.","part":0},{"p":"${var:?} aborts with a message if the variable is empty, which guards rm -rf against expanding to /*.","part":1},{"p":"(( i++ )) returns 1 when i is 0, so under set -e a zero counter kills the script; write i=$((i + 1)) instead.","part":2},{"p":"Exit status 127 means command not found; 128 + n means killed by signal n, so 130 is Ctrl-C and 137 is kill -9.","part":3},{"p":"From Python, pass subprocess.run a list, and never use shell=True with untrusted input: no shell, no splitting, no command injection.","part":4}]},"p51m1":{"big":"Parallel speedup is capped by the serial part (Amdahl's law) and by how fast memory can feed the processors (the roofline), so do the arithmetic before writing code.","why":"Every optimization decision in this domain, and every 'how fast will this model run on that machine' question, starts with these calculations. They tell you whether a kernel needs fewer bytes or fewer FLOPs.","parts":[{"t":"Amdahl's law: the serial part sets the ceiling","gist":"The part of a program that can't run in parallel caps the speedup: with 0.95 of it parallel, 24 cores give 11.2×, and no number of cores beats 20×. That's why a GPU port can be only about 2× faster.","core":true},{"t":"Gustafson's law, strong and weak scaling","gist":"Amdahl keeps the job fixed (strong scaling); real users grow the job with the machine. Gustafson's law covers that weak scaling and gives nearly linear speedup: 22.85 on 24 cores with a 0.05 serial fraction. Always say which you measured.","core":true},{"t":"The memory wall","gist":"Processors compute far faster than memory can deliver data: one DRAM access takes about 80–100 ns, and 89.6 GB/s shared by 24 cores is under 4 GB/s each. Little's law: bytes in flight = bandwidth × latency.","core":true},{"t":"The roofline model","gist":"Arithmetic intensity is FLOPs done per byte moved to or from DRAM. Attainable speed is the lower of peak compute and bandwidth × intensity. Below the ridge, about 50 FLOP/byte on your laptop GPU, a kernel is memory-bound.","core":true},{"t":"The working method","gist":"Measure where time goes first, classify each kernel as memory- or compute-bound, count copies like PCIe transfers as hidden serial time, and report scaling against the best serial code.","core":true}],"remember":[{"p":"Amdahl's law: S = 1 / ((1 − p) + p/N), and the limit as N grows is 1/(1 − p), so 0.95 parallel never exceeds 20×.","part":0},{"p":"If the Karp–Flatt serial fraction e stays flat as N grows, the limit is serial code; if it climbs, it's overhead that grows with N.","part":0},{"p":"Strong scaling holds the total problem size fixed (Amdahl); weak scaling holds the work per worker fixed (Gustafson).","part":1},{"p":"Little's law: bytes in flight = bandwidth × latency, which is why GPUs keep tens of thousands of threads resident.","part":2},{"p":"Roofline: attainable FLOP/s = min(peak, bandwidth × intensity). SAXPY, at 0.17 FLOP/byte, is memory-bound; big dense matrix multiply is compute-bound.","part":3}]},"p51m2":{"big":"OpenMP spreads a loop across all cores with one directive, but correct, fast results depend on avoiding races, balancing load and respecting cache lines and memory placement.","why":"OpenMP is how scientific codes, game engines and libraries use every core of one machine, and diagnosing races and false sharing is a staple of performance-engineering interviews.","parts":[{"t":"Fork–join with OpenMP","gist":"One thread runs until it hits a parallel region, a team of threads runs that region, and they join at the end. Outside variables are shared by default; write default(none) so you must label each one shared or private.","core":true},{"t":"Data races and reductions","gist":"When threads update a shared sum with nothing ordering them, updates get lost: a data race. A reduction gives each thread a private partial sum and combines them once at the end. Reordered float additions can change the last digits.","core":true},{"t":"Scheduling, load balance and hybrid cores","gist":"The slowest thread sets the time of a parallel region. With 8 fast P-cores and 16 slower E-cores, equal static blocks leave the P-cores waiting, while dynamic scheduling hands out chunks as threads free up.","core":true},{"t":"False sharing","gist":"Caches move 64-byte lines on x86. Per-thread counters packed in one array share lines, so each write bounces the line between cores and the loop can run slower than serial. Pad each counter to 64 bytes or accumulate locally.","core":true},{"t":"NUMA and first touch","gist":"On multi-socket servers each socket has its own local memory, and Linux places a page on the node of the thread that first writes it. So initialize data in parallel, with the same schedule as the compute loop.","core":false},{"t":"Measuring speedup properly","gist":"Compare against the best serial program, not your parallel code on one thread. Warm up, run at least five times, report the median, pin threads, and watch turbo and heat, which change clocks within seconds.","core":true}],"remember":[{"p":"reduction(+:sum) gives each thread a private partial sum, combined once at the end: 24 additions instead of a billion synchronizations.","part":1},{"p":"A parallel sum that differs in the 12th digit isn't a race: reductions reorder floating-point additions, which aren't associative.","part":1},{"p":"On a hybrid CPU, schedule(dynamic,c) balances the load; with static blocks the slower E-cores finish last and set the time.","part":2},{"p":"False sharing is independent data on one cache line; fix it with _Alignas(64) padding or a local accumulator written once at the end.","part":3},{"p":"Report speedup against the best serial program, not your parallel code on one thread, using the median of at least five runs after a warm-up.","part":5}]},"p51m3":{"big":"Contended locks and atomics serialize on one cache line, and hardware reorders memory, so fast, correct concurrency means synchronizing rarely and stating memory ordering explicitly.","why":"Correct, fast concurrency separates code that scales from code that only passes tests, and ordering bugs that hide on your Intel laptop can fail on a Mac.","parts":[{"t":"What synchronization costs","gist":"A contended lock moves its cache line between cores at about 100 ns per handoff, so time spent holding a lock becomes serial fraction: 5% caps speedup at 20×. Shrink critical sections, split locks, or let threads own their data.","core":true},{"t":"Atomics and compare-and-swap","gist":"An atomic is a read-modify-write that no other core can split. Compare-and-swap replaces a value only if it's unchanged since you read it, and underlies every lock-free structure. Atomics remove races but still serialize on one cache line.","core":true},{"t":"Lock-free, intuitively","gist":"Lock-free means some thread always finishes its operation in bounded steps, even if others stall; wait-free means every thread does. The hard parts are freeing memory safely and the ABA problem. Lock-free is about progress, not speed.","core":true},{"t":"Memory ordering","gist":"CPUs reorder memory operations: x86 lets a later load pass an earlier store, and Arm reorders more. To publish data, write it, then set a flag with a release store; the reader checks the flag with an acquire load.","core":true},{"t":"Deadlock, livelock and priority inversion","gist":"Break deadlock with a global lock order; randomize back-off against livelock. Priority inversion, where a high-priority thread waits on a low-priority lock holder that keeps getting preempted, reset Mars Pathfinder. Most parallel-code hangs are barriers some threads never reach.","core":false},{"t":"Synchronization on a GPU","gist":"A GPU synchronizes in layers: shuffles swap registers inside a warp of 32, __syncthreads() is a barrier inside a block, and shared-memory atomics are cheap. Keep global atomics few; an ordinary kernel has no grid-wide barrier.","core":true}],"remember":[{"p":"Time spent holding a contended lock is serial fraction: if threads hold a lock 5% of the time, no thread count beats 20×.","part":0},{"p":"Atomics remove the race but not the contention: many threads updating one atomic still serialize on its cache line. Accumulate locally, publish once.","part":1},{"p":"ABA problem: a CAS succeeds because the value looks unchanged, though the memory was freed and reused for a different object.","part":2},{"p":"Publishing data needs a release store paired with an acquire load; writing both as relaxed usually works on x86 and can fail on Arm.","part":3},{"p":"volatile is not synchronization: it gives no atomicity and no ordering between cores. Use C11 atomics or locks.","part":3}]},"p51m4":{"big":"MPI runs many copies of one program that share no memory and cooperate only by messages, so scaling depends on keeping communication small relative to computation.","why":"This is how every supercomputer and every large AI training cluster actually runs, and NCA-AIIO tests the cluster, networking and scheduling side directly.","parts":[{"t":"The message-passing model","gist":"MPI launches N copies of one program, called ranks, numbered 0 to N − 1. They share no memory and cooperate only by messages. Two ranks that both send a large message first can deadlock; use MPI_Sendrecv or nonblocking calls.","core":true},{"t":"The cost of a message","gist":"A message costs latency plus size over bandwidth: on InfiniBand NDR, 8 bytes take 1.5 µs and 1 MB takes 21.5 µs. A ring allreduce, combining data from all ranks, sends about 2n bytes per rank.","core":true},{"t":"Domain decomposition and halo exchange","gist":"Each rank owns a grid block plus a ring of ghost cells, and each step swaps edges with its neighbours: the halo exchange. Communication grows with a block's surface, work with its volume, so square blocks beat strips.","core":true},{"t":"Running on a supercomputer: Slurm","gist":"Slurm decides who gets which nodes when. Pending jobs are ordered by a priority mixing fair-share, age and size, and backfill starts smaller jobs in gaps only if they'll finish in time, so an accurate --time starts sooner.","core":true},{"t":"Interconnects and RDMA","gist":"Supercomputer networks such as InfiniBand and Slingshot share RDMA: the network card reads and writes remote memory without involving the remote CPU or either kernel, for microsecond latency. GPUDirect RDMA lets the card reach GPU memory directly.","core":false},{"t":"Failures and checkpointing","gist":"At scale, failure is routine: if each node fails about once in 5 years, a 10,000-node machine loses one every 4.4 hours. Jobs checkpoint, saving state to restart from, about every √(2 × checkpoint cost × mean time between failures).","core":true}],"remember":[{"p":"A blocking MPI_Send may return once a small message is buffered (eager) but wait for a matching receive on large ones (rendezvous), so send–send code deadlocks at scale.","part":0},{"p":"Ring allreduce makes each rank send 2(p − 1)/p × n bytes, almost exactly 2n however large p is, so it's bandwidth-optimal.","part":1},{"p":"Strong scaling ends when halo exchange time approaches compute time; weak scaling, with fixed work per rank, keeps that ratio constant.","part":2},{"p":"Backfill starts a smaller job early only if its time limit fits a gap, so an accurate --time starts sooner than a lazy 48-hour request.","part":3},{"p":"Young's checkpoint interval is τ ≈ √(2CM) for checkpoint cost C and system MTBF M: C = 5 and M = 263 minutes give about 51 minutes.","part":5}]},"p51m5":{"big":"A GPU runs threads in warps of 32 and hides memory latency by switching among many resident warps, so fast kernels keep enough bytes in flight and data close.","why":"Knowing the machine is the only way to write fast kernels and to choose correctly between the laptop and the Mac Studio for a given model.","parts":[{"t":"From the chip to the lane","gist":"Your laptop GPU has 82 streaming multiprocessors (SMs) of 128 FP32 lanes each: its 10,496 'CUDA cores' are lanes, and the SM is the closer match to a CPU core. Memory is 24 GB of GDDR7 at 896 GB/s.","core":true},{"t":"SIMT and warps","gist":"You write code for one thread, and the GPU runs it in warps of 32, one instruction for all 32 lanes. If lanes in a warp branch differently, both paths run one after the other with lanes masked: branch divergence.","core":true},{"t":"Latency hiding and occupancy","gist":"When a warp waits on memory, the SM switches to another ready warp at no cost, if enough are resident. Occupancy, resident warps ÷ 48, is set by whichever of registers, shared memory or block size runs out first.","core":true},{"t":"The memory hierarchy","gist":"Memory runs from registers (about 1 cycle) through shared memory and L1, L2, and GDDR7 (several hundred cycles) to host RAM over PCIe, the slowest. Registers the compiler spills land in DRAM, so spills slow a kernel.","core":true},{"t":"Tensor cores and precision","gist":"A tensor core does a small matrix multiply-accumulate as one operation, in low precisions like FP16, BF16, FP8 and FP4. So the headline 1,824 AI TOPS is about 40 times the roughly 45 TFLOP/s of FP32 on CUDA cores.","core":true},{"t":"Discrete VRAM versus unified memory","gist":"Your laptop's 24 GB of VRAM sits behind PCIe, at least 14× slower, so the working set must fit in it. Apple's unified memory is one shared pool, up to 512 GB at 1.2 TB/s on the M5 Ultra.","core":true}],"remember":[{"p":"Branching on threadIdx.x / 32 keeps whole warps on one path at no cost; branching on threadIdx.x % 2 halves throughput.","part":1},{"p":"Occupancy = resident warps ÷ 48 on compute capability 12.0, set by the scarcest resource; it's a means to hide latency, not the goal.","part":2},{"p":"'Local memory', where the compiler spills registers, actually lives in DRAM and is merely cached, so a spill-heavy kernel is a slow kernel.","part":3},{"p":"The 1,824 AI TOPS figure is a low-precision tensor-core rate; FP32 code on the CUDA cores peaks near 45 TFLOP/s.","part":4},{"p":"A 70B model at 4-bit, about 40 GB, does not fit the laptop's 24 GB of VRAM but fits the M5 Ultra.","part":5}]},"p51m6":{"big":"A CUDA kernel runs once per thread; it nears the roofline when warps read consecutive memory, reused data is staged in shared memory and profiling guides each change.","why":"These skills are exactly what the NVIDIA DLI CUDA certificate assessments test, and they turn your RTX 5090 laptop into a real development machine for AI and HPC.","parts":[{"t":"Kernels, grids, blocks and threads","gist":"A kernel is a function every thread runs; each thread finds its element from its block and thread index. Blocks run in any order, so one binary scales across GPUs. Launches return at once, so check errors and synchronize.","core":true},{"t":"Memory coalescing","gist":"DRAM is read in 32-byte sectors. When a warp's 32 threads read 32 consecutive floats, nothing is wasted; at a stride of 8 floats or more, only 12.5% is used. Use a struct of arrays, not an array of structs.","core":true},{"t":"Shared-memory tiling: matrix multiply step by step","gist":"Naive matrix multiply reads 8 bytes from DRAM per 2 FLOPs. Tiling loads a 32 × 32 tile into shared memory once and reuses each element 32 times, raising intensity from 0.25 to 8 FLOP/byte. Two barriers guard each tile.","core":true},{"t":"Streams and overlap","gist":"Work in one stream runs in order; separate streams can overlap copies with kernels if host memory is pinned (page-locked). Four chunks on four streams cut a 100 ms job to about 55 ms with two copy engines.","core":true},{"t":"Profiling: Nsight Systems first, Nsight Compute second","gist":"Nsight Systems shows a timeline of where time goes, including copies and idle gaps; fix that first, since the kernel may not be the problem. Nsight Compute then explains why one kernel is slow, but never take timings from it.","core":true},{"t":"The Python routes, and Apple's","gist":"CuPy gives NumPy-style arrays on the GPU, Numba compiles thread-level kernels written in Python, and Triton has you program a block of data at a time. Metal and MLX express the same ideas on Apple Silicon under other names.","core":false}],"remember":[{"p":"Each thread's global index is blockIdx.x × blockDim.x + threadIdx.x; a grid-stride loop lets one launch handle any n.","part":0},{"p":"Kernel launches are asynchronous: time GPU work with CUDA events or synchronize first, never with a bare CPU timer around the launch.","part":0},{"p":"A warp reading 32 consecutive floats uses four 32-byte sectors fully; a stride of 8 floats or more drops efficiency to 12.5%.","part":1},{"p":"Tiling raises matrix-multiply intensity by the tile size: with T = 32, from 0.25 to 8 FLOP/byte.","part":2},{"p":"Profile with Nsight Systems first (where time goes), then Nsight Compute (why a kernel is slow); ncu replays kernels, so never take timings from it.","part":4}]},"p51m7":{"big":"Performance work is a loop of measure, profile, classify, change and re-measure, and for AI it rests on counting bytes per parameter and per token.","why":"This is the arithmetic behind every AI infrastructure decision, and behind your own plan to fine-tune on the laptop and serve large models on the M5 Ultra.","parts":[{"t":"A method, not a bag of tricks","gist":"Define a metric and repeatable workload, measure a baseline, profile the whole program first, classify the hot spot, then change one thing and re-measure. In AI, MFU divides the FLOPs the model needs per second by hardware peak.","core":true},{"t":"Vectorization (SIMD)","gist":"SIMD instructions process a vector register of values: 8 FP32 with AVX2, 4 with NEON. Compilers vectorize loops unless aliasing (pointers that may overlap), dependences, strides or strict float reductions block them. It pays on compute-bound loops only.","core":true},{"t":"Mixed precision and quantization","gist":"Mixed precision computes in a narrow format like BF16 or FP8 but accumulates, and keeps master weights, in FP32. Quantization stores weights in 8, 4 or fewer bits; each halving roughly doubles memory-bound decode speed, at an accuracy cost.","core":true},{"t":"Parallelizing training","gist":"Mixed-precision training with Adam needs 16 bytes per parameter, so a 7B model needs 112 GB before activations. Data, sharded, tensor and pipeline parallelism split the job across GPUs, and each adds communication.","core":true},{"t":"Parallelizing inference","gist":"Prefill processes the prompt in parallel and is compute-bound. Decode makes one token per step, reading every weight plus the KV cache (stored attention keys and values), so it's memory-bound: bandwidth ÷ bytes per token caps it.","core":true},{"t":"Portable alternatives, honestly","gist":"CUDA has the deepest libraries and tools but runs only on NVIDIA. HIP, SYCL and OpenCL trade some of that for portability, and El Capitan, Frontier and Aurora run AMD or Intel GPUs. Learn CUDA deeply and one portable layer.","core":false}],"remember":[{"p":"Auto-vectorization is blocked by possible aliasing (add restrict), loop-carried dependences, non-unit strides and strict floating-point reductions.","part":1},{"p":"Mixed-precision training with Adam needs 16 bytes per parameter: 2 BF16 weights, 2 gradients, 4 FP32 master weights, 4 + 4 for Adam's moments.","part":3},{"p":"Pipeline bubble = (p − 1)/(m + p − 1): 4 stages with 16 micro-batches waste 3/19, about 16%.","part":3},{"p":"KV cache per token = 2 × layers × KV heads × head dimension × bytes per value; Llama 3.1 8B needs 128 KiB per token.","part":4},{"p":"Decode ceiling = bandwidth ÷ bytes read per token (weights plus KV cache): 50 GB per token at 1.2 TB/s tops out near 24 tokens per second.","part":4}]},"p52m1":{"big":"Sampled numbers tell the truth only if the signal has nothing at or above half the sampling rate; anything higher folds back as an alias no later filter can remove.","why":"Every sensor on a robot, every audio clip and every SDR stream starts here, and a sampling mistake can't be fixed by any filter or controller later.","parts":[{"t":"Continuous, discrete, digital","gist":"Sampling keeps a signal's value every T seconds, a rate fs = 1/T; quantization rounds each value, and a signal discrete in both time and amplitude is digital. Frequencies f and f + fs give identical samples: that's aliasing.","core":true},{"t":"The sampling theorem and the Nyquist frequency","gist":"A signal with no energy above B hertz is fully captured by sampling faster than 2B. Half the sampling rate is the Nyquist frequency; energy above it folds back below, so an analog low-pass filter must come before the converter.","core":true},{"t":"Aliasing in the field","gist":"Aliasing bites robots too: a motor driver's 20.1 kHz ripple, sampled unfiltered at 1 kHz, becomes a phantom 100 Hz oscillation. When a frequency appears that nothing in the physics explains, compute the aliases of every known source first.","core":true},{"t":"Quantization and bit depth","gist":"A converter rounds each sample to one of 2^N levels, so the step is full-scale range ÷ 2^N. Each bit adds 6.02 dB of signal-to-quantization-noise ratio: ideally 6.02N + 1.76, or 98.1 dB at 16 bits. Real converters fall short.","core":true},{"t":"Signal-to-noise ratio, dBFS and headroom","gist":"SNR compares signal power with noise power, in decibels. dBFS measures levels against digital full scale: 0 dBFS is the largest code, so real levels are negative. Leave headroom to avoid clipping, but recording too quietly wastes bits.","core":true},{"t":"Audio and sensor numbers to know","gist":"Typical rates and bit depths: CD audio at 44.1 kHz and 16 bits, pro audio at 48 kHz and 24 bits, telephone voice at 8 kHz. Data rate is samples per second × bits × channels.","core":false}],"remember":[{"p":"An alias lands at |f − k·fs| for the nearest multiple of fs: a 30 kHz tone sampled at 44.1 kHz appears at 14.1 kHz.","part":1},{"p":"Aliasing can't be removed in software afterwards; an analog anti-aliasing low-pass filter must come before the converter.","part":1},{"p":"For control loops, sample 10 to 30 times faster than the closed-loop bandwidth, not just above twice it.","part":1},{"p":"Ideal SQNR = 6.02N + 1.76 dB for a full-scale sine: 16 bits give 98.1 dB, and each extra bit adds 6.02 dB.","part":3},{"p":"0 dBFS is the largest code a converter can produce; speech peaking at −12 dBFS leaves 12 dB of headroom before clipping.","part":4}]},"p52m2":{"big":"The DFT tells you which frequencies a signal contains and how strong each is, and its resolution is set by how long you record, not by computing power.","why":"Reading a spectrum lets you find hum, failing bearings, vibration and interference in minutes, and it is the same skill as reading an SDR waterfall.","parts":[{"t":"Fourier series: everything periodic is a sum of sines","gist":"Any reasonable periodic signal is a sum of sinusoids at whole-number multiples of its fundamental frequency. Sinusoids matter because a linear time-invariant system turns a sine into a sine of the same frequency, only scaled and shifted.","core":true},{"t":"The discrete Fourier transform","gist":"The DFT turns N samples into frequency bins spaced fs/N apart, so resolution is 1 ÷ record length: separating tones 2 Hz apart at 48 kHz takes 24,000 samples. Resolution is bought with time, not computation.","core":true},{"t":"The FFT and why it changed the world","gist":"The FFT computes the DFT in about N log2 N steps instead of N² by splitting it into halves. At N = 4,096 that's 341 times less work, which is why real-time spectrum analyzers and OFDM radios like Wi-Fi exist.","core":true},{"t":"Spectral leakage and window functions","gist":"If the record doesn't hold a whole number of cycles, a pure tone smears across bins: spectral leakage. A window function tapers the record to zero at both ends, trading main-lobe width against side-lobe level; Hann is the general-purpose default.","core":true},{"t":"Power spectra, noise floors and spectrograms","gist":"For noise, use the power spectral density (PSD), which doesn't change with FFT size; Welch's method averages segments for a steadier estimate. A spectrogram stacks short FFTs to show frequency content over time, trading time detail against frequency detail.","core":true},{"t":"Reading a spectrum like an engineer","gist":"Plot in dB and read in order: the noise floor's shape, then lines. 60 Hz and its harmonics mean mains coupling; a line at a motor's rotation rate means imbalance or misalignment; unexplained lines call for checking aliases and intermodulation.","core":true}],"remember":[{"p":"Bin spacing and resolution are fs/N = 1 ÷ record length: at 48 kHz with N = 4,096, bins are 11.72 Hz apart.","part":1},{"p":"Zero padding only interpolates a smoother curve between bins; it adds no resolution. Only a longer record does that.","part":3},{"p":"Every window trades main-lobe width against side-lobe level: rectangular reaches −13 dB, Hann −31.5 dB, Blackman −58 dB.","part":3},{"p":"A longer FFT lowers the displayed noise floor per bin, because the same total noise is spread over N/2 bins.","part":4},{"p":"Mains hum shows at 60 Hz and harmonics; a strong 120 Hz family points to rectifier ripple, a dominant odd series to magnetic coupling or a ground loop.","part":5}]},"p52m3":{"big":"A linear time-invariant system is fully described by its impulse response, and filtering is convolution with it; every filter also adds delay, which matters most inside control loops.","why":"Cleaning sensor data without adding too much delay is what makes the difference between a smooth robot and one that shakes itself apart.","parts":[{"t":"Linear time-invariant systems and the impulse response","gist":"Feed a single unit pulse into a linear time-invariant system and its output, the impulse response, tells you everything. Every output is the input convolved with it: flip, slide, multiply and add. In frequency terms, convolution becomes multiplication.","core":true},{"t":"The moving average: the filter everyone writes first","gist":"Averaging M samples cuts rms white noise by √M and puts perfect nulls at multiples of fs/M, which can remove mains hum and its harmonics. But it separates frequencies poorly and delays the signal by (M − 1)/2 samples.","core":true},{"t":"FIR and IIR: two families","gist":"FIR filters use only current and past inputs: always stable, and able to delay every frequency equally (linear phase). IIR filters also feed back past outputs: far cheaper for sharp edges, but they can go unstable.","core":true},{"t":"The z-transform, intuitively","gist":"Let z⁻¹ mean 'delay one sample', and a filter becomes a ratio of polynomials, H(z). Its zeros are frequencies it can kill, its poles frequencies it emphasizes. It's stable only if every pole lies strictly inside the unit circle.","core":true},{"t":"Designing and applying filters with SciPy","gist":"Specify passband, stopband, ripple, attenuation and latency first. Design with SciPy's butter, firwin or iirnotch, use second-order sections for IIR, plot the response you built, and keep sosfiltfilt offline: it needs future samples.","core":true},{"t":"Filters inside control loops","gist":"Every filter delays, and inside a feedback loop delay eats phase margin until a well-tuned loop can oscillate. So control engineers use low-order IIRs placed well above the loop's bandwidth and notch specific interference instead of low-passing everything.","core":true}],"remember":[{"p":"Convolving x = [1, 2, 3] with h = [0.5, 0.5] gives [0.5, 1.5, 2.5, 1.5]; the output length is 3 + 2 − 1 = 4.","part":0},{"p":"An M-point moving average cuts rms white noise by √M and delays by (M − 1)/2 samples: 50 points at 1 kHz is 24.5 ms.","part":1},{"p":"A linear-phase FIR still delays: a constant (N − 1)/2 samples, so 131 taps delay 65 samples.","part":2},{"p":"A filter is stable only if every pole is strictly inside the unit circle; its DC gain is H(1) and its Nyquist gain H(−1).","part":3},{"p":"sosfiltfilt is zero-phase but non-causal, so it is for offline analysis only; a live system must use a causal filter such as sosfilt.","part":4}]},"p52m4":{"big":"Feedback corrects the measured error, dividing sensitivity to plant changes and disturbances by 1 + loop gain, but too much gain with too much delay makes a loop oscillate.","why":"These few formulas let you predict how any motor, heater or joint will respond before you write a single line of controller code.","parts":[{"t":"Open loop versus closed loop","gist":"Open-loop control acts blind. A closed loop measures the output, forms the error e = r − y and corrects. That divides sensitivity to plant changes and disturbances by 1 + L, the loop gain, but passes sensor noise through.","core":true},{"t":"Transfer functions and block diagrams","gist":"A transfer function turns a system's differential equation into algebra. Blocks in series multiply, in parallel add, and a forward path G with feedback H reduces to G/(1 + GH). Each pole's real part sets decay and imaginary part oscillation.","core":true},{"t":"First-order systems","gist":"A first-order system, like a motor's speed or a heating hotend, rises without overshoot, reaching 63.2% of its final value in one time constant τ and settling in about 4τ. A step test gives its gain and τ.","core":true},{"t":"Second-order systems and the damping ratio","gist":"Add a second energy store and a system can oscillate. Its natural frequency ωn sets the speed and its damping ratio ζ the shape: overshoot depends on ζ alone, 16.3% at 0.5 and 4.6% at 0.7. Aim for 0.6–0.8.","core":true},{"t":"Steady-state error","gist":"The final value theorem tells you where a response ends up without simulating. Proportional control alone leaves a steady error of 1/(1 + Kp·K) after a unit step; only an integrator, the I in PID, removes it completely.","core":true},{"t":"Stability, delay and margins","gist":"A system is stable when every pole lies in the left half-plane (negative real part). Phase and gain margins say how far a loop is from oscillating; aim for 45–60° and 6–12 dB. Delay costs phase but no gain.","core":true},{"t":"From continuous to discrete","gist":"A computer controller is sampled, and holding each command for one period acts like about half a sample of delay. So sample at 10–30 times the closed-loop bandwidth; the python-control library does the analysis.","core":false}],"remember":[{"p":"With loop gain L, sensitivity S = 1/(1 + L): at L = 100, a 20% plant change moves the output only 0.2%.","part":0},{"p":"A first-order step response reaches 63.2% of its final value at τ and 95.0% at 3τ, and settles within 2% in about 4τ.","part":2},{"p":"Second-order overshoot depends only on ζ: 52.7% at 0.2, 16.3% at 0.5, 4.6% at 0.7, 0% at 1; ωn only scales time.","part":3},{"p":"Proportional control leaves a steady-state error of 1/(1 + Kp·K): with K = 1 and Kp = 9, the output settles at 90%.","part":4},{"p":"A pure delay Td costs ω·Td radians of phase: 20 ms at a 10 rad/s crossover eats 11.5° of phase margin.","part":5}]},"p52m5":{"big":"PID adds three terms, P for stiffness, I to remove steady-state error and D for damping, and a real loop also needs anti-windup and a filtered derivative.","why":"PID runs the overwhelming majority of industrial feedback loops, and a controls engineer is largely someone who knows its failure modes, like windup and derivative kick, on sight.","parts":[{"t":"The three terms, physically","gist":"P is a spring: the farther off, the harder it pulls, but alone it leaves an offset. I is memory that removes lasting error but slows the response. D is a damper that also amplifies noise.","core":true},{"t":"From equation to code: the discrete PID","gist":"In code the integral becomes a running sum and the derivative a difference. Run the loop on a fixed schedule, never 'work, then sleep', which drifts. Add four protections: derivative on measurement, a filtered D term, output limits and anti-windup.","core":true},{"t":"Derivative kick and noise","gist":"Differentiating the error turns a setpoint step into a one-sample spike: derivative kick. Use the derivative of the measurement instead. D also amplifies sensor noise, so it is always low-pass filtered, and that filter's delay costs phase.","core":true},{"t":"Integral windup","gist":"When the actuator is pinned at its limit, a naive integrator keeps piling up error it can't act on, then drives far past the setpoint: integral windup. Cures: conditional integration, back-calculation, integrator limits and bumpless transfer.","core":true},{"t":"Tuning by hand and by Ziegler–Nichols","gist":"By hand, raise Kp until brisk, add Kd to damp overshoot, add Ki until the offset goes, then back off 10–20%. Ziegler–Nichols finds the gain Ku and period Tu of steady oscillation, then sets gains; it's aggressive, so detune.","core":true},{"t":"Structures beyond a single loop","gist":"Cascade control nests fast inner loops inside slower outer ones: current inside velocity inside position. Feedforward adds what you already know, like gravity torque, so feedback only corrects the model's errors. Gain scheduling switches gains with the operating point.","core":true},{"t":"On real hardware, safely","gist":"A first motor loop: gearmotor with encoder, small driver, microcontroller, PWM near 20 kHz, PID on a timer. Power the motor separately, fuse it, add a physical e-stop, and expect full speed the wrong way at first.","core":false}],"remember":[{"p":"The I term removes steady-state error but adds lag, making the response slower and more oscillatory; speed and damping come from P and D.","part":0},{"p":"Run the loop on a fixed schedule, a timer interrupt or next += T on a monotonic clock, never 'compute, then sleep(T)', which drifts.","part":1},{"p":"Derivative kick: a 10-unit step with T = 0.01 s and Kd = 0.5 fires 500 units of D for one sample; take D on the measurement.","part":2},{"p":"Anti-windup by conditional integration: stop integrating while the output is saturated in the direction the error is pushing.","part":3},{"p":"Ziegler–Nichols PID: Kp = 0.6Ku, Ti = Tu/2, Td = Tu/8. It targets quarter-amplitude decay, often 40–60% overshoot, so detune.","part":4}]},"p52m6":{"big":"Robots move every measurement between coordinate frames with transforms, relate joints and wheels to motion with kinematics, and fuse sensors while tracking how uncertain they are.","why":"Every robot must know where it is, where everything else is and how sure it is: the planner routes around transformed obstacle positions, and growing uncertainty tells the robot to slow down.","parts":[{"t":"Coordinate frames and the conventions that prevent crashes","gist":"Every sensor measures in its own frame. ROS conventions: x forward, y left, z up, SI units, and the chain map → odom → base_link → sensors. odom drifts but never jumps; map never drifts but may jump.","core":true},{"t":"Rotations and homogeneous transforms","gist":"A homogeneous transform packs a rotation and a translation into one matrix, so converting a point is one multiplication and chaining frames is multiplying transforms. Store 3D orientation as a unit quaternion, since roll, pitch and yaw suffer gimbal lock.","core":true},{"t":"Forward and inverse kinematics of a two-link arm","gist":"Forward kinematics turns joint angles into the hand's position. Inverse kinematics works backward and is harder: a two-link arm usually has two answers, elbow up and elbow down, none outside its reach, and trouble near singularities.","core":true},{"t":"Wheels, encoders and odometry","gist":"A differential-drive robot's speed and turn rate come from its two wheel speeds, measured by encoders. Integrating that motion over time is odometry, and its error grows without limit, heading worst of all, so something absolute must correct it.","core":true},{"t":"The sensors","gist":"Each sensor has strengths and failure modes: encoders suffer wheel slip, gyro bias drifts, GNSS fails indoors, lidar struggles with glass and black surfaces, and stereo depth error grows with distance squared.","core":false},{"t":"Sensor fusion: complementary and Kalman filters","gist":"No sensor is good at everything, so you fuse them. A complementary filter blends a drifting gyro with a noisy accelerometer. A Kalman filter alternates predict and update, weighting model and measurement by uncertainty, and ends more certain than either.","core":true}],"remember":[{"p":"Compose transforms by multiplying, T_world_sensor = T_world_base · T_base_sensor, and invert one as T⁻¹ = [Rᵀ −Rᵀ·p; 0 1].","part":1},{"p":"Never average or interpolate Euler angles; convert to quaternions or rotation matrices first. A pure yaw θ is (0, 0, sin(θ/2), cos(θ/2)).","part":1},{"p":"In inverse kinematics always use atan2, never atan: it knows which quadrant you are in.","part":2},{"p":"Odometry drifts without bound: 1° of heading error turns 10 m of travel into 0.17 m of sideways error.","part":3},{"p":"Kalman gain K = P/(P + R): prior P = 4.0 and measurement R = 1.0 give K = 0.80 and a posterior P of 0.80.","part":5}]},"p52m7":{"big":"ROS 2 middleware links perception, planning and control nodes running at different rates; develop in simulation first, and never treat software as a safety device.","why":"ROS 2 is the standard robot middleware in 2026, and since no ROS certification works as a hiring gate, employers judge you by repositories, simulation videos and systems you can explain.","parts":[{"t":"ROS 2: what it is and which version to install","gist":"ROS isn't an operating system but middleware: libraries, tools and conventions built on DDS publish–subscribe. Even-year releases get five years of support. For new work, pick Lyrical on Ubuntu 26.04, or Jazzy on 24.04 when a package needs it.","core":true},{"t":"The ROS 2 graph","gist":"Nodes are units of work that exchange typed messages on topics; services handle quick request–response calls and actions long tasks with feedback. Quality-of-service (QoS) settings must match: a reliable subscriber hears nothing from a best-effort publisher.","core":true},{"t":"tf2: transforms as a service","gist":"tf2 keeps the tree of frames: nodes broadcast transforms, and any node can ask where frame A was relative to frame B at time t. Use the measurement's time or obstacles smear. Most misplaced-obstacle bugs are bad transforms.","core":true},{"t":"Perception, planning, control","gist":"An autonomous robot runs three layers at three speeds: perception (10–30 Hz) models the world, planning (1–20 Hz) picks a route, and control (50–1,000 Hz) drives the actuators. Nav2 packages it for mobile robots, MoveIt 2 for arms.","core":true},{"t":"Path planning with A*","gist":"A* searches an occupancy grid by expanding cells in order of f = g + h: cost so far plus an estimate of what remains. If h never overestimates, A* stays optimal and usually explores far fewer cells than Dijkstra.","core":true},{"t":"SLAM in one honest paragraph","gist":"SLAM builds a map while locating the robot in it. Modern 2D lidar SLAM links poses in a graph; recognizing a revisited place, a loop closure, bends the trajectory to agree. It fails in featureless corridors, mirrored rooms and glass.","core":false},{"t":"Simulation first: Gazebo","gist":"Never debug a new behavior on hardware that can hurt someone: Gazebo simulates physics, sensors and actuators, bridged to ROS 2 by ros_gz. Simulation idealizes friction, latency and noise, so add them on purpose, then test slowly on hardware.","core":true},{"t":"Safety in robotics","gist":"Software is not a safety device. Assess risk, then design hazards out, guard, then warn. On your bench: a hardwired e-stop cutting motor power, a command watchdog and speed limits. Only test robots you own or are authorized to assess.","core":true}],"remember":[{"p":"Even-year ROS 2 releases are LTS for five years; for new work use Lyrical on Ubuntu 26.04, and avoid Kilted, which ends December 2026.","part":0},{"p":"A QoS mismatch, such as a reliable subscriber on a best-effort publisher, silently delivers nothing; ros2 topic info -v shows both sides.","part":1},{"p":"Pipeline rates: perception 10–30 Hz, planning 1–20 Hz, control 50–1,000 Hz; each layer runs faster than the one above it.","part":3},{"p":"Admissible h keeps A* optimal; h = 0 is Dijkstra; weighted A* (w > 1) is faster but its path may be up to w times longer.","part":4},{"p":"An e-stop must cut actuator power in hardware; a watchdog that zeroes velocity when /cmd_vel is silent for 0.5 s is an extra layer, not a substitute.","part":7}]},"p53m1":{"big":"A hardware threat model names the assets, the attacker and the physical boundary, and remembers that no component can vouch for itself, so trust must start from an immutable root.","why":"As a pentester you'll meet firmware implants that survive a disk wipe and be asked whether laptops survive theft; as an engineer you'll build the opposite. Both jobs start with a threat model.","parts":[{"t":"The machine below the operating system","gist":"Your PC is really a dozen or more small computers: a security processor, UEFI firmware in SPI flash, option ROMs and device controllers. The lower the layer, the more it can see and the slower it is to fix.","core":true},{"t":"Assets, adversaries and attack potential","gist":"Start with what you protect, like keys and firmware integrity, then name attackers by what they can do. Attack potential adds points for time, expertise, knowledge, window and equipment; under 10 means almost anyone can do it.","core":true},{"t":"Trust boundaries you can point to on the board","gist":"On a board, a trust boundary is usually a physical edge: the TPM bus, the memory bus, SPI flash, DMA from devices and debug ports. Every crossing needs a control and a test.","core":true},{"t":"The supply chain is attack surface","gist":"Every stage a chip passes through, from design to repair, can add a weakness. Leaked Boot Guard keys, the PKfail test key and LogoFAIL show one flaw spreading to many products at once.","core":true},{"t":"What software security cannot fix","gist":"Some weaknesses outlive every patch: boot ROM bugs, fuses, microarchitecture and physics. And a component cannot vouch for itself, so a check only means something if an earlier, more trusted part made it.","core":true},{"t":"Methods that produce a usable model","gist":"Turn the picture into a table an engineer can act on, using a data-flow diagram with STRIDE, attack trees, MITRE EMB3D and the hardware CWE list. Do hardware research only on devices you own.","core":false}],"remember":[{"p":"The lower the layer, the more it sees and the slower it is fixed: a boot ROM bug is never patched, only new silicon removes it.","part":0},{"p":"Attack potential scores five factors: elapsed time, expertise, knowledge, window and equipment. A path scoring under 10 is within reach of almost anybody.","part":1},{"p":"TPM-only BitLocker keys have been read off a discrete TPM's bus; the controls are an integrated TPM, encrypted TPM sessions and a pre-boot PIN.","part":2},{"p":"Supply-chain controls include provenance records, HSM key ceremonies with M-of-N quorum, device identity created at manufacture and firmware SBOMs.","part":3},{"p":"A component cannot vouch for itself: compromised firmware can tell the OS anything, so trust flows upward from an immutable root.","part":4}]},"p53m2":{"big":"A root of trust is tiny, unchangeable logic that starts a chain in which each boot stage checks the next, while measured boot records exactly what ran.","why":"It lets you audit whether a machine's boot chain can be trusted and design updates that refuse rollback, the trick BlackLotus used to bypass Secure Boot on fully patched Windows 11.","parts":[{"t":"What a root of trust does","gist":"A root of trust is the small set of logic, boot ROM and fused keys the rest of the platform relies on. A good one is small, can't be changed in the field, and refuses old, vulnerable images.","core":true},{"t":"The chain of trust, link by link","gist":"Boot is a relay race: the boot ROM checks the first stage's signature against a key hash in fuses, and each stage checks the next. Real attacks use stolen keys, unchecked links and rollback.","core":true},{"t":"UEFI Secure Boot","gist":"Secure Boot uses four key databases: PK controls KEK, and KEK controls db (allowed) and dbx (revoked, always wins). Machines without Microsoft's 2023 certificates keep booting but stop getting new boot protections.","core":true},{"t":"Verified boot versus measured boot","gist":"Verified boot checks a signature and refuses to run on failure. Measured boot records a hash of each stage in the TPM and always continues, proving exactly what ran. The two work together.","core":true},{"t":"Signed updates, anti-rollback and recovery","gist":"A firmware update must be signed, refuse a downgrade below the fused minimum version, go to an inactive A/B slot and fall back automatically. Recovery means restoring a protected golden image.","core":true},{"t":"Identity from measurements: DICE","gist":"DICE gives small devices without a TPM an identity cheaply: keys are derived from a fused secret plus a hash of the firmware, so changing the firmware changes every key.","core":false},{"t":"Open and vendor roots of trust","gist":"Open designs like OpenTitan and Caliptra can be audited by anyone; vendor designs like Pluton are patched centrally and quickly but must be taken on trust. Both beat a root nobody can name.","core":true}],"remember":[{"p":"The chain of trust is only as strong as its first link: the boot ROM, checking against a key hash burned into fuses.","part":1},{"p":"Secure Boot means only signed code runs: an old, signed, vulnerable loader passes until it is revoked in dbx or by SBAT generation numbers.","part":2},{"p":"Machines without the 2023 certificates keep booting, since firmware doesn't enforce expiry, but stop receiving new boot-level protections and revocations.","part":2},{"p":"Verified boot answers 'Is this allowed?'; measured boot answers 'Exactly what ran, in what order?' and only records, never blocks.","part":3},{"p":"A trustworthy update checks the signature and security version number, writes to an inactive A/B slot, and falls back automatically.","part":4}]},"p53m3":{"big":"The TPM records what booted in PCRs, releases a sealed key only when those values match, and signs quotes that prove the boot state to a remote verifier.","why":"BitLocker, Linux disk unlock, Windows Hello for Business keys and Intune device health checks all stand on it, and it explains why a disk asks for recovery after a BIOS update.","parts":[{"t":"What a TPM is, and what it is not","gist":"A TPM is a small, standard security chip in four forms: discrete, firmware, integrated and virtual. It isn't a crypto accelerator and can't see memory, so it guards a few small keys and knows only what it's told.","core":true},{"t":"PCRs and the event log","gist":"A PCR can only be extended: the new value is a hash of the old value plus a new digest. The event log isn't protected, but replaying it and matching the TPM's PCRs proves it is complete and in order.","core":true},{"t":"Keys and hierarchies","gist":"Keys come from four hierarchies whose seeds never leave the chip. The endorsement key proves the TPM is genuine, while a separate restricted attestation key signs quotes. Policy sessions set the conditions for using a key.","core":true},{"t":"Sealing: secrets bound to a state","gist":"Sealing releases a small secret only if the PCRs hold expected values. Bind to too much and every update forces recovery; bind to nothing and a tampered bootloader gets the key. Always escrow a recovery key.","core":true},{"t":"Remote attestation","gist":"A verifier sends a fresh nonce, and the TPM signs a quote of its PCRs plus that nonce. The verifier checks certificates, signature, freshness, log replay and reference values. It proves only what was measured.","core":true}],"remember":[{"p":"The CPU encrypts the disk; the TPM only guards the small key secret. A discrete TPM takes tens to hundreds of milliseconds per RSA-2048 signature.","part":0},{"p":"The event log is unprotected: only replaying it and matching the TPM's quoted PCR values proves it is complete and unaltered.","part":1},{"p":"The endorsement key identifies the TPM; a restricted attestation key, bound to it once at enrollment, signs quotes.","part":2},{"p":"BitLocker with Secure Boot seals to PCRs 7 and 11, so routine firmware updates don't trigger recovery; sealing to PCR 0 does.","part":3},{"p":"In attestation, the verifier's fresh nonce proves freshness, so an old good quote cannot be replayed.","part":4}]},"p53m4":{"big":"Confidential computing hides a workload's memory from the host and hypervisor, and attestation proves what launched, but your own bugs, availability, side channels and physical attacks remain outside it.","why":"In the cloud the hypervisor and its operators belong to someone else. Knowing exactly what a TEE promises lets you release keys only to a verified VM and state the risks honestly.","parts":[{"t":"Four isolation models","gist":"Isolation comes in four shapes: a process enclave (SGX), a secure world (TrustZone), a confidential VM (SEV-SNP, TDX, Arm CCA) and a separate coprocessor. They differ in what is isolated and what you must trust.","core":true},{"t":"How a confidential VM works","gist":"Each VM's memory is encrypted so the host sees only ciphertext, and integrity checks stop the hypervisor swapping or replaying pages. Devices stay outside, so the guest must encrypt its own disk and network traffic.","core":true},{"t":"Attestation: proving you are talking to a real, unmodified CVM","gist":"The chip signs a report with the launch measurement, report data, policy and TCB version. The verifier checks each one, and only then does a key service release a secret to that VM.","core":true},{"t":"What TEEs protect, and what they do not","gist":"TEEs protect guest memory from host software, the hypervisor and cloud admins working through software. They don't protect your own bugs, availability, side channels, physical memory-bus attacks or old restored snapshots.","core":true},{"t":"Apple's Secure Enclave and the coprocessor model","gist":"Apple's Secure Enclave is a separate core with its own boot ROM and OS, holding keys that even a compromised main kernel can't read. Its limit is that you trust Apple's closed design.","core":false}],"remember":[{"p":"Encrypting memory isn't enough: without integrity, like SEV-SNP's reverse map table or TDX's secure page tables, a hypervisor can remap or replay pages.","part":1},{"p":"A confidential VM's disk and network I/O use shared, unencrypted buffers, so the guest must encrypt everything itself, with TLS or dm-crypt.","part":1},{"p":"An SNP report carries the launch measurement, 64 bytes of report data, the guest policy and the TCB version, signed by the chip's VCEK.","part":2},{"p":"Secure key release: the key service wraps the key to the public key named in the report data, so only that VM can unwrap it.","part":2},{"p":"Vendors put physical attacks out of scope: in 2025 TEE.fail used a memory-bus interposer built for under $1,000 to extract keys.","part":3}]},"p53m5":{"big":"Shared caches and predictors let timing leak secrets across a boundary, so you match each mitigation to the boundary it protects and write code whose timing never depends on secrets.","why":"It lets you decide which mitigations to keep for a given threat model, show your own code doesn't leak through time, and tell executives what mitigations cost in numbers.","parts":[{"t":"Why sharing creates channels","gist":"Caches, predictors and buffers are invisible, but they change how long things take, so one security domain can infer another's activity from timing. Speculative work that gets thrown away still leaves these footprints.","core":true},{"t":"The principle: do not share secrets across a boundary","gist":"Every mitigation is one of six moves: don't map the secret, stop predictors carrying over, clear buffers, fence speculation, partition, or fix the hardware. Each one protects a specific boundary.","core":true},{"t":"Reading a machine's status","gist":"Linux lists each issue's status under /sys/devices/system/cpu/vulnerabilities; Windows uses Get-SpeculationControlSettings. Choose by threat model: keep the defaults on a PC, add core scheduling or SMT off on multi-tenant hosts.","core":true},{"t":"Constant-time code","gist":"Your code's timing and memory access must not depend on secrets: no secret branches, no secret indices, no variable-time instructions. memcmp stops at the first difference, so use a library compare function instead.","core":true},{"t":"Verifying timing independence","gist":"Time thousands of runs with a fixed input and a random input, then compare them with Welch's t-test. A |t| above 4.5 means a detectable difference; constant-time code stays small however long you run.","core":true},{"t":"The cost, with arithmetic","gist":"Work out costs with simple sums: 200,000 syscalls per second at 150 ns each is 3% of a core. Disabling SMT costs about a fifth of capacity. Measure your own workload, since vendor numbers are averages.","core":true}],"remember":[{"p":"KPTI protects the user-kernel boundary by not mapping the secret; core scheduling or SMT off protects SMT siblings and tenants.","part":1},{"p":"mitigations=off belongs only on isolated benchmark machines; a personal PC runs untrusted JavaScript every day, so keep the defaults.","part":2},{"p":"A constant-time compare touches every byte and never exits early; prefer library calls like hmac.compare_digest or CRYPTO_memcmp.","part":3},{"p":"In a Welch t-test timing check, |t| above 4.5 indicates a detectable difference, meaning the code leaks.","part":4},{"p":"200,000 syscalls per second times 150 ns is 30 ms of every second: 3% of a core.","part":5}]},"p53m6":{"big":"Physical security makes key extraction slow, costly and noticed, and certifications tell you exactly which product, in which configuration, was tested against which attacker.","why":"Contracts and procurement rules demand these certificates. Reading them honestly lets you pick the right TPM, secure element or HSM and see through 'FIPS compliant' claims that mean nothing official.","parts":[{"t":"Physical attack classes, conceptually","gist":"Physical attacks come in three classes by how much they disturb the chip: non-invasive ones watch timing, power or EM; semi-invasive ones inject faults; invasive ones open the package. Cost and expertise rise across the three.","core":true},{"t":"Evidence, resistance, detection, response","gist":"Tamper evidence leaves proof, resistance slows the attacker, detection notices, and response wipes keys before they leak. Inside the logic, masking and hiding blunt observation, and redundancy catches faults.","core":true},{"t":"Secure elements, TPMs and HSMs","gist":"Secure elements are small tamper-resistant chips like SIMs; a TPM is a standard one for platform measurement; an HSM is the data-center version, and high-end ones are far faster. All stop key extraction, not misuse by an authorized caller.","core":true},{"t":"FIPS 140-3","gist":"FIPS 140-3 certifies cryptographic modules at four levels; Level 3, typical for HSMs, adds tamper response and identity-based authentication. 'FIPS validated' means a current certificate for that exact module and version.","core":true},{"t":"Common Criteria","gist":"Common Criteria checks a product against its own claims, written in a Security Target. The EAL measures assurance effort, while AVA_VAN says which attacker it resists. Always ask: what, in which configuration, against whom?","core":true}],"remember":[{"p":"Tamper response acts before secrets leak, for example zeroizing keys; an HSM's tamper response is designed to destroy its keys.","part":1},{"p":"TPMs, secure elements and HSMs protect keys from extraction, not from misuse by an authorized caller, so log and limit usage.","part":2},{"p":"'FIPS validated' means a current CMVP certificate for that exact module and version; 'FIPS compliant' or 'FIPS-ready' means nothing official.","part":3},{"p":"FIPS 140-2 certificates were scheduled to move to the Historical list on 21 September 2026; buy FIPS 140-3 now.","part":3},{"p":"AVA_VAN.2 resists Basic attack potential and AVA_VAN.5 resists High; what was claimed matters more than the EAL number.","part":4}]},"p53m7":{"big":"Assurance is a measured state, not a purchase: you list what's in every image, ship updates through verifiable steps, disclose flaws on a clock and keep measuring the fleet.","why":"When a shared firmware bug appears, SBOMs answer 'which images contain it?' in minutes instead of weeks, and fleet measurement tells you which devices still need fixing.","parts":[{"t":"SBOMs for firmware","gist":"An SBOM is a machine-readable list of the components inside software or firmware. With one, finding which images contain a vulnerable part takes minutes, not weeks. VEX adds which vulnerabilities really affect you.","core":true},{"t":"Update pipelines that deserve trust","gist":"An update channel can install code on every device, so build it as verifiable steps: reproducible builds, HSM-backed signing, TUF distribution, device checks with A/B slots, and staged rings where the second ring is the real gate.","core":true},{"t":"Coordinated vulnerability disclosure","gist":"Researchers report privately, the vendor fixes, and both publish together. Hardware embargoes run longer than software's common ninety days, and the EU CRA now sets reporting clocks of 24 hours, 72 hours and 14 days.","core":true},{"t":"Verifying hardware before it exists","gist":"A chip bug found after manufacture can cost millions at advanced nodes, or last forever in a boot ROM. So teams simulate with coverage, prove properties formally, and check that secret data never flows to readable outputs.","core":true},{"t":"Operating a fleet","gist":"Pick a few indicators, like Secure Boot on, TPM ready and disk keys escrowed, and collect them continuously. Every red indicator becomes a ticket with an owner and due date, and exceptions expire.","core":true}],"remember":[{"p":"SPDX and CycloneDX are the two main SBOM formats; VEX states whether a CVE that is present is actually exploitable in that build.","part":0},{"p":"A signed update proves origin, not quality or freshness; you also need anti-rollback, staged rings, health checks and a recovery path.","part":1},{"p":"For a hidden 0.5% failure rate, a 1% ring of 40 devices catches it only about 18% of the time; a 10% ring of 400, about 87%.","part":1},{"p":"For an actively exploited vulnerability under the EU CRA: early warning within 24 hours, notification within 72 hours, final report within 14 days after a fix.","part":2},{"p":"Formal verification proves only the properties you wrote, within the bound you chose; missing properties leave bugs unproven.","part":3}]},"p54m1":{"big":"How the money arrives is a choice: recurring plans with healthy unit economics make a business predictable, so land with projects and grow them into monthly plans.","why":"With the right model for each service line and MRR that covers your fixed costs, you survive a month with no new project instead of every slow month becoming a crisis.","parts":[{"t":"Six models, by what the client actually buys","gist":"Technical businesses sell in six ways: your hours, your judgment, a finished project, a fixed package, ongoing IT (MSP) or ongoing security (MSSP). They differ in what the client buys, how money arrives and who carries risk.","core":true},{"t":"Break-fix versus managed: follow the incentive","gist":"With break-fix you earn more when the client's systems fail. A flat monthly fee flips that, so every outage you prevent is profit. The price is that you carry the risk, so define the scope precisely.","core":true},{"t":"Recurring revenue and the arithmetic of churn","gist":"MRR is revenue that repeats each month without a new sale, and it's worth more because it's predictable. Churn compounds: losing 2% a month loses 21.5% of the base a year.","core":true},{"t":"Unit economics: does one more client make you richer?","gist":"Unit economics asks whether one client is profitable once every cost of serving them is counted, using ARPA, gross margin, CAC, payback and LTV. With few clients, treat LTV as a range.","core":true},{"t":"Leverage: Maister's profit equation","gist":"Maister's equation says profit = margin × productivity × leverage. Solo, leverage is 1, so profit rises only through margin or productivity, both with ceilings. Leverage comes from people, process and software.","core":true},{"t":"Productized services: expertise sold like a product","gist":"A productized service has a fixed scope, price and timeline and can be bought from a web page. It shortens sales and makes work delegable; requests outside the box are change orders, not favors.","core":false},{"t":"Your service lines, mapped","gist":"Each service line has a natural model and a recurring hook. Land with a project, expand to a plan, and track coverage, MRR divided by fixed monthly costs; above 1.0 you survive a slow month.","core":true},{"t":"How this maps to the certification","gist":"How these ideas appear in ITIL AI Governance, the ITIL Foundation and the CMC's exam on the business of consulting.","core":false}],"remember":[{"p":"Break-fix pays you when systems fail; a flat managed fee pays you when nothing breaks.","part":1},{"p":"Churn compounds: retention after a year is (1 − c)^12, so 2% a month loses 21.5% a year and 1% loses 11.4%.","part":2},{"p":"Gross margin = (revenue − direct costs) ÷ revenue; payback = CAC ÷ monthly gross profit; LTV = monthly gross profit ÷ monthly churn.","part":3},{"p":"Maister: profit = margin × productivity × leverage. Solo, leverage is 1.","part":4},{"p":"Coverage = MRR ÷ fixed monthly costs. Raising it past 1.0 is a small technical business's first strategic goal.","part":6}]},"p54m2":{"big":"Most client projects are lost before work starts, so choose the right client, write a scope both sides can test, and price and sign every change before doing it.","why":"Clear scope is how a small firm stays paid and stays friends with its clients; otherwise every ambiguity becomes an argument about money.","parts":[{"t":"A niche: an industry times a problem","gist":"A niche is an industry crossed with a problem you solve repeatably. Specializing lets you reuse work, gain referrals, charge more and market more easily. Write an ideal client profile and check every lead against it.","core":true},{"t":"The pipeline, in numbers","gist":"Sales is a funnel of leads, calls, proposals and signatures, each with a conversion rate you can measure and fix. Qualify early with BANT: budget, authority, need and timeline.","core":false},{"t":"The discovery call","gist":"A 30 to 45 minute call where you mostly listen and put numbers on the problem, the outcome, the budget and who decides. Send a summary within 24 hours, and don't quote a price for anything unclear.","core":true},{"t":"Requirements engineering for clients","gist":"Turn notes into requirements that someone who doesn't trust you yet can test. Prioritize with MoSCoW, and give every deliverable acceptance criteria with a clear pass or fail, written as Given/When/Then.","core":true},{"t":"Proposals that get signed","gist":"A proposal is a short decision document of two to four pages. Offer three options, with the middle one as your recommendation, so the client asks 'which one?' instead of 'should we hire them?'","core":true},{"t":"The statement of work","gist":"The SOW defines one project and sits under a master services agreement that holds the legal terms. It lists scope, explicit exclusions, acceptance, client responsibilities, payment and how changes are handled.","core":true},{"t":"Change control and scope creep","gist":"Scope creep is scope that grows without a matching change in price, schedule or approval. The fix is a signed change order before any work starts, or a prepaid bank of hours for small requests.","core":true},{"t":"How this maps to the certification","gist":"How contracting an engagement shows up in the CMC panel interview, ITIL AI Governance's improvement model and Project+.","core":false}],"remember":[{"p":"After a discovery call, send a summary in the client's own words within 24 hours, and never quote a price on the call for unclear work.","part":2},{"p":"MoSCoW means Must, Should, Could, Won't this time; acceptance criteria use Given/When/Then with an observable pass or fail.","part":3},{"p":"Offer three options: the middle is your recommendation, the top anchors the price, the bottom keeps a budget-limited client from walking away.","part":4},{"p":"The SOW sits under the MSA and lists scope, exclusions, acceptance, client responsibilities, fees and the change process.","part":5},{"p":"No work on a change starts until its change order, with price and schedule effect, is signed.","part":6}]},"p54m3":{"big":"Price from arithmetic, not competitors: your rate has a floor you calculate from costs and utilization, a ceiling set by the client's gain, and a risk you choose to carry.","why":"It keeps you from selling hours below break-even and tells you whether an offer, including hosting and AI receptionists, makes money before you sell it.","parts":[{"t":"The rate formula, from what you need to live","gist":"Start from the pay you want, add self-employment tax, benefits, overhead and profit, then divide by billable hours. The model agency gets about $151 an hour; its loaded cost, about $129, is the break-even rate.","core":true},{"t":"Utilization: the lever that moves everything","gist":"Utilization, billable hours divided by available hours, moves the rate most: 45% needs about $202 an hour, 75% about $121. A solo owner rarely sustains more than 60–65%, so track your real hours.","core":true},{"t":"Pricing models and who carries the risk","gist":"Each pricing model puts the risk somewhere: hourly on the client, a fixed fee on you, a retainer shared. Hourly punishes getting faster; fixed fees reward it once you've measured the work a few times.","core":true},{"t":"Value pricing","gist":"Value pricing sets the fee by what the result is worth to the client, not by your hours. Put a number on the gain in discovery, compare your price with the client's profit, and show the arithmetic.","core":false},{"t":"Estimating with ranges","gist":"Give each task an optimistic, likely and pessimistic estimate and combine them with PERT. Quote a fixed fee from the high end, the 80th percentile, because early estimates can be far off.","core":true},{"t":"Operating-cost models: hosting and AI receptionists","gist":"A $79 care plan costs about $30 to deliver, but only if someone else does the 20 minutes of work. An AI receptionist costs about $0.094 a minute, mostly voice, so sell included minutes plus overage.","core":true},{"t":"API versus local hardware","gist":"Local hardware costs amortization, power and admin, about $572 a month here. Break-even is local monthly cost divided by the API price per token. Local wins on privacy, control and predictable cost, not usually on price per token.","core":true},{"t":"How this maps to the certification","gist":"How the cost side of AI fits ITIL AI Governance, and why the IMC USA Code of Ethics and CMC interview care about how you set fees.","core":false}],"remember":[{"p":"Rate = (pay + self-employment tax + benefits + overhead) ÷ (1 − profit margin) ÷ billable hours.","part":0},{"p":"Utilization is billable ÷ available hours, and a solo owner rarely sustains more than 60–65%.","part":1},{"p":"PERT mean = (O + 4M + P) ÷ 6. Quote a fixed fee from the P80 estimate, not the average.","part":4},{"p":"In an AI receptionist, voice, not the model, dominates cost: about $0.094 a minute in total, with tokens about $0.009.","part":5},{"p":"Break-even tokens per month = local monthly cost ÷ API price per token.","part":6}]},"p54m4":{"big":"Contracts and insurance carry most of the real risk, an LLC keeps one bad client from becoming a personal judgment, and tax dates on your calendar prevent costly surprises.","why":"It makes you a client who asks a lawyer or CPA the right questions, cheaply. The lesson is general education as of October 2026, not legal or tax advice.","parts":[{"t":"Sole proprietorship or LLC","gist":"A sole proprietorship is automatic, but its debts are your personal debts. An LLC keeps business debts and contract claims with the LLC if you keep it separate, though not your own negligence or personal guarantees.","core":true},{"t":"Texas specifics","gist":"A Texas LLC is formed by filing a Certificate of Formation for $300, naming a registered agent. Most small LLCs owe no franchise tax but must still file a Public Information Report by May 15 each year.","core":true},{"t":"Sales tax on digital services","gist":"Texas taxes data processing, which has generally included hosting and remotely accessed software, on 80% of the price: an effective 6.6% in San Antonio. Confirm each service line in writing before you invoice.","core":true},{"t":"Contracts that allocate risk","gist":"The MSA's clauses decide who carries risk: limitation of liability caps damages, indemnity covers third-party claims, and IP terms set ownership. A contractor owns its code unless a signed writing assigns it.","core":true},{"t":"Insurance","gist":"Match insurance to what you sell: general liability for physical damage, E&O for losses your work causes, and cyber for breaches. Tell the broker exactly what you sell, since some policies exclude security testing or AI.","core":true},{"t":"Money basics: taxes, cash and records","gist":"Nobody withholds your self-employment tax, 15.3% on 92.35% of net earnings, so you pay estimated tax quarterly. Keep business money separate, reconcile monthly and hold three to six months of fixed costs.","core":true},{"t":"When to hire a lawyer or a CPA","gist":"Hire a business attorney once for your MSA and SOW templates, and whenever a client's contract has unlimited liability, broad indemnity or IP assignment of your tools. See a CPA at formation and before estimated payments.","core":true},{"t":"How this maps to the certification","gist":"How these clauses show up in the CMC ethics exam, ITIL AI Governance's regulation area and ITIL supplier management.","core":false}],"remember":[{"p":"An LLC doesn't protect you from your own negligence, personal guarantees, or a court disregarding it because you mixed personal and business money.","part":0},{"p":"A Texas LLC files a Public Information Report every year by May 15, even when no franchise tax is due.","part":1},{"p":"Texas taxes data processing on 80% of the price, so at San Antonio's 8.25% the effective rate is 6.6%.","part":2},{"p":"A contractor owns the code it writes unless a signed writing assigns it; the client paying doesn't transfer ownership.","part":3},{"p":"Estimated tax is paid quarterly with Form 1040-ES: April 15, June 15, September 15 and January 15.","part":5}]},"p54m5":{"big":"Recurring revenue lasts only if delivery is boringly reliable: promise what you measure, page on what users feel, prove backups by restoring them, and communicate early and on schedule.","why":"Churn is mostly a delivery problem, so these habits are what clients renew for, and they keep a one- or two-person firm from promising coverage it cannot give.","parts":[{"t":"Promise only what you can measure: SLI, SLO, SLA","gist":"An SLI is a measurement, an SLO your internal target and an SLA the contract with its remedy. Set the SLA looser than the SLO, and remember that dependencies multiply, pulling your real availability down.","core":true},{"t":"Error budgets and what one person can promise","gist":"The error budget is 1 − SLO: at 99.9%, about 43.8 minutes of downtime a month. When it runs low, freeze risky changes. Define support hours honestly, because one person can't cover 24×7.","core":true},{"t":"Monitoring and alerting","gist":"Watch what clients feel first, like external site checks and certificate expiry, then the causes. Page only on symptoms someone must act on now and send the rest to tickets, or you'll learn to ignore alerts.","core":true},{"t":"Tickets, RMM and PSA","gist":"RMM tools manage every client endpoint; PSA tools run tickets, time and billing. Treat the RMM as a skeleton key to every client: in 2021 attackers used Kaseya's to reach roughly 1,500 businesses.","core":true},{"t":"Backups you have actually restored","gist":"Follow 3-2-1-1-0 and state each client's RPO and RTO. Do the arithmetic, since offsite restores can take hours, and test restores quarterly: a backup you've never restored is a hope, not a control.","core":true},{"t":"Patching","gist":"Patch monthly around Patch Tuesday, starting with a pilot ring, but break the cycle for actively exploited flaws. Track patch compliance as a KPI and report it monthly, since that's what the client sees.","core":true},{"t":"Incident communication","gist":"Clients forgive outages, not silence. Send the first P1 notice within 30 minutes, update on schedule even with no news, don't speculate on cause, and send a blameless report within five business days.","core":true},{"t":"Documentation, runbooks and offboarding","gist":"Keep one documentation space per client and write runbooks a subcontractor could follow at 2 a.m. Offboard cleanly: hand everything over, rotate credentials, remove your access and delete data as the contract requires.","core":true},{"t":"How this maps to the certification","gist":"How these practices map to ITIL Foundation and to the implement-and-maintain stage of ITIL AI Governance.","core":false}],"remember":[{"p":"99.9% availability a month allows about 43.8 minutes of downtime; 99.99% allows only about 4.4 minutes.","part":0},{"p":"When the error budget runs low, freeze risky changes until the window resets and spend the time on the cause.","part":1},{"p":"Secure the RMM first: phishing-resistant MFA, separate least-privilege admin accounts, and alerts on new admins and script deployments.","part":3},{"p":"Restoring 200 GB offsite over 100 Mbit/s takes about 4.4 hours, so a 4-hour RTO needs a local copy.","part":4},{"p":"A P1 first notice goes out within 30 minutes: what's affected, what you know, what you're doing and when the next update comes.","part":6}]},"p54m6":{"big":"Security and AI services are safe to sell only when the paperwork comes first: written authorization for testing, BAAs for health data, and disclosure, consent and retention for AI calls.","why":"They are your highest-margin lines and the two that can end the business with one careless engagement; doing them by the book protects everyone, including you.","parts":[{"t":"Authorized security work is a paperwork discipline","gist":"Accessing a computer without the owner's consent is a crime; written authorization turns the same work into a service. It must come from someone with authority over those exact systems, and a client can't authorize what it doesn't own.","core":true},{"t":"Scope, rules of engagement and the report","gist":"Rules of engagement set limits everyone can check: scope, allowed methods, testing windows, contacts, a stop procedure and data handling. If you also run the client's IT, you're testing your own work, so disclose it.","core":true},{"t":"Compliance-driven clients: HIPAA","gist":"An MSP, host or AI vendor handling protected health information for a covered entity, such as a medical practice, will generally be a business associate and needs a BAA before any PHI flows, even if encrypted.","core":true},{"t":"Compliance-driven clients: PCI DSS","gist":"Keep card numbers out of your systems by using the processor's hosted payment page. Never let an AI receptionist take cards by voice without PCI-validated capture: one recorded card number pulls the whole call system into scope.","core":true},{"t":"AI receptionists: consent, disclosure and retention","gist":"About a dozen states require every party's consent to record, so every greeting says the call may be recorded and is automated. Keep AI calls inbound unless consent is documented, avoid voiceprints, and delete data on a written schedule.","core":true},{"t":"Vendors: DPAs, BAAs and where the data goes","gist":"A DPA states what a vendor may do with personal data you send it. For every AI vendor, record whether it trains on your data, how long it keeps prompts, where it processes data and who its sub-processors are.","core":true},{"t":"Local or cloud AI for a client","gist":"Local AI keeps data on hardware you control but makes its security your job; cloud gives elastic capacity and frontier models under a DPA or BAA. The usual answer is hybrid, with routing rules written down.","core":true},{"t":"How this maps to the certification","gist":"How this ground is covered by ITIL AI Governance, the CMC ethics exam and the CIPT privacy certification.","core":false}],"remember":[{"p":"A client cannot authorize testing of what it does not own, such as its web host's shared servers or its SaaS vendors' platforms.","part":0},{"p":"A vendor that stores or transmits PHI is a business associate even with encryption, and needs a BAA before any PHI flows.","part":2},{"p":"Use the processor's hosted payment page so card numbers never touch the client's server; that keeps your hosting out of most of PCI DSS.","part":3},{"p":"Texas is one-party consent, but callers from all-party-consent states such as California and Florida mean every greeting must disclose recording.","part":4},{"p":"Since February 2024 the FCC counts AI voices as 'artificial' under the TCPA, so outbound AI calls need prior express consent.","part":4}]},"p54m7":{"big":"Growth is a series of small, deliberate choices about whom you serve, how you prove results, whom you partner with and when to add people, tracked on a one-page plan.","why":"It keeps you working on the business instead of in it forever, so you can add capacity, or stay small on purpose, without becoming an exhausted manager.","parts":[{"t":"Positioning before marketing","gist":"Positioning is one sentence naming the client, a problem with a number attached, the offer and the proof. Then pick two or three channels that fit your niche and capacity, and buy ads only once you know your conversion rates.","core":true},{"t":"Reputation: reviews, testimonials and the rules","gist":"Ask real clients for reviews at a moment of visible success, and answer every review calmly. The FTC's 2024 rule bans fake reviews, reviews bought for their sentiment, undisclosed insider reviews and hiding negative ones.","core":true},{"t":"Case studies and the portfolio","gist":"A case study proves your positioning with a baseline number, what you did and the measured result with dates. Get written permission, anonymize when you must and redact every screenshot.","core":true},{"t":"Partnerships and referrals","gist":"Referral partners serve your clients without competing and send you work; white-label partners deliver services under your brand, like a 24×7 security operations center. You stay accountable for everything a partner does in your name.","core":true},{"t":"Subcontracting and the first hire","gist":"Subcontract first, since it's a variable cost. Hire when utilization, pipeline and cash all agree, and price the hire at loaded cost, about 1.25 times salary. Classification depends on control and dependence, not the label you choose.","core":true},{"t":"Tools and automation","gist":"A small firm runs on a stack of tools plus an automation layer. Automate a process only after doing it by hand three times and writing the runbook, and protect every automation token as a credential.","core":false},{"t":"Knowing when to stay small","gist":"Growth should be questioned, not assumed: measure profit per owner-hour, not revenue. Staying small is a real strategy, but keep any single client under about a quarter of revenue and plan for key-person risk.","core":true},{"t":"The one-page operating plan","gist":"A one-page plan, reviewed every quarter, holds your positioning, offers, targets, capacity, risks and milestones. It tracks KPIs like MRR, coverage, churn, gross margin, utilization, days sales outstanding and cash runway.","core":true},{"t":"How this maps to the certification","gist":"How the case studies and references you collect now feed a future CMC application, and where ITIL AI Governance's final areas fit.","core":false}],"remember":[{"p":"A positioning statement names the client profile, a problem with a number attached, the offer and the proof.","part":0},{"p":"The FTC's rule, in force since 21 October 2024, bans fake and AI-generated reviews and reviews bought conditioned on their sentiment.","part":1},{"p":"Hire when utilization stays above about 80% for three months, the pipeline covers the salary one and a half times, and cash covers three to six months.","part":4},{"p":"A first employee costs roughly 1.25 times salary: a $52,000 technician is about $65,200 loaded.","part":4},{"p":"Cash runway = cash ÷ fixed monthly costs; keep three to six months.","part":7}]}});
